window.ADSChangelog = {"entries": [{"title": "Foundation audit B06 (P2) - audited the border-width drift a code comment had only acknowledged", "body": "Finding from the 5 October 2026 Foundation audit: \"Picker 0/1/2/3/4; CSS comments acknowledge 1.5/2.5/5; Card 1.5 disclosed... Scale/consumer width drift lacks governed exceptions,\" asking for an actual audit, not just the existing comment's bare acknowledgment that a mismatch exists.\n\nResolved each of the three off-scale values individually rather than leaving them bundled as one unexamined \"drift\" note:\n\n- **1.5px** - grepped every real usage: dozens of them, across Card's own border and every outlined/underlined form control's resting state. Pervasive and deliberate, not a stray mistake. Promoted to a real token, `--border-width-1-5`, since this is a justified semantic/optical exception (Card's own documentation already explains why: \"a card's border is its primary edge, no shadow backs it up\"), not drift to migrate away.\n- **2.5px** - exactly one real usage: Spin's default-size loading ring, deliberately between its own Small (2px) and Large (3px) variants to keep 3 visually distinct stroke weights proportional to 3 different ring diameters. A narrow, component-specific exception, documented as such in a code comment rather than promoted to a general token or flagged as an error.\n- **5px \"border\"** - turned out not to be a border-width candidate at all: both real occurrences (`.info-tip::before`, `.dynamic-tooltip::after`) are the CSS transparent-border triangle technique that draws a tooltip's arrow/caret shape, an unrelated geometric trick that happens to use the `border` property. Documented this explicitly so a future reader doesn't mistake it for a 4th drifted width.\n\nReplaced `theme.css`'s bare acknowledgment comment with the full audit above. Added a brief cross-reference to Card's own existing 1.5px disclosure on `borders.html` confirming it's a real, tokenized, sitewide-shared value now, not unique or accidental.\n\nScope boundary, stated plainly: didn't mass-migrate the dozens of existing literal `1.5px` CSS declarations to reference the new `var(--border-width-1-5)` token - that's a much larger, separate mechanical refactor than this documentation-audit finding warrants, and changing dozens of working component rules carries real regression risk disproportionate to a P2 finding. The token now exists and is documented as the canonical name for that value; adopting it in existing rules can happen incrementally.\n\nVerified: confirmed `--border-width-1-5` resolves to `1.5px` via computed style. Screenshotted the updated Component defaults section - no horizontal overflow. Full 157-page regression sweep: 0 console errors. All 14 unit tests still pass."}, {"title": "Foundation audit B05 (P2) - a diverged border preset stayed active, and exported both seed and current values unreconciled", "body": "Finding from the 5 October 2026 Foundation audit: \"Preset remains active after edits; export includes seeds and current values; Standard85 versus Default70... Seed identity and effective border/state precedence unclear.\"\n\nReproduced the same preset-divergence bug already fixed for Typography's platform chips (T10) and generalized for Background's auto/custom badge (C06/C08): clicking \"Standard\" (85% opacity) correctly marked it active, but manually changing the opacity to 42% left \"Standard\" still showing active - a preset label that no longer described the real configuration. Added `clearPresetIfDiverged()`, checked against every field the preset sets (width/color/opacity/style); unlike Typography's version, no extra click-guard was needed here since `applyPreview()` (where the check lives) is only ever triggered by a manual field edit, never by a preset click itself (`selectSystem()` sets field values directly without dispatching events).\n\nThis directly fixed the export half too: the Markdown's \"## Border presets\" section reads `document.querySelector(\".system-card.is-active\")`, so once a diverged preset correctly stops being \"active,\" that section stops claiming a seed identity (e.g. \"Standard, 85%\") that contradicts \"## Configure your border\"'s real current value (42%) sitting right below it - confirmed by generating the Markdown live. Added an explicit `Custom` fallback line to that section instead of leaving it blank when nothing matches, naming the actual situation instead of silently omitting it.\n\nInvestigated \"Standard85 versus Default70\" rather than assuming it was a typo: Border presets' \"Standard\" preset (85% opacity) and Border states' \"Default\" row (70% opacity) are two different, real numbers on the same page describing what sounds like the same thing. Checked what a real shipped component (Input) actually uses: `var(--line-strong)`, a fixed token - neither 85% nor 70%, confirming both figures on this page are illustrative/configurable values, not yet wired into real components at all (a separate, bigger gap tracked as its own finding, not solved here). Added a paragraph distinguishing the two: Border presets is a starting point for a from-scratch custom system, Border states documents a component's resting value independent of whichever preset is configured - neither is a live default today.\n\nVerified: manually diverging a preset's opacity now clears its `is-active` class immediately and the generated Markdown shows `Custom` with the real current values, not a contradictory mix of seed and edited numbers. Confirmed clicking a preset still correctly shows it active (no regression) and that Reset/other fields still work. Screenshotted the page - no horizontal overflow from the new paragraphs. Full 157-page regression sweep: 0 console errors. All 14 unit tests still pass."}, {"title": "Foundation audit R08 (P2) - Reset showed Balanced, then flipped to a different philosophy on reload", "body": "Finding from the 5 October 2026 Foundation audit: \"Reset removes record and selects Balanced; later load with no record inherits product recommendation... Reset can show Balanced then different philosophy on reload.\"\n\nReproduced exactly: set a project profile to \"Marketing Website\" (maps to the Expressive philosophy), loaded `radius.html` fresh - correctly showed Expressive. Clicked \"Reset to defaults\" - showed Balanced, as the button's handler hardcoded. Reloaded the page - it silently became Expressive again, since Reset only cleared Radius's own saved record, and the next load's \"nothing saved\" branch re-ran the exact same project-recommendation mapping that had applied the first time. Reset's own immediate result and what the page actually settles on a moment later disagreed - it looked like Reset hadn't stuck.\n\nExtracted the \"nothing of my own saved\" logic (try the project's recommended philosophy, else fall back to the page's own Balanced default) into one `applyUnsavedState()` function, now called from both the initial page load *and* the Reset button - so Reset's own immediate result is now provably the same state a reload shows right after, instead of two different paths computing two different answers for the same question.\n\nVerified: with the Marketing Website profile, Reset now shows Expressive immediately (not Balanced) and a reload afterward still shows Expressive - no more flip. With no project profile at all, Reset still correctly shows Balanced both immediately and after a reload - confirmed no regression on the plain case. Full 157-page regression sweep: 0 console errors. All 14 unit tests still pass."}, {"title": "Foundation audit R06 (P2) - radius invariance across states and overflow-clipping risk were never stated", "body": "Finding from the 5 October 2026 Foundation audit: \"No radius invariance across focus/error/selected or overflow-focus clipping guidance... Rounded containers can obscure indicators.\"\n\nChecked whether the invariance already holds before documenting it as fact: grepped every `.is-focus`/`.is-error`/`:focus-within` rule in `shell.css` and confirmed none of them touch `border-radius` - hover/focus/error/selected already only ever change color, border and background, never the shape. Documented this as an explicit rule rather than an implicit accident, since a future component could easily break it without anyone noticing it was ever a rule.\n\nChecked the overflow-clipping risk concretely rather than asserting it: `.card-demo-panel` does use `overflow: hidden`, and the Input focus state uses `outline-offset: 2px` (meaning the focus ring draws outside the element's own border box) - a genuine real combination where clipping is structurally possible. Checked whether it's currently visible: Card's own content padding is 14px (10px at Small size), comfortably past the ~4px a focus outline needs, so today's real Card is safe. Documented this as a constraint to check explicitly on any new rounded-plus-`overflow:hidden` container, not a guarantee the invariance rule above provides by itself - an honest \"safe today, not automatic\" note rather than either overstating a live bug or silently ignoring the real risk.\n\nVerified: screenshotted the page - renders with no horizontal overflow. Full 157-page regression sweep: 0 console errors. All 14 unit tests still pass."}, {"title": "Foundation audit R05 (P2) - attached/full-screen/circle rules were scattered or implied, never stated on Radius itself", "body": "Finding from the 5 October 2026 Foundation audit: \"Roles round overlays; Shadows says viewport-touching edge unrounded; equal-size circle rule exists... No consolidated attached/full-screen/pill/circle/responsive rules.\"\n\nConfirmed the cross-page gap: Shadows' own text (\"edge touching the viewport stays unrounded, not unshadowed\") only exists to clarify *shadow* behavior for an attached drawer/sheet - Radius's own \"Floating / overlay\" role never states the same attached-edge exception at all, so a reader of Radius alone would reasonably round all four corners of a side drawer or bottom sheet, including the one flush against the screen. Added a paragraph stating the Radius half of that same rule directly: an attached surface only rounds its free corners, via CSS's own per-corner `border-radius` (e.g. a bottom sheet: `border-radius: 12px 12px 0 0`), with an explicit cross-reference back to Shadows for the companion rule rather than leaving the connection implicit. Also covered full-screen surfaces (no visible corner at all, so radius is moot, not a deliberate 0).\n\nAlso generalized the existing \"Circular only when width equals height\" note (currently scoped to one row - Avatar/Icon Button/Toggle) into this system's one standalone rule for the `Full` value: the same token is a true circle or a stadium/pill shape purely based on the element's own aspect ratio, never by choosing a different radius value - a rule any future circular/pill component should be able to find stated once, not rediscover per-component.\n\nVerified: screenshotted the \"Radius by component role\" section - both new paragraphs render with no horizontal overflow, directly below the existing role table. Full 157-page regression sweep: 0 console errors. All 14 unit tests still pass."}, {"title": "Foundation audit R04 (P2) - the nested-corner formula contradicted its own worked example", "body": "Finding from the 5 October 2026 Foundation audit: \"Formula max(0, outer-padding) alongside independent Button-in-Card exemption... Formula can accidentally square all children,\" worried the stated rule and its own example disagreed.\n\nConfirmed by just doing the arithmetic the page itself provides: Card's radius is 12px, its body padding is 16px. The stated formula, `inner radius = max(0, outer radius - padding)`, literally computes `max(0, 12-16) = 0` - a fully square corner. But the very same paragraph says a Button inside one \"needs no adjustment at all,\" i.e. keeps its own 8px. The formula and its own example gave two different answers for the identical numbers - not a hypothetical risk, an actual contradiction on the page as written.\n\nRewrote the rule to state what the example actually implies: the formula is a *cap*, only in effect while the gap is tighter than the outer radius. The moment the gap meets or exceeds the outer radius, the formula stops applying entirely and the inner element keeps its own independent radius unchanged - a result of `max(0, ...)` reaching 0 under this formula never means \"therefore square the child,\" it means the constraint has gone irrelevant. Added a second worked example in the regime where the cap *does* bite (a tab strip inset 4px inside a 12px container caps at 8px, genuinely smaller than its own default), so both directions of the rule now have a concrete number attached, not just one side.\n\nAlso addressed \"borders/asymmetric insets/radii omitted\": added a sentence that the real gap for this formula is the padding minus any border width sitting in between (a border visually eats into the gap too), and that asymmetric padding means applying this per corner independently rather than one page-wide number.\n\nVerified: screenshotted the updated section - renders with no horizontal overflow. Full 157-page regression sweep: 0 console errors. All 14 unit tests still pass."}, {"title": "Foundation audit R03 (P2) - Radius's taglines, full scales and role table looked contradictory, undocumented", "body": "Finding from the 5 October 2026 Foundation audit: \"Sharp 0-4 includes 6/8; Balanced 4-16 includes 24/Full; roles allow values absent from some scales... Designers cannot infer prohibition/preference.\"\n\nConfirmed the first half directly: Sharp's own tagline says \"0-4px\" but its listed scale is \"0, 2, 4, 6, 8\" - genuinely goes past the tagline, and the same pattern holds across all 5 philosophies (Compact \"2-8px\" vs. scale to 12; Balanced \"4-16px\" vs. scale to 24/Full; etc.) - not a typo, a consistent, unexplained design choice repeated 5 times. Added a paragraph clarifying the tagline names the *characteristic core range* while the scale additionally always includes `0` (square-corner exceptions every philosophy still needs) and a generous top/`Full` value (circular/pill elements, which exist under every philosophy) - neither is a contradiction, the tagline was never describing those two anyway.\n\nChecked the second half against the real mechanism rather than assuming: \"Radius by component role\"'s table (e.g. \"Interactive components: 4, 6, 8px\") looked like it could list a value absent from a given philosophy's scale - checked `shell.js`'s `RADIUS_PHILOSOPHY_TOKENS` and confirmed every philosophy actually defines a value for every one of the 7 named slots (`2`/`sm`/`6`/`md`/`12`/`16`/`24`) - nothing is ever literally absent. The real situation is subtler and more interesting: real components reference the slot by name (`var(--radius-md)`, confirmed against `shell.css` - never a literal `8px`), so the role table's numbers are Balanced's own resolved values for each slot, not a cross-philosophy literal promise. Added a paragraph explaining this with the system's own real numbers: picking Sharp doesn't drop Interactive components below their intended size tier, it reinterprets what the `md` slot resolves to under Sharp instead (4px, not 8px) - the role stays the same, only the philosophy's numbers behind it change.\n\nVerified: screenshotted both the \"Radius philosophy\" and \"Radius by component role\" sections - both new paragraphs render with no horizontal overflow, and the worked Sharp/`md` example (8px -> 4px) is cited from the real token map, not invented. Full 157-page regression sweep: 0 console errors. All 14 unit tests still pass."}, {"title": "Foundation audit S07 (P2) - a literal off-scale value, an undocumented fluid exception, and a real RTL bug", "body": "Finding from the 5 October 2026 Foundation audit: \"No interpolated spacing rule; New Project uses clamp(20px,3vw,32px); first-party literals persist... Implementation contradicts rule,\" pointing at `experience.css` specifically - the AI Rule on Spacing's own page says every margin/padding/gap must be a scale step, never interpolated between two, yet `.setup-panel` uses exactly that.\n\nAudited every margin/padding/gap literal in `experience.css` (the evidence file) against all four spacing systems' own scales (2px/4px \"Balanced\"/8px/Adaptive). Found one genuine, if minor, violation: `.skip-link{padding:12px 18px}` - 18px isn't a step on any of the four scales. Changed to 16px (the nearest real step, visually near-identical). Everything else in the file was already on-scale.\n\nThe `clamp(20px, 3vw, 32px)` on `.setup-panel` turned out not to be a bug to remove, but a real gap in the written rule: both 20 and 32 are themselves valid Balanced-scale steps, and fluid interpolation between two approved endpoints for a container's own outer padding is a legitimate, common responsive pattern - the AI Rule just never said so. Extended the AI Rule text on `spacing.html` to document this as the one approved exception (container-level outer padding only, both clamp endpoints must themselves be real scale steps, never for a component's internal spacing), using the system's own real example as the citation instead of inventing a hypothetical one.\n\nFound a real bug while checking \"logical/native spacing lacks explicit policy\": `.export-category-summary .export-category-count{margin-right:auto}` uses a physical direction property, not a logical one - reproduced visually in RTL mode (`md-export.html`, `dir=\"rtl\"`) and confirmed it renders backwards, with the chevron stuck on the physical left and the count/checkbox pushed right, the wrong sides for RTL. Changed to `margin-inline-end:auto`. Added one sentence to the AI Rule stating logical properties are required wherever spacing differs by writing direction.\n\nVerified: screenshotted the category row in both LTR (checkbox+label on the left, count+chevron on the right, unchanged) and RTL (now correctly mirrored - chevron on the left/\"end,\" count next to the checkbox and label on the right/\"start\") after the fix. Confirmed the extended AI Rule text renders with no horizontal overflow. Full 157-page regression sweep: 0 console errors. All 14 unit tests still pass."}, {"title": "Foundation audit S05 (P2) - Spacing's Component defaults table was missing categories and had one genuinely ambiguous row", "body": "Finding from the 5 October 2026 Foundation audit: \"Exact label/helper/error/group/list/toolbar/empty/wrapped spacing incomplete; scrim versus content padding ambiguous.\"\n\nChecked the real CSS behind the existing Modal/Drawer rows before writing anything, and found the ambiguity was real, not just a wording nitpick: `.modal-demo-scrim{padding: var(--space-24)}` and `.modal-demo-body{padding: 16px 20px}` are two genuinely different paddings - the scrim's padding keeps the dialog off the viewport edge, the dialog's own padding is its internal content spacing - but the table listed only \"24px\" with \"padding around the dialog within its scrim,\" which reads as one value when it's actually two. Split both the Modal and Drawer rows into their real scrim vs. content numbers.\n\nAdded four categories that were simply missing, each checked against its real component CSS rather than invented: Form field (`.input-demo-field{gap: 6px}` - label, control and helper/error text all share one consistent gap, not a different value per pair), List (`.list-demo-row` - 10px padding/10px gap at Middle density, 6px/8px Small, 14px/12px Large), Empty state (`.empty-demo-panel` - 24px padding, 10px gap). Checked checkbox/radio group spacing and Toolbar too, and found neither has a formalized real value - there's no dedicated grouped-checkbox component class, and Toolbar isn't a distinct component in this library at all - said so plainly in a closing note instead of inventing numbers to fill the row, the same discipline applied to C09's data-series gap earlier today.\n\nVerified: screenshotted the updated table - renders cleanly with no horizontal overflow, every value reads clearly including the two-part scrim/content splits. Full 157-page regression sweep: 0 console errors. All 14 unit tests still pass."}, {"title": "Foundation audit S04 (P2) - Consumer App/AI Product cards and an unexplained silent fallback", "body": "Finding from the 5 October 2026 Foundation audit: \"Consumer/AI mapped but cards absent; safely leaves no product selected; labels differ... Common setup types silently fall back,\" asking for a canonical taxonomy and an explained fallback.\n\nMost of this was already resolved earlier today as part of audit finding X09 (same evidence file, `spacing.js`): Consumer App and AI Product cards now exist on `spacing.html` (were missing entirely), and the \"Data-Heavy Enterprise\"/\"Data-Heavy Application\" label mismatch is fixed. Verified both are still correct and didn't regress.\n\nThe one part X09 didn't cover: when New Project's product type is \"Other\" (free text, genuinely unmappable to any card), Spacing/Radius/Grid & Layout all silently left nothing selected with zero explanation - indistinguishable from the page just ignoring the saved project entirely, exactly the \"setup ignored\" risk the finding names. Added `showProductFallbackNotice()` to all three pages' shared `loadX()` pattern: when nothing auto-selects specifically because the saved product type is \"Other\" (not because no project exists at all - that case correctly shows nothing, there's nothing to explain), a small notice now names the user's own free-text product description and says to pick whichever card is closest, making clear this is a deliberate \"can't map this\" rather than a broken auto-select. The notice hides itself the moment the user picks any card.\n\nVerified: with a profile whose `productType` is `\"Other\"` and `productOther: \"A bespoke internal CRM\"`, all three pages show the notice quoting that exact text; clicking any card hides it immediately. Confirmed a normally-mapped product type (SaaS) shows no notice on any of the three pages, and confirmed no profile at all still shows nothing (the correct \"nothing to explain\" case). Full 157-page regression sweep: 0 console errors. All 14 unit tests still pass."}, {"title": "Foundation audit T10 (P2) - view/copy clicks wrote unchanged data, and a diverged preset stayed marked active", "body": "Finding from the 5 October 2026 Foundation audit: \"Copy/view/category actions can write unchanged values; edited styles leave preset active; reseeding lacks visible diff/undo,\" worried that preset identity should describe the actual configuration, and that non-data clicks were triggering writes.\n\nConfirmed both independently. `typography.js` delegated `input`/`change`/`click` all the way up to `<main>` - but `input`/`change` only ever fire on a real form control in the first place, so the `click` listener was the only one actually catching irrelevant clicks: Human/Machine View toggles, the Upload/Browse-by-category method tabs, font category filter chips, and Copy Markdown all triggered a full `persistTypography()` write even though none of them touch a single Typography value. Added a `NON_DATA_CLICK` selector denylist checked only for `click` events (an `input`/`change` event is a real value change by definition, so it's never filtered) - `#regenerateDarkColorsBtn` and the platform preset chips still work exactly as before, since both are genuine data-changing clicks not in the denylist.\n\nSeparately, reproduced the preset-divergence claim directly: picking the \"SaaS\" platform preset correctly marked its chip active and applied its sizes to every level, but manually changing even one row's size afterward left the chip reading \"SaaS\" - a label that no longer described the actual configuration (the same \"preset vs. custom\" identity problem already fixed for Background's dark-mode status badge earlier this session, C06/C08's work). Added `clearPresetIfDiverged()`, run on every real data change except a fresh preset click itself: it compares every level the active preset defines against the row's actual current size, and removes `is-active` from the chip the moment even one no longer matches - there's no third \"Custom\" chip to light up instead, matching this system's existing convention (Background does the same: a `badge-custom` label replaces `badge-auto`, not a new badge element).\n\nVerified: clicking the Machine View toggle and Copy Markdown on a freshly-cleared profile left `localStorage.getItem('ads:typography')` at `null` both times, while picking an actual font still correctly wrote a non-null value. Picked \"SaaS,\" confirmed its chip read active, manually changed the H1 row's size, and confirmed the chip's `is-active` class was removed immediately. Confirmed Reset and Regenerate dark colors (both genuine data-changing clicks, unaffected by the new filter) still work with no console errors. Full 157-page regression sweep: 0 console errors. All 14 unit tests still pass."}, {"title": "Foundation audit T08 (P2) - a failed Google Fonts load was invisible, and fell back to the wrong font category", "body": "Finding from the 5 October 2026 Foundation audit: \"Load failure/unsupported faces... and file bounds unspecified... Declared family may render differently by environment.\" Reproduced directly by blocking `fonts.googleapis.com`/`fonts.gstatic.com` requests in Playwright and picking a curated Serif font (Fraunces): the preview claimed \"Using Fraunces (serif)\" with total confidence, while the actually-rendered font silently fell back to `ui-sans-serif, system-ui, sans-serif` - a failed *serif* pick rendered as sans-serif, a visible typeface-category change nobody was told about.\n\nTwo separate bugs, fixed together since both live in the same `setFamily()`/`injectLink()` path in `typography.js`:\n\n1. The CSS fallback stack was hardcoded to sans-serif regardless of the chosen font's actual category. Added `CATEGORY_FALLBACKS` (one real CSS generic per category - `ui-serif, Georgia, serif` for Serif, `ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, monospace` for Monospace, `cursive` for Handwritten, sans-serif for Sans-serif/Display, since there's no true CSS generic for Display) and `fallbackForSource()`, which also recovers the right stack on reload from the saved profile's `source` field (already the lowercase category name) without needing to persist anything new.\n2. There was no way to ever detect a failed load at all. First attempted a fix using `document.fonts.load()`/`.check()` - reproducibly wrong, confirmed directly in the browser: when a font's `@font-face` rule was never registered (because its stylesheet request failed), the Font Loading API can't distinguish that from \"this name just isn't a registered webfont\" and resolves/returns success either way. Replaced that approach with the `<link>` element's own real `load`/`error` events instead - `injectLink()` now returns a cached `Promise<boolean>` per href, and `setFamily()` uses it to show \"Fraunces didn't load (offline, blocked, or unavailable) - showing the serif fallback instead.\" only on a genuine fetch failure.\n\nVerified: with Google Fonts requests blocked, picking Fraunces now shows the failure message immediately (not after an arbitrary timeout) and the computed `font-family` correctly reads `Fraunces, ui-serif, Georgia, serif` - the serif fallback, not sans-serif. With real network access, the same pick shows \"Using Fraunces (serif)\" with no false failure. Checked Monospace/Handwritten/Display picks each resolve to their own correct fallback stack. Confirmed the Upload tab (a separate, already-robust `FontFace().load().catch()` path, untouched by this fix) still works. Confirmed a fresh page load's default Inter selection shows no false failure message on either font picker. Full 157-page regression sweep: 0 console errors. All 14 unit tests still pass."}, {"title": "Foundation audit G07 (P2) - Split View/Master-Detail never linked to Splitter's already-documented resize rules", "body": "Finding from the 5 October 2026 Foundation audit: \"Resizable/minimum panes, persisted widths, sticky/overlay collision and content priority unspecified\" for Grid & Layout's Split View pattern, recommending it link Splitter/Affix/Table rather than leaving pane behavior undefined.\n\nChecked what Splitter and Affix already document before writing anything new, rather than assuming the gap meant nothing existed: `splitter-default.html` already states real rules - \"Enforce sensible minimum sizes on both panels\" and \"The divider is keyboard-operable (arrow keys), not mouse-only\" - and `affix-default.html` already covers overlap (\"Account for the affixed element's height when it overlaps content below it\"). The actual gap was narrower than it first looked: Grid & Layout's Split View pattern card never told anyone these rules existed, so a reader could easily reinvent (or skip) minimum-size and keyboard-resize behavior Splitter already solved.\n\nExtended Split View's description to state plainly that a resizable version of this pattern *is* Splitter's own layout (not a plain CSS split), cite its already-documented minimum-size and keyboard-resize rules by name instead of repeating or reinventing them, note that equal weighting is a default rather than a rule (favor the pane with reliably more important content), and point to Affix's overlap guidance for anything pinned inside a pane. Extended Master-Detail's description the same way, since it's the same underlying resizable-pane pattern with a narrow list pane instead of an even split.\n\nScope boundary, stated plainly: persisting a user's manually-dragged split ratio across reloads is genuinely undocumented too, but that gap lives in Splitter's own component page, not in Grid & Layout's pattern card (this finding's own evidence citation) - rewriting Splitter's documentation is a separate task, not attempted here.\n\nVerified: screenshotted the full Layout patterns section after the edit - both cards render with no horizontal overflow and no text clipping, just visibly denser cards than their grid-mates (a density trade-off, not a defect). Confirmed the minimum-size/keyboard-resize claims cited are real, current text already on `splitter-default.html`, not invented. Full 157-page regression sweep: 0 console errors. All 14 unit tests still pass."}, {"title": "Foundation audit C09 (P2) - no documented status-color recipe, no selection/data-series exclusion", "body": "Finding from the 5 October 2026 Foundation audit: \"No exact foreground/background/icon triplets or component examples; warning/error/info/selection/data-series distinctions incomplete,\" worried that without a documented recipe, each component would end up inventing its own.\n\nChecked what real components actually do before writing anything, rather than inventing a single universal recipe: Alert (`shell.css`'s `.alert-demo-banner--*`) uses a 3px status-colored left border plus a status-colored icon, with the banner's own neutral background and default body text left alone. Badge's status dot and Table's status cell (`table.html`'s real markup) both use a plain solid status-colored dot next to default-colored text, no tint or border at all. Tag's status variant is the one place that *does* tint the background (~12% opacity) with full-strength status-colored text. Form validation text (`.setup-error`) uses plain danger-colored text with no icon or background. Four genuinely different recipes, not one - documenting a single invented triplet would have been wrong.\n\nAdded two paragraphs to Colors' \"Semantic & status colors\" section: the four real recipes above, and an explicit exclusion - a selected row/card/option uses Brand (confirmed: `.product-card.is-active`/`.group-btn-segment.is-selected` both use `--red-500`/`--brand-500`, never `--danger-500`), since selection isn't a severity judgment and reusing Danger's red there would misread as an error state. Also confirmed there is no chart/data-visualization component in this system at all today (grepped for one; none exists), so documented that a future one should get its own hue set rather than reusing Success/Warning/Danger/Info, instead of inventing a speculative data-series palette now for a component that doesn't exist yet.\n\nVerified: screenshotted the updated section - renders cleanly with no overflow, all four recipes and the two exclusions read clearly below the existing explanatory copy. Confirmed the selection-uses-Brand claim against the actual CSS (`--red-500`/`--brand-500`, not `--danger-500`) and the danger-vs-brand separation claim already documented just above it in the same section. Full 157-page regression sweep: 0 console errors. All 14 unit tests still pass."}, {"title": "Foundation audit C08 (P2) - Brand's CSS variables stay named \"red\" after a rebrand", "body": "Finding from the 5 October 2026 Foundation audit: \"Brand of any hue writes red-named variables... Purpose names should survive branding,\" noting a developer could mistake brand for error styling (or vice versa) since the variable name never stops saying \"red\" even after Brand is changed to any other hue. Confirmed directly: `shell.js`'s `applySavedColorsForTheme()` writes a custom brand hex straight onto `--red-500`/`--red-600`/`--red-400`/`--red-tint`/`--red-glow` - set Brand to pure blue and `--red-500` computes to `#0000FF`.\n\nRenaming `--red-*` itself was out of scope and unnecessarily risky - that name is referenced throughout `shell.css`/`experience.css`/every component page, and a sweeping rename is a much larger, separate initiative than one finding warrants. Instead added purely additive semantic aliases in `theme.css`: `--brand-500`/`-400`/`-600`/`-tint`/`-glow`, each just `var(--red-*)`, plus `--surface-page`/`-panel`/`-raised` aliasing `--graphite-950`/`-900`/`-850` (the same misleading-physical-name problem for Background & surface's 3 user-editable steps). Being plain `var()` references rather than duplicated values, each alias re-resolves live against whatever its primitive currently holds - no `shell.js` changes needed at all, and zero risk of the two drifting apart.\n\nDocumented the mapping in two places per the finding's \"show them in all views\": a new paragraph in Colors' existing \"Light & dark theme behavior\" section explaining the primitive-vs-semantic relationship in plain terms, and a `semanticAliases` field added to that same section's Machine View JSON for AI/developer consumption. Left `design-tokens.json` untouched - that artifact is already known-stale for unrelated reasons (tracked separately as audit finding X01/X03) and will pick this up when that's addressed, rather than this fix overlapping into that one's scope.\n\nVerified: computed `--brand-500` equals `--red-500` by default (`#ff031a`), and after saving a custom pure-blue Brand color and custom Background surface colors, both `--brand-500`/`--surface-page` live-tracked the new values (`#0000FF`/`#223344`) exactly matching their primitives - confirmed in the same page load, no reload-order dependency. Confirmed the extended Machine View JSON still parses as valid JSON. Full 157-page regression sweep: 0 console errors. All 14 unit tests still pass."}, {"title": "Foundation audit C06 (P2) - derived colors had no hierarchy guarantee or documented formula", "body": "Finding from the 5 October 2026 Foundation audit: \"No role-validity/perceptual-order guarantee or hierarchy constraint for highly custom colors,\" asking for the derivation formulas and a validated surface distinction, not just a code comment.\n\nConfirmed the real risk first: Background & surface elevation's dark-mode suggestion (`suggestDarkSurface()` in `foundations.js`) independently clamps each of Page/Panel/Raised into a \"reads as dark\" lightness range, but never checked that the three stayed *distinguishable from each other* - a user picking three very close or identical custom light surface colors would get three nearly-identical dark ones, collapsing the elevation hierarchy the Live preview is supposed to demonstrate. Computed the real numbers before picking a threshold: this system's own defaults have a ~1.76-point minimum pairwise lightness gap in light theme and ~1.96 in dark - and confirmed light theme's own defaults aren't even monotonically ordered (Panel, pure white, is lighter than both Page and Raised, by design), so a naive \"must increase\" rule would have been wrong.\n\nAdded `checkElevationHierarchy()`, checking only mutual distinguishability (every pair of Page/Panel/Raised at least 1.2 lightness points apart - comfortably below both real default gaps, so neither theme's default ever triggers it), wired into the existing `renderElevation()` live-preview function so it runs on every light/dark field edit and on load. Shows an amber (the system's real warning token) advisory line under the Live preview naming exactly which pair is too close, when triggered - informational, not blocking; the save/apply path is untouched, matching the finding's own \"allow deliberate overrides\" ask.\n\nAlso documented the formulas for real, not just in a code comment: extended the existing \"Light & dark theme behavior\" section's human-readable explanation with the actual brand (HSL lighten +12/desaturate -8, clamped) and surface (RGB offset-from-default, factor 0.4, clamped to 2-20% lightness) formulas, and added structured, versioned fields (`formulaVersion`, `darkAccentFormula`, `darkSurfaceFormula`, `elevationHierarchyCheck`) to that section's Machine View JSON for AI/developer consumption.\n\nVerified: on a fresh load, both warning lines are hidden (`hidden: true`) for both themes - the real defaults never false-trigger. Set all three light surface colors to near-identical hex values and confirmed the warning appears, correctly naming all 3 pairs (\"Page and Panel, Panel and Raised, Page and Raised...\"); restored the original defaults and confirmed it clears. Repeated for dark theme after enabling the dark override. Confirmed the extended Machine View JSON still parses as valid JSON. Full 157-page regression sweep: 0 console errors. All 14 unit tests still pass."}, {"title": "Foundation audit NP10 (P2) - Name, email and designation were required with no stated purpose", "body": "Finding from the 5 October 2026 Foundation audit: \"Name/email/designation required despite no account or email service; ... Mandatory identity benefit is unclear.\" Confirmed: nothing downstream actually needs these three values - there's no account and no email ever sent, the post-save routing added earlier today (NP06) is driven entirely by `role`, and Markdown only ever includes identity when the user separately opts in on MD Export (`includeIdentity`, already established). Requiring them was pure friction with no functional payoff.\n\nMade `name`, `email` and `designation` optional in `project-model.js`'s `validate()` - blank is now valid, but each is still bounded/type-checked exactly like before *if* a value is provided (email must still be a valid address if non-blank; name/designation still 1-100 characters if non-blank). `project`, `productType`, `role` and `platforms` are unchanged and still required - each of those drives real behavior (filenames, Foundation recommendations, post-save routing, platform guidance). Removed the blanket `required=true` pass in `experience.js`'s `setupForm()` for these 3 fields specifically, added a `.badge-optional` \"Optional\" badge next to each label (the same badge class Colors Foundation already uses for its own optional dark-theme fields - not a new pattern), and rewrote step 2's intro copy to state plainly why role is required and the other three aren't, instead of the now-inaccurate \"All fields are required.\"\n\nFixed two places that would have shown an awkward blank/gap once identity could be empty: the Review step's definition list now shows \"Not provided\" instead of a blank `<dd>`, and Settings' profile-summary line now joins only the non-empty parts (`[project, name, designation, role].filter(Boolean).join(' · ')`) instead of leaving a stray \" · · \" where a blank name/designation used to sit.\n\nAlso addressed the finding's secondary ask for \"category/Other/browse-return... examples\": added one sentence to step 1's intro clarifying \"Other\" is only for when nothing else fits and that every choice stays editable later from Settings - addressing the stated worry that classification uncertainty creates unnecessary friction.\n\nAdded a new unit test confirming blank name/email/designation now validate cleanly, while an invalid (non-blank) email, and an overlong (non-blank) name/designation, still correctly produce an error. Updated the existing \"requires all five...\" test, since only 3 fields (project/productType/role, plus platforms) are actually required now, not 5.\n\nVerified: completed the full New Project flow live with all three identity fields left blank - confirmed `required` is `false` on all three inputs, the step correctly advances, and the final saved profile has empty strings for `name`/`email`/`designation` with setup still showing as complete. Confirmed a non-blank invalid email is still rejected with \"Enter a valid email address, or leave it blank.\" on the correct field. Confirmed Settings' profile summary reads cleanly (\"Anon Project · Designer\", no stray separators) with blank identity fields. Full 157-page regression sweep: 0 console errors. All 14 unit tests pass (8 in `project-model.test.js`, 6 in `overview-model.test.cjs`)."}, {"title": "Foundation audit NP08 (P2) - New Project's own UI ignored the saved Radius philosophy", "body": "Finding from the 5 October 2026 Foundation audit: \"setup radii/padding often literal... bespoke radii do not consistently follow saved philosophy,\" recommending setup's chrome map to semantic tokens instead. Confirmed the actual mechanism before touching anything: `shell.js`'s `applySavedRadius()` overrides the sitewide `--radius-2`/`--radius-sm`/`--radius-6`/`--radius-md`/`--radius-12`/`--radius-16`/`--radius-24` custom properties to match whatever philosophy (Sharp/Compact/Balanced/Soft/Expressive) is saved on the Radius page - any element using `var(--radius-*)` picks up the change live; any element with a literal pixel value never does. `.setup-panel`, `.setup-notice`, `.setup-steps li`, `.setup-field input/select`, `.export-preview`, `.export-category-details` and `.export-component-option` in `experience.css` all used literal pixel values (16px/12px/24px/8px/8px/6px respectively) matching the *default* Balanced-equivalent values exactly - so they looked correct until a user actually changed their Radius philosophy, at which point New Project and MD Export's own chrome would silently stay frozen at the old shape while every other page's components changed around it.\n\nReplaced each literal value with the matching `var(--radius-*)` custom property (16px→`var(--radius-16)`, 12px→`var(--radius-12)`, 24px→`var(--radius-24)`, 8px→`var(--radius-md)`, 6px→`var(--radius-6)`). Left `.skip-link`'s own 8px alone - shared global a11y chrome used identically on all 157 pages, outside New Project's own scope.\n\nAlso checked the finding's narrower \"no long-label/soft-keyboard contract\" claim directly rather than assuming: filled Project name and a long \"Describe your product type\" value at a 375px mobile viewport and confirmed no horizontal overflow and no visual breakage - native input text scrolling handles it, the same as any text input sitewide. Not a defect; no change needed there.\n\nVerified: with no saved Radius philosophy, `.setup-panel`'s computed radius is unchanged at 16px (no regression for the default/most-common case). With \"Sharp\" saved, it now correctly computes to 8px, `.setup-steps li` to 8px and form inputs to 4px - previously all three would have stayed at their old literal values regardless. Confirmed the same on `md-export.html`'s `.export-category-details`. Full 157-page regression sweep: 0 console errors. All 13 unit tests still pass."}, {"title": "Foundation audit NP07 (P2) - completed setup's back-button and final-submit validation were out of sync", "body": "Finding from the 5 October 2026 Foundation audit: \"Deep links are discarded; completed back/forward behavior undefined; final submit checks step-2 keys before full model validation.\" Investigated all three claims against the live page rather than the finding's own static reconstruction (the audit disclosed it couldn't open a live browser).\n\nThe always-reset-to-step-1-on-load behavior (`history.replaceState(null,'','#step-1')`) turned out to be a deliberate, already-documented choice - its own code comment explains a resumed draft starts at step 1 so required fields stay reviewable - so left that one alone; it's a stated policy, not a silent gap.\n\nThe other two reproduced exactly as described. Completing setup, then pressing the browser Back button, left the success screen (\"Your setup is ready\") fully visible while the step-indicator chips above it silently jumped to \"2. About you\" with a red active outline - two contradictory states on screen at once, confirmed via screenshot. Root cause: the `hashchange` listener still fires after completion and unconditionally calls `show()`, which only toggles the step indicator and the (already-hidden) form's internal `[data-step]` panels - it never knew the form itself, and the success screen, were showing something else entirely. Fixed by having the listener detect it's in the completed state and properly transition back into edit mode first (re-show the form and step indicator, hide the success screen) before handling the requested step - a real resume, not a reset, since every field's value was still sitting in the merely-hidden form the whole time.\n\nSeparately, the final \"Save my design system\" submit only re-validated `secondKeys` (name/email/designation/role), never `firstKeys` (project/productType/platforms) - reaching step 3 only proves `firstKeys` passed validation at whatever earlier moment the hash first advanced past step 1, not that it still does at the actual moment of saving. `M.save()` itself would still catch a stale `firstKeys` failure (it validates everything), but it would surface as a generic \"Could not save: ...\" status message with no visible, focused field to correct - the user would be looking at step 3's review screen with no way to see which earlier field was wrong. Restructured the submit handler so a true final submit (`step === 3`) explicitly re-validates both `firstKeys` and `secondKeys` first and navigates back to whichever step actually failed, landing the user on the real invalid field with its real error message.\n\nVerified: reproduced the back-button desync via screenshot before fixing (step indicator showed step 2, main panel still showed the success screen), then confirmed after the fix that pressing Back now correctly re-shows the real step-2 form with its field values intact (tested: the Name field still read \"N\", nothing was lost). Simulated a stale `firstKeys` failure by clearing the Project name field directly after reaching step 3 without navigating back, then clicking Save - confirmed it now lands on `#step-1` with \"Project name must contain 1–100 characters.\" shown directly on the field, instead of a generic error with no visible target. Re-ran the full normal happy-path flow (step 1 to 2 to 3 to save) to confirm no regression - hash transitions and the final saved profile are unchanged. Full 157-page regression sweep: 0 console errors. All 13 unit tests still pass."}, {"title": "Foundation audit NP06 (P2) - the role field barely changed anything, and export had no readiness signal", "body": "Finding from the 5 October 2026 Foundation audit: \"Role mainly changes destination; no role-specific checklist or Foundation completion condition exists,\" noting the save handler's entire role-handling logic was one ternary that only swapped the \"Customize foundations\" button's link/label - Developer got Components, Product Manager got Templates, and the other 4 roles (Designer, Design System Lead, QA / Accessibility, Other) were all silently folded into the same generic \"foundations.html#colors\" destination with no differentiation at all. Separately, \"Export Markdown\" was always immediately clickable with no visibility into whether any Foundation had actually been configured yet.\n\nReplaced the single ternary with a `ROLE_NEXT` map giving each of the 5 named roles (`project-model.js`'s `ROLES`) its own destination and a concrete first task, grounded in real pages rather than invented ones - Design System Lead and QA / Accessibility now both route to `accessibility.html`, to the release contract and known-gaps sections added earlier today (X10), not a placeholder. \"Other\" keeps the previous generic destination with a generic task, since there's no real role to tailor to.\n\nAdded a \"Foundation readiness\" list to the success screen: one line per configurable Foundation section (Colors, Grid & Layout, Typography, Spacing, Radius, Borders, Shadows, Icons - the 8 `FOUNDATION_TOKEN_MAP` already groups; AI & Agents has no Save state of its own, so it's not listed), each checked live against whether its own `localStorage` key(s) already hold a value - a red dot and \"Configured\" if so, a gray dot and \"Default\" if not. Explicitly informational, not a gate: the help text underneath says so outright, and Export Markdown stays immediately clickable regardless, consistent with every other Foundation page already allowing Save/export at any completion state - this finding asked for a completion *signal*, not a new blocking requirement the rest of the site doesn't have.\n\nNew CSS: `.setup-readiness` in `experience.css`. New HTML: `#roleTaskText`/`#foundationReadiness` in `new-project.html`'s `#setupDone` block.\n\nVerified: completed the full 3-step flow for all 6 roles with fresh storage each time - confirmed Developer/Product Manager still route exactly as before (no regression), and Designer/Design System Lead/QA / Accessibility/Other each now get their own distinct destination and task text instead of sharing one generic bucket. Pre-seeded `ads:colors` and `ads:spacing` before completing setup and confirmed the readiness list correctly showed those two as \"Configured\" and the other 6 as \"Default.\" Screenshotted the result. Full 157-page regression sweep: 0 console errors. All 13 unit tests still pass."}, {"title": "Foundation audit NP04 (P2) - the Other description fields had no type guard or length bound in the model", "body": "Finding from the 5 October 2026 Foundation audit: \"Model accepts overlength Other values and future version 999; Other descriptions lack type guards.\" Probed `project-model.js` directly rather than trusting the claim: a 5,000-character `productOther` passed `validate()` with zero errors (the HTML input has `maxlength=\"100\"`, but nothing in the model itself enforces that bound), and a numeric `productOther` (e.g. `12345`, simulating a malformed or externally-restored payload) made `validate()` **throw** an uncaught `TypeError: p.productOther?.trim is not a function` instead of returning a validation error - a crash, not a rejection. Separately, directly writing a profile with `version: 999` to storage and reading it back succeeded silently, with no indication this is a newer schema than the current code understands.\n\nAdded `isBoundedText()`, the same 1-100 character / string-typed / trimmed check the three main fields (Project name, Your name, Designation) already used inline, and applied it to `productOther`/`roleOther` too - so the model itself is now the authority on that bound (matching the UI's own `maxlength=\"100\"`) instead of relying on the browser having enforced the input. Added `SUPPORTED_VERSION = 1` and a check in `read()` that throws a clear, actionable error (\"Saved setup was created by a newer version of this app...\") for a STRICTLY GREATER version - never an older or missing one, which existing saved projects legitimately have and which `LEGACY_PRODUCT_MAP` already migrates.\n\nAdded 2 new unit tests (5 -> 7): one confirming overlength and non-string `Other` values are now rejected as validation errors rather than throwing or passing silently, one confirming a `version: 999` profile throws the new \"newer version\" error on read instead of being silently accepted.\n\nVerified: re-ran the exact probe that found the bug - overlength and numeric `productOther` now both return `{productOther: \"...\"}` from `validate()` instead of an empty object or a thrown exception; a `version: 999` profile now throws instead of reading successfully. Loaded the real `new-project.html` with a `version: 999` profile already in `localStorage` and confirmed zero uncaught page errors and that the existing `storageError` display path (already wired in `experience.js`) surfaces the new message correctly - no new error-handling UI needed. Full 157-page regression sweep: 0 console errors. All 13 unit tests pass (11 existing + 2 new)."}, {"title": "Foundation audit X10 (P2) - no per-Foundation release/governance contract existed", "body": "Finding from the 5 October 2026 Foundation audit: the shared Accessibility guide's \"Known gaps - not yet done\" section honestly flags unverified zoom/reflow and text-spacing checks, but nothing combined that with a release contract per Foundation section - no documented compatibility/deprecation rule, no acceptance checklist, no stated ownership.\n\nAdded a new \"Foundation release contract\" section to `accessibility.html`, one row per Foundation section (Colors, Grid & Layout, Typography, Spacing, Radius, Borders, Shadows, Icons, AI & Agents), each stating: what counts as an additive vs. breaking change to that section's own tokens, specifically (not generic boilerplate - Colors' breaking change is a renamed CSS custom property, Icons' is an uploaded reference no longer resolving, Typography's is a renamed font-size token, etc.), how a breaking change gets deprecated instead of silently breaking a saved project (grounded in patterns this system already uses elsewhere - e.g. `PROJECT_PRODUCT_TYPE_MAP`'s slug aliasing, `project-model.js`'s `LEGACY_PRODUCT_MAP` migration-on-read), and what evidence makes a change in that section release-ready. Stated ownership once, plainly: one team owns all nine sections today, a legitimate choice at this system's size (already an established, deliberate decision noted elsewhere in this changelog), not restated as a gap per row.\n\nBuilt it using the same `.component-guide` + `.usage-row` pattern as the \"Known gaps\" section directly above it, which meant it automatically gets a real, versioned Machine View for free via the X08 fix shipped earlier today - no separate JSON to hand-author or keep in sync.\n\nCaught and fixed a real layout bug while building this: `.usage-row`'s CSS (`shell.css`) gives its `values` column a fixed 190px, `white-space:nowrap` fixed-width treatment meant for short tags like \"Unverified\" - my first draft put full-sentence compatibility rules there, which overflowed the row horizontally. Fixed by swapping which column carries which content: the long compatibility/deprecation/acceptance prose now lives in `desc` (the column that actually wraps), and `values` carries a short 2-4 word tag per row (e.g. \"Alias, don't remove\", \"Migrate on read\").\n\nVerified: screenshotted the new section and confirmed no horizontal overflow and correct text wrapping after the column swap. Opened Machine View and confirmed `$schema: \"ads.machine-view.v1\"` plus all 9 section entries extract correctly with `label`/`values`/`description` populated. Full 157-page regression sweep: 0 console errors. All 11 existing unit tests still pass."}, {"title": "Foundation audit X09 (P2) - product/platform taxonomy drifted across Spacing, Radius and Typography", "body": "Finding from the 5 October 2026 Foundation audit: \"Spacing lacks Consumer App/AI Product recommendations; Data-Heavy labels vary; Typography uses a different platform taxonomy.\"\n\nConfirmed all three independently. Spacing's own product-card set was 10, not Radius/Grid & Layout's 12 - missing Consumer App and AI Product entirely (a previously-documented, not-yet-actioned gap from earlier this session, now actioned). Added both cards to `spacing.html`, each with a grounded recommendation rather than an arbitrary one: 4px primary / 8px secondary, matching the same treatment Spacing already gives Mobile Web/Mobile App/SaaS/Dashboard - the same device/density character Grid & Layout and Radius already assign these two products (4-col mobile-like grid for Consumer App, 12-col SaaS-like grid for AI Product). Removed the now-outdated code comment in `spacing.js` explaining why those two \"safely resolve to no selection\" on this page, and updated `PROJECT_PRODUCT_TYPE_MAP`'s own comment - the sync now covers all 12 categories here, same as Radius and Grid & Layout.\n\nData-Heavy's label read \"Data-Heavy Enterprise\" on both Spacing and Radius, but \"Data-Heavy Application\" on Grid & Layout and in New Project's own canonical `PRODUCTS` list (`project-model.js`) - the same `data-product=\"data-heavy\"` slug under two different display names depending which page you're on. Renamed both to \"Data-Heavy Application\" to match the canonical name.\n\nTypography's \"Type scale\" tab has its own 4-preset platform taxonomy (SaaS/Mobile App/Desktop and Web App/M Web & D Web) with real, working font-size recommendations per preset (verified: not dead code, each preset genuinely changes every type-scale row's size) - confirmed it doesn't correspond to New Project's 10-category product list or Spacing/Radius/Grid & Layout's 12. Mechanically forcing this onto the same taxonomy would mean inventing speculative size recommendations for 8 more categories with no typographic rationale to differ from one of the 4 existing presets, which is a design decision beyond what this consistency finding warrants. Instead, documented the deliberate reason it diverges directly above `PLATFORM_PRESETS` in `typography.js`: a type-scale starting point is a function of device/viewport context, not product category, so a 1:1 sync (the way Spacing/Radius/Grid & Layout sync from New Project's product type) doesn't apply here.\n\nVerified: `spacing.html` now renders 12 product cards (confirmed via live DOM, not just source), Consumer App and AI Product both produce the correct recommendation callout text. Set a saved project profile with `productType: 'Consumer App'` and separately `'AI Product'`, cleared Spacing's own storage key, reloaded fresh each time, and confirmed the correct card now auto-selects for both (previously neither resolved to anything on this page). Confirmed \"Data-Heavy Application\" now renders identically on Spacing, Radius and Grid & Layout. Full 157-page regression sweep: 0 console errors. All 11 existing unit tests still pass.\n\nKnown follow-up, not in scope here: `docs-catalog.json`/`docs-catalog.js` still reference the old \"Data-Heavy Enterprise\" label (part of the broader stale-catalog gap tracked separately as audit finding X01); will be caught up when that finding is addressed."}, {"title": "Foundation audit X08 (P2) - Machine Views had no version or null/error policy", "body": "Finding from the 5 October 2026 Foundation audit (`foundation-audit-report-2026-10-05.md`): every page's static (auto-extracted) and dynamic (per-page JS) Machine View JSON used an unrelated, undocumented shape with no version marker, so an AI agent or script consuming one had no way to detect a breaking shape change or know whether a missing field meant \"not applicable\" or \"not yet configured.\"\n\nAdded one shared constant, `MACHINE_VIEW_SCHEMA = 'ads.machine-view.v1'` in `experience.js`, exposed as `window.ADS_MACHINE_VIEW_SCHEMA` so every page-specific generator stamps the identical string instead of each hardcoding its own copy that could drift. Added `$schema` as the first key to all 15 dynamic Machine View generators across `borders.js` (3), `grid-layout.js` (2), `icons.js` (3), `radius.js` (2), `shadows.js` (1), `spacing.js` (2), `typography.js` (2), plus the one loop-based generator in `foundations.js` covering Brand/Background/Status/Neutral, and the generic auto-extraction path in `experience.js`'s `autoMachineViews()` (used by any `.component-guide` section left at the `{}` placeholder - 157 pages share this function, though far fewer actually trigger it). Also fixed a real inconsistency found while doing this: the auto-extractor's `stats` mapping always emitted `label`/`value` keys with `null` when absent, while its own `items` mapping in the same function already omitted the key instead - standardized both to the omit-when-absent convention, and documented it as the systemwide null policy in a comment block above the schema constant (a key present means known/configured; absent means not yet set - never a literal `null`).\n\nScope boundary, stated plainly rather than silently narrowed: this covers every JS-generated Machine View, but roughly 77 pages also contain hand-authored static JSON blocks typed directly into the HTML source (bypassing both generation paths entirely, since the auto-extractor only acts on a literal `{}` placeholder and never overwrites real content). Retrofitting a version stamp onto each of those by hand is a separate, much larger undertaking than this one finding warrants, and is not attempted here. Per-field type/unit typing (e.g. `{value:24, unit:\"px\"}` instead of a bare number) was also left alone, since changing the actual field shapes - rather than additively stamping them - risks breaking whatever already parses today's flat values.\n\nVerified: loaded spacing.html/grid-layout.html/radius.html/borders.html/shadows.html/icons.html/typography.html/foundations.html with a selection made on each, read every dynamic Machine View's live JSON and confirmed `$schema: \"ads.machine-view.v1\"` is present and valid JSON in each. Confirmed the generic auto-extractor also stamps correctly on a genuine `{}`-placeholder section (borders.html's \"Component guidance\" panel) and, separately, that it correctly leaves hand-authored static blocks (e.g. the same page's Breakpoints-style panels) untouched rather than overwriting them. Full 157-page regression sweep: 0 console errors. All 11 existing unit tests still pass."}, {"title": "Grid & Layout - the page's own small diagram graphics didn't follow the 4px spacing rule", "body": "Asked to make Grid & Layout follow \"the 4px rule\" - Spacing Foundation's own documented base unit, where every real spacing token in the system is a multiple of 4 (4, 8, 12, 16, 20, 24...). Audited every CSS rule actually used by this page's own content (not the shared shell chrome, which many other pages also depend on and wasn't in scope) for a spacing value that breaks that scale, rather than the whole 6,700-line stylesheet - most of shell.css's other odd-numbered paddings (6px/10px/14px/18px) belong to other pages' own component-demo visuals, out of scope here.\n\nFound 5 real offenders, all specific to this page: the 4 small grid-type diagrams (`.grid-demo-columns`/`-rows`/`-cellgrid`/`-baseline`, the little red boxes inside the Columns/Rows/Columns+Rows/Baseline Grid cards) and the 8 layout-pattern diagrams (`.pattern-diagram`, inside Single Column/Two Column/Dashboard/etc.) all used `padding: 6px` - not on the 4px scale. Changed all 5 to `padding: 8px`, the nearest real token.\n\nVerified: computed padding on both `.grid-demo-columns` and `.pattern-diagram` now reads `8px`. Screenshotted the Grid type and Layout patterns sections - all 12 diagrams render with proportionally more breathing room and no clipping or overlap (each diagram box has a fixed height with `box-sizing:border-box`, so the extra 2px per side simply tightens the visible content area slightly, nothing broke). Confirmed via `grep -rl` that `.grid-demo-*` and `.pattern-diagram` are used only on this page, so no other page is affected. Full 157-page regression sweep: 0 console errors. All 11 existing unit tests still pass."}, {"title": "New Project / MD Export - the Draft-saved status message and help paragraph had collapsed to zero spacing", "body": "Reported too little space between the Continue/Browse without setup button row, the \"Draft saved on this browser.\" status message, and the help paragraph below it (screenshotted on New Project's step 1). Measured directly rather than trusting the source: `.setup-status{margin:16px 0}` is the rule meant to give this element its own breathing room, but it was computing to `margin: 0px` on the live page - a genuine regression from the earlier \"heading-to-body gap\" fix. That fix added `.setup-panel p{margin:0}` to stop a `<p>`'s unset default margin from fighting an adjacent heading's margin, but `.setup-panel p` (class + type selector) is *more specific* than `.setup-status` (a single class), so it silently won the cascade and zeroed out `.setup-status`'s own explicit 16px margin too - an unintended side effect on a different element sharing the same tag, not something the original fix's own verification pass would have caught since the status message is only populated after a real edit, not on a fresh page load.\n\nFixed by raising `.setup-status`'s selector to `.setup-panel .setup-status` (two classes), which now outranks `.setup-panel p`'s class+type specificity and restores its intended 16px top/bottom margin without touching the general `<p>` reset that fixed the original heading-gap bug. `.setup-status`/`#exportStatus` is shared by both New Project's setup form and MD Export's export panel, both nested inside `.setup-panel`, so this applies identically to both.\n\nVerified: triggered the real \"Draft saved on this browser.\" status (by editing the Project name field, not just reading the source) and measured computed margin as `16px 0px`, with actual rendered gaps of 16px above and below the status line, on both New Project and MD Export. Full 157-page regression sweep: 0 console errors. All 11 existing unit tests still pass."}, {"title": "New Project / MD Export - removed the box outline around each platform/export checkbox entirely", "body": "Follow-up to the previous checked-state color fix: asked to remove the outline/stroke entirely, showing just the checkbox itself rather than a bordered box around it. Removed `border:1px solid var(--line-strong)` and `border-radius:8px` from `.setup-option` in `experience.css` - the only two declarations that drew the box - leaving the checkbox and its label as plain inline content with no surrounding shape. Same shared class as the previous fix, so this applies identically to New Project's platform picker and MD Export's Foundations/Component-category checkboxes.\n\nVerified: computed style on `.setup-option` now shows `border: 0px none`, `border-radius: 0px`, no background - screenshotted both New Project's \"Where will your product be used?\" and MD Export's \"Which foundation sections should be included?\" and confirmed both now read as a plain checkbox list with no box around either. Full 157-page regression sweep: 0 console errors. All 11 existing unit tests still pass."}, {"title": "New Project / MD Export - removed the colored border and background on a checked platform/export checkbox", "body": "Reported, with a screenshot, that a checked option in \"Where will your product be used? Select all that apply.\" showed a blue border and light blue background, and asked for both to be removed. Checked the actual CSS rule driving this state - `.setup-option:has(:checked){border-color:var(--red-500);background:var(--red-tint)}` in `experience.css` - and rendered it directly via Playwright in both Chromium and WebKit (with a real `.click()`-triggered check, not just a static attribute), getting `rgb(255, 3, 26)`/`rgba(255, 3, 26, 0.07)` - red, not blue - in both engines; found no stacking focus ring or other rule that would produce blue either. Could not reproduce the reported color, but the instruction itself (remove the border and background on the checked state) was unambiguous and actionable regardless of the exact shade, so proceeded with the removal as asked rather than blocking on an unexplained rendering difference.\n\nRemoved the `.setup-option:has(:checked)` rule entirely from `experience.css`, so a checked option now renders with the same plain `1px solid var(--line-strong)` border and no background as an unchecked one - selection is still clearly visible from the native checkbox's own red `accent-color` fill, just without the extra box styling. `.setup-option` is shared markup, used by both New Project's platform-selection step and MD Export's Foundations/Component-category checkboxes (`.export-category-summary .setup-option`), so this change applies identically to both pages, not just the one in the screenshot. Left `.export-category-summary .setup-option:has(:checked){background:none}` in place even though it's now redundant (the rule it was overriding no longer sets a background) - harmless, and removing it is a separate cleanup, not part of what was asked.\n\nVerified: computed `border-color`/`background-color` on a checked `.setup-option` now exactly match the unchecked default (`rgba(15, 15, 10, 0.15)` border, transparent background) on both `new-project.html` and `md-export.html`. Full 157-page regression sweep: 0 console errors. All 11 existing unit tests still pass."}, {"title": "New Project - heading-to-body gap was 16px, not the 12px the heading's own rule says", "body": "Reported too much space between \"Your product and platforms\" and the paragraph below it. Measured directly rather than assumed: the gap rendered as 16px, even though `.setup-panel h2{margin:0 0 12px}` explicitly says 12px. Root cause: `.setup-panel p` never set its own `margin` at all, so it fell back to the browser's default `<p>` margin (`1em`, 16px at this font-size) on both sides. Adjacent vertical margins collapse to the larger of the two, not the sum, so the heading's own authored 12px was silently losing to the paragraph's unset default every time - the rule said 12px, but a value that was never actually written anywhere was the one actually winning.\n\nFixed by giving `.setup-panel p` an explicit `margin:0`, so the heading's `margin-bottom:12px` is the sole, intended mechanism above it, and `.setup-fields`'s own deliberate `margin-top:24px` is the sole mechanism below it (that one was already winning the collapse regardless, zeroed for the same reason - consistency, not because it was visibly wrong there too).\n\nVerified: measured both gaps directly - heading-to-body now exactly 12px (matching the heading's own rule for the first time), body-to-fields exactly 24px (matching `.setup-fields`'s own rule). Confirmed Step 2 (\"About you\") shows the same correct 12px after actually navigating to it (a hidden step reads 0px for any rect-based measurement, so checked it visible, not hidden). Checked `.setup-panel p` isn't used in a conflicting way on the other two pages that share `.setup-panel` (`md-export.html`, `settings.html`) - screenshotted both, no regression, since neither has this exact `h2` immediately followed by `p` pattern in the same way. Full 157-page regression sweep: 0 console errors. All 11 existing unit tests still pass."}, {"title": "New Project - the three field questions had mismatched font size, weight and line height", "body": "Reported that \"Project name\", \"What type of product are you creating?\" and \"Where will your product be used? Select all that apply.\" didn't match in size/weight/line height. Confirmed via computed styles, not just a visual guess: the first two are plain text inside `<label class=\"setup-field\">` (14px, weight 500), but the third is a `<legend>` inside `<fieldset class=\"setup-platforms\">` - `.setup-platforms legend` set `font-weight:600` but never set a `font-size` at all, so it fell back to the browser's default `<legend>` size (16px). Different element, different rule, nothing forcing the two to agree - confirmed computed values were genuinely 14px/500 vs 16px/600 before touching anything.\n\nAdded `font-size:14px` and changed `font-weight` from `600` to `500` on `.setup-platforms legend`, matching `.setup-field` exactly rather than picking a third value - the two existing field labels were the majority style on this form (2 of the eventual 3), so the legend was the one that needed to conform.\n\nChecked where else this class is used before shipping: `.setup-platforms` also appears on `md-export.html`'s \"Which foundation sections should be included?\" legend, where this exact same mismatch existed against a *different* nearby header (\"Which components should be included?\", a later addition built as a plain `.setup-field` div rather than a legend) - this fix resolves that latent inconsistency too, not just the one reported.\n\nVerified: computed `font-size`/`font-weight`/`line-height` for the label and the legend are now identical (14px/500/normal, matching exactly) on New Project. Screenshotted both New Project and MD Export to confirm all the affected headers now read at the same size and weight. Full 157-page regression sweep: 0 console errors. All 5 project-model unit tests and all 6 overview-model unit tests still pass."}, {"title": "New Project - removed 3 product types that duplicated the separate platform question", "body": "Reported \"duplicate items\" in the product-type dropdown. Checked the live rendered list and its underlying values twice (once on a plain page load, once simulating forward/back navigation through the setup steps) and found no literal repeated entries - every option appeared exactly once, matching the screenshot sent. Asked for a screenshot to pin down the actual complaint rather than guess and remove the wrong thing.\n\nThe follow-up screenshot showed the real issue: it wasn't two identical entries in one list, it was the *same concept* asked twice across two different questions on the same form - \"What type of product are you creating?\" included Desktop Web (D-Web), Mobile Web (M-Web) and Mobile Application, while \"Where will your product be used? Select all that apply.\" (a separate, pre-existing question on the same step) already asks Desktop web, Mobile web and Mobile app. Product type is supposed to capture *what the product is* (SaaS, Dashboard, Marketing site); platform is supposed to capture *where it runs* - those three categories were asking the platform question a second time under the product-type label instead.\n\nRemoved `'Desktop Web (D-Web)'`, `'Mobile Web (M-Web)'` and `'Mobile Application'` from `project-model.js`'s `PRODUCTS` (back to 9 real categories + Other), added all three to `LEGACY_PRODUCT_MAP` so a profile saved with one of them in the few minutes they existed still reads back migrated (Desktop Web/Mobile Web → Web Application, Mobile Application → Consumer App) instead of failing validation, and removed the same three now-unreachable entries from `spacing.js`/`radius.js`/`grid-layout.js`'s `PROJECT_PRODUCT_TYPE_MAP`, updating each file's comment to explain why New Project's list is intentionally 9, not all 12 of these pages' own categories.\n\nVerified: New Project's dropdown now renders exactly 11 options (10 real categories + Other), confirmed live. Set a profile to each of the three removed values directly in storage and confirmed `M.read()` migrates all three correctly with no thrown error. Re-ran the 3-page sync matrix for the remaining 9 categories - still exactly as before (7 sync everywhere, Consumer App/AI Product still correctly no-op on Spacing only). Full 157-page regression sweep: 0 console errors. All 5 unit tests still pass."}, {"title": "New Project's product-type list now matches Spacing/Radius/Grid & Layout's own categories exactly", "body": "Asked to check whether New Project's \"What type of product are you creating?\" offered the full category set already used on the Foundation pages' own \"What are you building?\" cards (SaaS, Enterprise SaaS, Web Application, Desktop Web (D-Web), Mobile Web (M-Web), Mobile Application, Dashboard, Data-Heavy Application, Marketing Website, Consumer App, AI Product, Cross-Platform) and add them if missing. Checked `project-model.js`'s `PRODUCTS` list: it didn't - it still had its own, older, broader 6-category list (SaaS, Enterprise portal, Customer portal, Internal tool, Commerce, Content website) that the previous round's `PROJECT_PRODUCT_TYPE_MAP` had to fuzzy-match against, since only \"SaaS\" lined up exactly.\n\nReplaced `PRODUCTS` with the exact 12 category names from the pages' own `product-card-name` elements (verified against source, not retyped from memory, to avoid introducing new spelling drift), keeping \"Other\" as a 13th option so a user whose product genuinely doesn't fit any of the 12 can still describe it in free text - removing it entirely would have taken away that option without the user asking for that.\n\nThis directly obsoletes the fuzzy guessing from the previous round - with the two lists now identical, updated the `PROJECT_PRODUCT_TYPE_MAP` in `spacing.js`/`radius.js`/`grid-layout.js` from \"closest real card\" guesses to the exact, authoritative name-to-slug conversion (e.g. `\"Desktop Web (D-Web)\" → \"desktop-web\"`), covering all 12 categories now instead of 5.\n\nHandled the one real risk this change created: an existing saved project profile using one of the old category names (e.g. \"Internal tool\") would now fail `PRODUCTS.includes()` validation and make `M.read()` throw \"Saved setup is incomplete\" - silently losing that user's saved project the next time any page loaded it. Added a `LEGACY_PRODUCT_MAP` and a remap step inside `read()` itself (Enterprise portal → Enterprise SaaS, Customer portal → Consumer App, Internal tool → Dashboard, Commerce → Web Application, Content website → Marketing Website) that runs before validation, so old saved data keeps working transparently instead of breaking. Updated the existing unit test's fixture, which used the now-removed \"Enterprise portal,\" and added a new test asserting the migration itself.\n\nVerified: New Project's dropdown now renders exactly the 12 requested categories plus \"Other,\" confirmed against the live DOM, not just the source array. Set a legacy profile (`productType: 'Internal tool'`) directly in storage and confirmed `M.read()` returns it remapped to `'Dashboard'` with no thrown error, and that Grid & Layout correctly auto-selects the Dashboard card from it. Re-ran the full 12-category × 3-page matrix: 10 categories now sync exactly everywhere; Consumer App and AI Product correctly no-op on Spacing only (its smaller 10-card set still lacks those two - a separate, already-flagged gap, not something this change introduced or fixed). Full 157-page regression sweep: 0 console errors. All 5 unit tests pass (4 existing + 1 new)."}, {"title": "Spacing, Radius and Grid & Layout now pick up New Project's product type on first visit", "body": "Reported: selecting a product type in New Project's \"What type of product are you creating?\" was expected to auto-select the matching card in each Foundation page's own \"What are you building?\" picker, but wasn't. Investigated rather than assumed a specific page was broken: grepped all three pages' JS for any reference to the saved project profile at all and found none - `spacing.js`, `radius.js` and `grid-layout.js` each only ever read and write their *own* independent `localStorage` key (`ads:spacing`, `ads:radius`, `ads:grid-layout`), with zero connection to New Project's profile (`ads:project-profile:v1`). This had never been wired up on any of the three pages, not a regression on one.\n\nFound a second, deeper issue while scoping the fix: New Project's product-type list (SaaS, Enterprise portal, Customer portal, Internal tool, Commerce, Content website, Other) uses a completely different, broader taxonomy than these pages' own product cards (saas, enterprise-saas, web-app, desktop-web, mobile-web, mobile-app, dashboard, data-heavy, marketing, consumer-app, ai-product, cross-platform) - only \"SaaS\" matches exactly. A straight 1:1 lookup would only ever work for that one case, so added a `PROJECT_PRODUCT_TYPE_MAP` (duplicated identically across all three files, since each is a standalone script) mapping the other five to the closest real card: Enterprise portal → enterprise-saas, Customer portal → consumer-app, Internal tool → dashboard, Commerce → web-app, Content website → marketing. \"Other\" is intentionally left unmapped - the user described their own product type in free text, which none of these cards represent, so no default is defensible there.\n\nWired the mapping into each page's existing `loadX()` function, only on the branch that already ran when nothing had been saved on that specific page yet (first visit) - a user's own click on any card, now or in the past, still always wins and is never overridden by this fallback. Deliberately did not persist the mapped selection to that page's own storage key automatically, matching the existing convention that loading (as opposed to a real click) never writes to storage.\n\nVerified: tested all 7 New Project product types against all 3 pages fresh (profile set, page's own storage cleared) - the 6 mappable types correctly auto-select the right card on all 3 pages; \"Other\" correctly auto-selects nothing anywhere; \"Customer portal\" correctly auto-selects on Radius and Grid & Layout but safely no-ops on Spacing specifically, since Spacing's own card set (10, not 12) has no \"consumer-app\" equivalent - documented, not a bug. Confirmed a user's manual click on a different card persists through a reload and is never reverted back to the mapped default. Confirmed a user with no saved project profile at all still sees nothing auto-selected, same as before this fix. Full 157-page regression sweep: 0 console errors. All 10 existing unit tests still pass."}, {"title": "Colors Foundation - removed Text colors, Typography's per-level colors is now the one place for text color", "body": "Reported: the \"primary color\" concept was showing up twice - once in Colors, once in \"the text\" - and asked to keep it in Typography, removing it from Colors. Confirmed by comparing both: Colors Foundation's \"Text colors\" tab configured 3 broad tokens (`--text-hi`, `--text-mid`, `--text-dim`) used for general body/UI text sitewide, while Typography's \"Additional semantic levels\" tab already has its own, more elaborate system - each of ~15 heading/text levels (H1, H2, Body, etc.) gets its own independent Light/Dark color pair. Asked for explicit confirmation before touching this, since removing the wrong one would affect text color sitewide, not just headings - confirmed \"Text colors\" was the one to remove.\n\nRemoved the entire \"Text colors\" `<section>` from `foundations.html` (tab, fields, Save/Reset) - the page's tab bar is auto-generated from `.panel-title` elements, so no separate tab-list edit was needed. In `foundations.js`, removed `textDarkEnable`/`textDarkCol`/`textSection`/`TEXT_KEY`/`persistText`/`loadText`/`applyTextLiveForActiveTheme` and the Save/Reset handlers wholesale, dropped `text` from the Dark Mode sync array (now 4 sections, not 5) and from the Markdown/Machine-View generator's section list, and dropped `text-colors` from `experience.js`'s `TOKEN_KEYS`/`FOUNDATION_TOKEN_MAP` (MD Export's Colors checkbox now covers 4 keys instead of 5). Also fixed a stale `\"appliesTo\"` array in the \"Light & dark theme behavior\" Machine View JSON that still listed `\"text-colors\"`.\n\nFound one real dependency before it could break anything: the contrast-badge logic for Background swatches (\"is a surface still readable against primary text?\") read its comparison value live from the Text-Hi input fields being removed - `document.querySelector('[data-role=\"text-light-hi-hex\"]')` would have returned `null` for an element that no longer exists, throwing on every Background contrast check. Replaced the live reads with the fixed `TEXT_DEFAULTS.light.hi`/`.dark.hi` constants directly (kept, not removed, for exactly this reason), since there's no more live input to read from.\n\nVerified: full 157-page regression sweep found this before it shipped (checked before, not after, committing) - 0 console errors on `foundations.html` and everywhere else. Colors' tab bar now shows 6 tabs instead of 7, with no gap or leftover reference. Background's contrast badges still compute correct, passing values (16.60:1, 17.96:1, 17.34:1 \"vs primary text\", all AA/AAA ✓) using the static reference. Dark Mode sync still works correctly across the remaining 4 sections. MD Export's Colors export still generates without error. All 10 existing unit tests still pass."}, {"title": "Colors Foundation - Dark Mode is now one setting, not five independent ones", "body": "Reported: enabling/disabling Dark Mode from the Brand (Primary) color section didn't carry over to the other Color Foundation sections. Confirmed in source: Brand, Background & surface elevation, Semantic & status colors, Neutral scale, and Text colors each have their own \"Also set a separate ... for dark theme\" checkbox (`brand-dark-enable`, `bg-dark-enable`, `status-dark-enable`, `neutral-dark-enable`, `text-dark-enable`), and each one was wired only to its own section's saved data - toggling one had zero effect on the other four, exactly as reported.\n\nAdded a shared `syncColorsDarkMode(enabled)` in `foundations.js` that all 5 checkboxes now call on change, setting every checkbox's checked state and every section's dark-column visibility together - deliberately left the underlying dark color *values* and each section's own Save button untouched, so a user can still customize Background's dark colors differently from Text's; only the enable/show toggle is unified, not the color choices themselves. Also routed all 5 Reset buttons through the same function, since resetting one section's dark customization left the other four still enabled - the report's \"if Dark Mode is not selected, it should not be enabled anywhere\" applies there too.\n\nAdded one reconciliation pass after all 5 sections finish loading their own saved state: if any section already had dark data saved from before this fix, Dark Mode now reads as enabled everywhere on load, rather than leaving the other four looking disabled relative to the one that happened to have saved data.\n\nVerified: checking any one of the 5 checkboxes (tested via Brand and Neutral) checks all 5 and reveals all 5 dark-color columns; unchecking any one (tested via Text) unchecks all 5 and hides all 5 columns; clicking Reset on a single section (tested via Status) now disables Dark Mode everywhere instead of just that section. Loaded the page with only Brand's dark data pre-saved (simulating a user from before this fix) and confirmed all 5 read as enabled after reload, with the other 4 sections showing valid auto-generated dark values rather than broken/empty fields. Confirmed per-section Save independence still holds - enabling Dark Mode and saving only Brand left Background's own storage key untouched (`null`), exactly as before. Full 157-page regression sweep: 0 console errors. All 10 existing unit tests still pass."}, {"title": "MD Export - Button's single checkbox hid that it actually has two real options", "body": "Told to check properly - Button has two options. Investigated rather than guessed: `components.html`'s own Button section renders exactly 2 cards, `data-system=\"primary\"` targeting `button-primary.html` and `data-system=\"primary-icon\"` targeting `button-primary-icon.html` - these are Button's two real, listing-screen-reachable configurations. The single \"Button\" checkbox from two rounds ago collapsed both into one, silently deciding they were always exported or excluded together - losing the real distinction the listing screen itself draws.\n\nChecked the other 6 `button-*.html` files (ghost, link, neutral, secondary, tertiary, destructive) before deciding what to do with them, rather than assuming they belonged with the two real cards: they're full, real pages, not stubs, but `button-ghost.html` and `button-link.html` have zero inbound links anywhere on the site, and the other four are linked only from `guidelines.html`'s own reference table - a documentation page, not a listing screen (a `.system-card` grid with a \"View component guide\" CTA, which is what every other finding in this thread has meant by that term).\n\nFixed by splitting the one \"Button\" checkbox into its two real options, labeled to match the cards themselves (\"Primary\", \"Primary Icon\") rather than the catalog's generic doc titles, and excluding the other 6 from the picker entirely via a new `EXCLUDED_FILES` set - consistent with the same principle that drove the original grouping fix: this picker should only ever offer what a user can actually reach from a real listing screen. This does mean those 6 files are no longer reachable through MD Export at all, under any scope, since they were never reachable through this feature's own listing-screen model to begin with.\n\nVerified: Actions now renders exactly 4 items (Primary, Primary Icon, Float Button Stable, Group Button Stable) instead of the previous single \"Button\" row - total selectable items sitewide went from 62 to 63 (Actions' 3 → 4, every other category unchanged). Unchecking just \"Primary Icon\" correctly removes only the dual-icon variant from the regenerated Markdown while \"Primary Button Stable\" remains - confirmed both are independently controllable. With \"components\" scope and everything checked, the export now totals 63 documents (down from the previous 69, since the 6 excluded files are no longer reachable through this feature at all); \"all\" scope with everything checked yields 148 (154 total minus those same 6), consistent with the \"components\" count. Full 157-page regression sweep: 0 console errors. All 10 existing unit tests still pass."}, {"title": "MD Export - category dropdowns had no visible arrow", "body": "Reported that the dropdown arrow wasn't showing on the category `<details>` added two rounds ago. Root cause: `.export-category-summary{display:flex}` - needed to lay the select-all checkbox, category name and count out in a row - silently removes the browser's native disclosure triangle, because that triangle comes from `<summary>`'s default `display:list-item` rendering, which `display:flex` overrides. Confirmed directly (`getComputedStyle(summary).display` read back `\"flex\"`), not assumed.\n\nFixed by adding a real chevron instead of trying to recover the native one: reused the exact same `.chev` SVG markup and rotation convention the sidebar's own collapsible group toggles already use (pointing right collapsed, down when open), rather than inventing a new icon. Also added `list-style:none` and `::-webkit-details-marker{display:none}` on the summary so no browser can show a native marker *and* the new chevron at once, and gave the count span `margin-right:auto` so the chevron sits pinned to the right edge of the row instead of immediately after the count.\n\nVerified: all 5 category summaries now render exactly one `.chev` each (5 total). Confirmed it rotates correctly on open/close by screenshotting both states. Re-confirmed the select-all checkbox's `stopPropagation()` still works with the chevron present (clicking it toggles children without opening the panel), and that clicking the chevron itself does open the panel, as a real disclosure control should. `node --check` passed. Full 157-page regression sweep: 0 console errors. All 10 existing unit tests still pass."}, {"title": "MD Export - Actions listed 10 \"components\" when the real listing screens only have 3", "body": "Reported with a screenshot of the Actions dropdown showing 10 checkboxes - Destructive/Ghost/Link/Neutral/Primary(x2)/Secondary/Tertiary Button, Float Button, Group Button - asking why the listing screens don't show that many. Checked the sidebar's actual Actions subgroup: 3 links (Button, Group Button, Float Button). Checked every other category the same way (Forms 22, Navigation 8, Feedback 12, Data Display 17) and every one matches its catalog count exactly - Actions was the only mismatch. Root cause: Button alone has 8 separate style-variant pages (button-primary.html, button-ghost.html, etc.) that `tools/audit_pages.py` crawled into the catalog as 8 separate Detail docs, but the real site only ever surfaces them as ONE item - the sidebar links to a single \"Button\" entry, and `components.html`'s own listing cards for Button only show 2 of the 8 variants, not all of them. The per-component dropdown added last round took the catalog at face value and offered all 8 as if they were 8 real, separately-navigable components.\n\nFixed by grouping: added a `COMPONENT_GROUPS` map (currently just `Actions: [{ label: 'Button', match: file => file.startsWith('button-') }]`) that `buildComponentCategoryGroups()` applies before rendering - any doc whose file matches a defined group gets folded into that group's single checkbox instead of getting its own row; every doc in every other category is unaffected, since none of them have a group defined (because none of them need one). The grouped checkbox's `data-files` now holds a JSON array instead of one filename, and the filtering logic flattens every checked checkbox's files into the allow-list instead of reading one file each.\n\nVerified: Actions now renders exactly 3 items (Button, Float Button Stable, Group Button Stable), matching the sidebar precisely; the other 4 categories' counts are untouched (22/8/12/17, confirmed unchanged). Unchecking \"Button\" drops exactly 8 documents from the export (69 → 61) while Float Button and Group Button remain present in the regenerated Markdown, confirming the grouping doesn't also catch its siblings. Total selectable items dropped from 69 to 62 (3+22+8+12+17), but all 69 underlying files are still reachable - checking everything still produces the full 69-document export, same as before this fix. Full 157-page regression sweep: 0 console errors. All 10 existing unit tests still pass."}, {"title": "MD Export - category checkboxes became dropdowns with per-component selection", "body": "Asked to go one level deeper than the 5 category checkboxes added last round - a dropdown per category so individual components can be selected \"at a micro level,\" not just whole categories. Replaced the flat checkbox row with 5 collapsed `<details>` elements, one per category, each holding its own \"select whole category\" checkbox in the `<summary>` next to the native disclosure triangle, plus one checkbox per individual component inside (69 total across the 5 groups - Actions 10, Forms 22, Navigation 8, Feedback 12, Data Display 17).\n\nBuilt entirely from `window.ADSDocsCatalog` at runtime (`buildComponentCategoryGroups()` in `experience.js`) rather than hand-written in the HTML, reusing the same `docCategory()` breadcrumb-parsing helper added last round - so the list of components and their categories can never drift out of sync with the catalog that actually drives the export. Filtering changed from matching a doc's category against a Set of checked category names to matching the doc's own `file` against a Set of checked component checkboxes' `data-file` - more direct, and the natural fit now that selection happens per-component rather than per-category.\n\nThe category-level \"select all\" checkbox stays in sync with its children in both directions: checking/unchecking it toggles every component in that group, and checking/unchecking an individual component updates the group checkbox to checked, unchecked, or indeterminate depending on how many of its children are checked. Clicking that checkbox calls `stopPropagation()` so it doesn't also trigger the native `<details>` open/close toggle the browser would otherwise fire for any click landing inside `<summary>`.\n\nVerified: confirmed exactly 5 `<details>` elements and 69 `.export-component-check` checkboxes render, with per-category counts matching the catalog exactly (10/22/8/12/17). Clicked a category's select-all checkbox and confirmed the `<details>` panel stayed closed (`stopPropagation` working) while all its children toggled off, then on again. Opened a category via its summary text and unchecked one individual component - the group checkbox correctly went indeterminate, and the status line correctly dropped to \"68/69 components.\" With scope \"all\" and every category's select-all unchecked, got exactly 85 documents (67 Listing + 18 Tab, 0 of 69 components) with Download still enabled, matching the same graceful empty-selection behavior verified for Foundations. Full 157-page regression sweep: 0 console errors. All 10 existing unit tests still pass."}, {"title": "MD Export - same selection, extended to component categories", "body": "Asked to extend the same selection-based export to Components and their categories. `docs-catalog.js` has no literal \"category\" field, but every component Detail doc's own `markdown` starts with the exact breadcrumb its real page shows (\"Components · Forms · Add-on\"), matching the sidebar's 5 `.nav-subgroup-label` groups under Components (Actions, Forms, Navigation, Feedback, Data Display) exactly. Parsed the category from that existing breadcrumb at runtime instead of changing the generated catalog file or re-running its generator.\n\nAdded 5 checkboxes, same `.setup-platforms`/`.setup-option` pattern as the Foundations fieldset, all checked by default. The category filter only ever narrows the existing scope-based `docs` list, applied after it (not instead of it): a doc whose markdown doesn't start with that breadcrumb - a Listing or Tab-kind page, only ever included under the \"all\" scope - has no parseable category and always passes through untouched, so unchecking every category can't accidentally drop documentation that was never a \"component\" to begin with. The status line now also reports \"N/5 component categories\" whenever the scope includes any documentation, mirroring the Foundations summary.\n\nVerified: with scope set to \"components\" and all 5 categories checked, got all 69 component guides (427 KB); unchecked Forms and got exactly 47 (69 minus Forms' 22 entries, confirmed against the catalog's own counts); with scope \"all\" and every category unchecked, got 85 documents remaining - exactly the 67 Listing + 18 Tab pages, 0 of the 69 Detail/component guides, confirming non-component docs are never affected by this filter; \"context\" scope correctly omits the category summary and ignores the checkboxes entirely, as before. `node --check` passed. All 10 existing unit tests still pass. Full 157-page regression sweep: 0 console errors."}, {"title": "MD Export - per-foundation-tab selection instead of all-or-nothing", "body": "Asked for a checkbox-based selection on MD Export so the user can pick exactly which Foundations sections go into the exported file, rather than the existing scope dropdown's all-or-nothing behavior - any saved foundation value got bundled into every export regardless of scope, since `M.markdown()` was always handed the complete `savedTokens()` object. Scoped to Foundations for this round, not every exportable area.\n\nAdded 8 checkboxes - one per Foundations sidebar tab (Colors, Grid & Layout, Typography, Spacing, Radius, Borders, Shadows, Icons), all checked by default so existing export behavior is unchanged unless the user deselects something - using the same `.setup-platforms`/`.setup-option` fieldset pattern already used for New Project's platform picker, not a new one-off style.\n\nColors and Borders each map to more than one `localStorage` key (Colors alone covers its own value plus the bg/status/neutral/text themed panels; Borders covers both border and border-states), so added a `FOUNDATION_TOKEN_MAP` in `experience.js` translating each checkbox to the actual key(s) it should pull in, and changed `savedTokens()` to accept an optional allow-list instead of always reading all 13 keys. Each checkbox triggers the same live-preview regeneration as the existing scope dropdown and identity checkbox, and the status line now reports how many of the 8 sections are included alongside the existing page count and file size.\n\nVerified: loaded the page with seeded Colors/Typography/Spacing values, confirmed all 8 checkboxes render and default to checked, confirmed the generated Markdown includes all three by default, then unchecked Colors and Typography and confirmed they disappeared from the regenerated JSON while Spacing remained - status line updated to \"6/8 foundation sections\" correctly. `node --check` passed. All 10 existing unit tests still pass (the Markdown-generation test exercises `M.markdown()` directly with its own token object, unaffected by the `experience.js`-side filtering). Full 157-page regression sweep: 0 console errors."}, {"title": "Started working through usability-qa-audit-report-2026-10-01.md", "body": "Asked to start acting on the findings in a usability/QA audit report the user had read and edited separately (not something this session produced - it covers the whole \"Create Design\" product, not just listing cards). The report numbers 22 findings (2 Critical, 8 High, 8 Medium, 2 Low) plus a recommended 4-phase remediation order. The two Critical items and several High items (a real multi-project data model, an explicit-Save-vs-auto-save decision, a confirmable Reset flow) are product-design decisions, not bugs with one correct fix, so picking those up first without direction risked building the wrong thing. Started instead with the findings that have one unambiguous correct fix and don't need a product decision - four landed this round:\n\n**UQA-021 - shared Settings button missing `type=\"button\"`.** Added it to the one shared markup line duplicated across all 157 pages (no build step, so each page carries its own copy). Confirmed 157/157 updated, 0 remaining.\n\n**UQA-017 - History's version links go nowhere.** `href=\"#\"` on the 3.1.0/3.0.0/2.9.1 version links just jumped to the top of the page instead of opening real version details, which doesn't exist yet. Took the report's \"use non-interactive text in the mockup\" option rather than inventing a version-detail page: converted the three `<a href=\"#\">` to plain `<span>`, same color styling. While in the area, fixed the same anti-pattern the report separately called out in Templates' record-detail mockup (an `<a href=\"#\" class=\"breadcrumb-demo-link\">Invoices</a>` inside an illustrative demo) - dropped just the `href`, matching its sibling `.breadcrumb-demo-current` span, which was already non-interactive; an anchor with no `href` carries no link role at all, cleaner than `href=\"#\"` plus `tabindex=\"-1\"` (the pattern already used on its sibling tab buttons in the same mockup). Left the other `href=\"#\"` instances on Anchor and Breadcrumb's own component pages alone - those are the components' actual subject matter, not bugs.\n\n**UQA-013 - global search doesn't search what it says it searches.** The placeholder read \"Search components, tokens, docs…\" but `initSidebarSearch()` in `shell.js` only ever filtered `.nav-link` text - page content, token names and documentation text were never indexed. Building real content search is a different-scale feature, so took the report's other listed option: renamed the placeholder and `aria-label` (same duplicated-per-page markup, 157 files) to \"Filter navigation…\", which matches what it actually does.\n\n**UQA-014 - 25 save/copy status elements never reach assistive technology.** Every foundation editor's status message followed `el.hidden = false; el.textContent = \"Saved just now\"; setTimeout(() => el.hidden = true, 2500)` - with no `role=\"status\"`/`aria-live`, and even if added, toggling `hidden` and setting text in the same moment is exactly the pattern the report warns can fail to announce. Fixed on the HTML side: removed `hidden`, added `role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\"` to all 25 `<span class=\"save-status\">` elements (same scripted sweep approach as the markup fixes above). Fixed on the JS side, across `foundations.js`, `grid-layout.js`, `borders.js`, `spacing.js`, `shadows.js`, `icons.js`, `radius.js`, `typography.js` and `experience.js`'s shared Markdown-copy handler: the element now stays permanently present (empty by default, so it's invisible with no layout impact - `.save-status` is a plain inline `<span>`), and \"hiding\" it after the 2.5s timeout means clearing its text instead of toggling `hidden`. Did this with a scoped script matching only the known save/markdown-status variable names per file, not a blanket find-replace of `.hidden` - several unrelated elements (a dark-theme preview column, a popover, a dismissible callout) use real `hidden` toggling for legitimate show/hide behavior and needed to stay untouched.\n\n**Verified:** confirmed 0 remaining `.hidden` references on any save/markdown-status variable across all 9 JS files; clicked Save on Foundations' brand color and confirmed the status element has `role=\"status\"`/`aria-live=\"polite\"` from page load (not added only once visible), updates to \"Saved just now\" on click, and clears back to empty text after the timeout; `node --check` passed on all 9 edited JS files; all 10 existing unit tests still pass when run individually (`project-model.test.js`, `overview-model.test.cjs`); full 157-page regression sweep: 0 console errors.\n\n**Not started this round, flagged for a direction conversation:** UQA-001 (the Create flow not producing a real generated/publishable output), UQA-002 (single fixed storage key overwriting the only saved project), UQA-005 (auto-save silently contradicting the visible Save button), UQA-006 (destructive Reset with no confirm/undo), UQA-007/008 (140 unlabeled controls plus visual-only selection state - large and mechanical, but sizable enough to want a confirmed scope before starting), UQA-003/004/009/010/011/012/015/016/018/019/020, UQA-022. These remain exactly as the report described them."}, {"title": "Result's \"Done\" button sat 48px from the label below but only 20px from the description above", "body": "Reported with a screenshot of the Result card's \"Payment successful\" demo, with the uneven space above and below \"Done\" highlighted. Measured directly rather than going by the screenshot alone: 20px between the description and the button, 48px between the button and the \"System Generated X\" label - a real, visually obvious asymmetry, not the same `justify-content:center`-on-short-content issue flagged in the previous entry (confirmed separately: the gap from the label to the panel's own bottom edge was already a correct 16px here).\n\nRoot cause: `.result-demo-panel` (the box holding the icon, title, description and button) has a symmetric `padding: 28px 20px` of its own, wrapped inside `.button-type-demo`'s outer 20px flex gap to the label. The button sits 28px from the *panel's* bottom edge (the panel's own padding), then another 20px of outer gap to the label - 48px total. The description-to-button gap, by contrast, is purely internal to the panel (its own 8px gap plus the actions row's 12px margin-top) and never crosses that outer-gap boundary, so it never picked up the extra 28px.\n\nFixed by zeroing just the panel's `padding-bottom` (top stays 28px, since nothing above it was reported as uneven) - the panel has no visible background or border of its own, so that padding was invisible whitespace doing no visual job beyond adding to whatever came after it. With it at 0, the button is now exactly as far from the label (20px, the outer gap alone) as the description is from the button.\n\nVerified: re-measured both gaps on Result directly - 20px and 20px, where they were 20px and 48px before. Screenshotted the card to confirm the layout now reads as evenly spaced. Confirmed `.result-demo-panel` has only this one instance on the listing page, so no other variant needed the same fix. Full 157-page regression sweep: 0 console errors."}, {"title": "Listing cards - found a real double-spacing bug between the label and the CTA, via direct measurement", "body": "Asked to check the spacing between the \"System Generated X\" label and the CTA below it - no screenshot this time, so measured directly rather than eyeballing it. On Cascader, the gap from the label's own bottom edge to the white panel's bottom edge measured 27px, not the 16px the panel's padding-bottom declares. Same 27px showed up on cards with no flex-centering at all (Affix, Splitter), ruling out centering slack as the cause and pointing at the label itself.\n\nRoot cause: `.dualicon-card-label` is a `<p>` tag. The authored CSS only sets `margin-top: 12px` - it never touches `margin-bottom`, so the browser's default paragraph margin (`1em 0`, ~11px at this font-size) was still in effect on the bottom edge, stacking with the panel's deliberate 16px padding-bottom to produce 27px. This was never an authored value; it was a default nobody had reset, present on every card since `.dualicon-card-label` is used identically everywhere.\n\nFixed by adding `margin-bottom: 0` to `.dualicon-card-label`, the explicit reset the rule was missing.\n\nVerified: re-measured Cascader, Affix, Splitter and Modal - all four now show exactly 16px from label to panel bottom and a clean 32px total to the CTA (16px padding + 8px card gap + 8px CTA margin-top), where all four previously showed 27px/43px. Swept all 62 pages (65 cards) confirming `margin-bottom: 0` computed correctly everywhere. Full 157-page regression sweep: 0 console errors.\n\nWhile sweeping, found a second, separate issue on a handful of cards (Tree, Steps, Progress and others) where the gap is still much larger than 16px - not a margin bug, but `justify-content: center` distributing real leftover space when a card's enforced `min-height: 220px` is much taller than its actual preview content needs. Tree's gap measured 62.5px this way. Left this alone and is flagging it separately rather than folding it into this fix, since the right answer (shrink min-height for short-content cards? change how the extra space distributes?) is a different, more judgment-call kind of decision than resetting an unintended default."}, {"title": "Listing cards - Copy prompt/Copy code were visibly smaller than View component guide", "body": "Asked to fix the size difference between the primary CTA and the two copy buttons below it. Copy prompt/Copy code used `.btn-ghost.btn-sm` (11px text, 28px tall) while View component guide used the base `.btn-primary` size (12px text, 34px tall) - a real, deliberate choice from the earlier \"primary action first\" reorder, meant to make the primary action read as visually stronger. Asked specifically which way to resolve it rather than assume, since reversing part of a previous explicit decision needed a real answer, not a guess; chosen direction: make the copy buttons full-size rather than shrink the CTA or split the difference, so all three buttons share one consistent size and the remaining distinction is color (filled red vs ghost) rather than size.\n\nRemoved `btn-sm` from the two copy buttons specifically - matched on `data-role=\"copy-prompt-*\"` / `data-role=\"copy-code-*\"` rather than a blanket find-replace of the class string, since `.btn-ghost.btn-sm` is also used by 13 unrelated buttons elsewhere (icon upload trigger, the four \"Regenerate from Light\" buttons) that weren't part of this request and needed to stay untouched.\n\nVerified: confirmed exactly 130 copy buttons changed (65 Copy prompt + 65 Copy code, matching the known card count) and the other 26 `.btn-sm` buttons sitewide untouched. Measured Cascader directly - all three buttons now 34px tall, 12px text, 0px difference. Swept all 62 pages (65 copy rows) for wrapping or overflow now that the buttons are wider - 0 exceptions, including the two-button rows on narrower \"wide\" cards like Modal. Confirmed Copy prompt's click handler still copies correctly (clipboard matched the expected prompt text). Full 157-page regression sweep: 0 console errors."}, {"title": "Listing cards - matching 16px padding on all four sides of the preview panel", "body": "Asked to apply the same padding on top/bottom as left/right. The preview panel had 16px top/bottom but 0 horizontal - deliberate at the time, since Transfer's two-column demo already sized itself to exactly 100% of the available width with no slack and full padding pushed it past the card's edge. Asked specifically to match the padding rather than touch the control sizing, so changed `.button-type-demo` to `padding: 16px` on all sides and gave the two demos that needed the old zero-horizontal behavior - Transfer and Calendar - a scoped `padding-left/right: 0` override instead of leaving the gap unmatched for every other demo.\n\nThat surfaced a second, pre-existing bug while verifying: with real padding finally in place, Transfer's content still overflowed its own box by 33px, visibly truncating \"Target 2 items\" to \"Target 2 ite\". Measuring the actual minimum width needed (two 140px columns + 36px arrows + 16px gaps = 332px) against the card's real content-box width (298.65px) showed this was never a new regression - Transfer's columns have always needed slightly more room than the card's own 16px padding allows, and were silently overflowing into that padding's whitespace the whole time. It was invisible with no visible edge to reveal it, and the previous fix only checked whether `.button-type-demo`'s own box reached the card's edges, not whether its children fit inside that box. Fixed the actual root cause - reduced `.transfer-demo-column`'s `min-width` from 140px to 120px, enough slack for both columns plus the arrows to fit within the real available width.\n\nVerified: wrote a deeper sweep that checks not just whether each `.button-type-demo` box reaches its card's edges, but whether any of its children overflow that box - caught both Transfer (33px) and Calendar (3px) this way after the padding change, confirmed both at 0px overflow after their fixes, and reran it clean across all 62 pages (65 demos) afterward, 0 exceptions. Screenshotted Transfer to confirm \"Target 2 items\" now renders in full, and Tree Select, Calendar, Cascader and Modal to confirm the new matching padding looks even and correct across plain and \"wide\" card layouts. Full 157-page regression sweep: 0 console errors."}, {"title": "Listing cards - preview panel wasn't actually reaching the card's edges on ~24 component types", "body": "Reported with a screenshot of Tree Select showing visible gaps on both sides of the white preview panel added earlier. Root cause: a separate, pre-existing rule sets `align-items: center` directly on `.system-card` for ~24 component types that need their preview content centered (Tree Select, Badge, Table, Card, Avatar, Progress, Skeleton and others). With that in place, any flex child with no explicit width - which `.button-type-demo` was - shrinks to fit its own content and centers, instead of stretching to fill the card. For Tree Select that meant the box was exactly 220px wide (matching its field's own inline width) inside a 320px card, leaving 2x17px of the card's plain background exposed on either side. This was always true structurally; it only became visible once `.button-type-demo` got a background color to show it.\n\nFixed by making `.button-type-demo` stretch explicitly (`align-self: stretch; width: 100%; box-sizing: border-box`) instead of relying on the parent's default alignment, which the ~24-selector rule overrides for this specific box. This doesn't touch the `align-items: center` rule itself, since other elements in those same cards (icons, short text) still want to be centered - only this one box needs to be forced back to full width.\n\nVerified: re-measured Tree Select directly - `.button-type-demo` now matches the card's full content-box width (286px) instead of shrinking to 220px. Swept all 62 `[data-detail-href]` pages (65 demos) - every one has the white background, matches its card's expected content width exactly, and none overflow, 0 exceptions (including the gap:20px \"wide\" cards like Cascader, Modal and Transfer, which combine both overrides). Screenshotted Tree Select, Badge, Table, Card and other affected types to confirm the panel now spans edge-to-edge while their content stays correctly centered inside it. Full 157-page regression sweep: 0 console errors."}, {"title": "Listing screens - three cards per row instead of four", "body": "Asked to make every listing screen show three cards per row. `.system-card-grid` (the shared grid behind every component's listing card and, more visibly, the 17-31 card foundation pages - Borders, Radius, Shadows, Icons, Overview, Spacing, Grid & Layout) defaulted to 4 columns above 1440px, dropping to 3 only below that. Changed the default itself to 3, so it's now 3 columns from full width down to the existing 1100px breakpoint (where it was already stepping down to 2, then 1 below 700px - both untouched).\n\nLeft three existing special cases alone since they solve unrelated problems: the ~14 components (Add-on, Calendar, Table, etc.) whose single wide card already reserved 3 columns above 1440px are now redundant with the new default (same value, so no visual change) but were left as-is rather than cleaned up, out of scope for this change; Modal, Tabs and Tree's 2-column reservation for their single extra-wide card stays, since those pages only ever show one card regardless of column count. Also found and deliberately left unchanged a third, different grid (`.product-card-grid`, an auto-fill grid used only in the \"What are you building?\" product-type pickers on Radius/Spacing/Grid & Layout) - a distinct picker pattern, not the component/token listing this request was about.\n\nVerified: measured `.system-card-grid`'s actual computed column count on Borders at five viewport widths (1600/1440/1200/1000/650px) - 3/3/3/2/1, confirming the new default holds through the full desktop range and existing responsive steps are untouched. Confirmed Modal, Tabs and Tree still reserve 2 columns and Table still reserves 3, both unchanged. Screenshotted Overview, Borders, Radius, Shadows and Icons to confirm real 3-per-row layouts read correctly at desktop width. Full 157-page regression sweep: 0 console errors."}, {"title": "Listing cards - gave the preview section its own white background", "body": "Asked to change the card's preview area (the component control plus its \"System Generated X\" label) from sitting directly on the card's off-white background to a distinct white section. Added `background: #fff` and `border-radius: var(--radius-md)` to the shared `.button-type-demo` rule in `shell.css`, so it now reads as a recessed white panel against the card's `rgb(246,246,244)` background instead of blending into it.\n\nAdded vertical padding (`16px 0`) for breathing room above/below the preview content, but deliberately left horizontal padding at 0: `.system-card` already gives this section its horizontal inset via its own 16px padding, and one demo - Transfer's two-column layout - already sizes itself to exactly 100% of that available width with zero slack. Tried 16px all around first and caught the regression directly: Transfer's content overflowed the card by 32px, pushed past its rounded border on both sides. Vertical-only padding removes zero width from any demo's content, so every demo keeps exactly the layout space it had before.\n\nVerified: swept all 62 `[data-detail-href]` pages (65 demos) - every `.button-type-demo` has the white background, 0 still overflow their card, 0 exceptions (including Transfer, re-checked after the fix). Screenshotted Tree Select, Cascader, Modal, Number, Avatar, Progress, Skeleton, Empty, Table and Card to confirm the panel looks correct across both the plain and \"wide\" card layouts and doesn't clash with components that already have their own dark or bordered preview chrome (e.g. Modal's dialog mockup still reads with proper contrast). Full 157-page regression sweep: 0 console errors."}, {"title": "Listing cards - removed the CTA arrow, fixed its off-center text", "body": "Asked to remove the trailing arrow from \"View component guide\" and fix the button's alignment. Root cause of the alignment problem: a leftover `padding-top:20px` override on `.component-open-link` from the earlier IA reorder, added back when the divider still needed extra clearance - it overrode only `.btn`'s top padding, leaving the bottom at `.btn`'s normal 8px. 20px top vs 8px bottom meant the label sat visibly low in the button instead of centered.\n\nRemoved the `→` from the label string in `experience.js`. Removed the `padding-top:20px` override in `experience.css` entirely instead of just rebalancing its value - `.btn`'s own symmetric `8px 16px` padding was already correct and is now what actually applies, so the label centers naturally with no override fighting it.\n\nVerified: swept all 62 `[data-detail-href]` pages (65 links) - every link reads \"View component guide\" with no arrow, every one has matching top/bottom padding (8px/8px), 0 exceptions. Screenshotted Cascader and Modal (the wide-card variant) to confirm the label now sits centered in the button. Full 157-page regression sweep: 0 console errors."}, {"title": "Listing cards - reworded the primary button's label", "body": "Asked to change the UX writing on the primary action button. \"Configure and view guide\" packed two verbs into one label and didn't clearly say what clicking it does - the destination is a detail page built around a single component's guide, not a configuration screen. Offered a few concrete rewrites rather than guess at a subjective copy call across 62+ pages; label changed to \"View component guide\", which names the actual destination in one clear verb phrase.\n\nChanged the single string in `experience.js` where the link is injected (`shared()`, appended to every `[data-detail-href]` card) - no other file references this label as live UI copy.\n\nVerified: swept all 62 `[data-detail-href]` pages (65 links) - every link now reads \"View component guide →\", 0 exceptions. Full 157-page regression sweep: 0 console errors."}, {"title": "Listing cards - corrected information architecture, primary action first", "body": "Asked to fix the card's information architecture. The problem: \"Configure and view guide\" is the card's actual primary action - the whole card already navigates there on click - yet it was rendered last, below Copy prompt/Copy code, styled as a plain text link with no visual weight. Two secondary utility actions were reading as more important than the real destination. Asked which reordering was wanted rather than guess at a subjective hierarchy call across 62+ pages; confirmed direction: primary action first, made visually stronger.\n\nReordered to Preview → label → divider → \"Configure and view guide\" (now a real `.btn.btn-primary`, full-width, matching the same primary-button treatment used elsewhere in the system rather than a one-off style) → Copy prompt/Copy code below it as secondary actions. Moved the divider back to sit above the link (it separates the demo/label section from the actions below, and the link is first among actions again). Removed the link's now-redundant one-off font/color/padding, superseded by the `.btn.btn-primary` classes, and removed a stale hover rule that would otherwise have overridden the button's real hover state with a leftover text-link tint.\n\nVerified: swept all 62 `[data-detail-href]` pages (65 cards) - every card has the link before `.card-copy-row` in DOM order, every link carries the real `.btn.btn-primary` classes, every link has the divider border and spans the card's full content width, every `.card-copy-row` no longer has one, 0 exceptions. Screenshotted Cascader, Number, Add-on, Tabs and Modal (the wide-card variant) to confirm the new hierarchy reads correctly. Clicked Copy prompt (still copies) and the Configure link (still navigates to the correct detail page) to confirm nothing broke functionally. Full 157-page regression sweep: 0 console errors."}, {"title": "Listing cards - moved the divider above Copy prompt/Copy code", "body": "Asked to move the separator line so it sits above Copy prompt/Copy code instead of above \"Configure and view guide\". Moved the `border-top` (plus its `padding-top:12px`) from `.component-open-link` to `.card-copy-row`, so the divider now separates the demo/label section above from the two copy buttons below, and \"Configure and view guide\" sits underneath with no divider of its own.\n\nVerified: screenshotted Cascader, Number and Add-on to confirm the divider now reads above the buttons on every card size; swept all 62 `[data-detail-href]` pages - every `.card-copy-row` has the border, every `.component-open-link` no longer does, 0 exceptions; clicked Copy code (still copies) and the Configure link (still navigates to the detail page) to confirm nothing broke structurally. Full 157-page regression sweep: 0 console errors."}, {"title": "Listing cards - two real double-spacing bugs, found via direct measurement not assumption", "body": "Reported with a screenshot of Cascader's card showing clearly oversized gaps in multiple places. Measured every child element's exact bounding box and computed margin/gap rather than guessing which token looked \"off\": found two separate instances of the same anti-pattern - a flex `gap` added to a container at some point, without zeroing an individual `margin-top` that already existed on a child from before the gap was introduced, so the two stack.\n\n**Bug 1**: `.button-type-demo` for ~55 \"needs more room for its preview\" components (Cascader, Tabs, Modal, Table, Card, Autocomplete, Date Picker, and ~48 others) is `display:flex` with `gap:20px` - but its `.dualicon-card-label` child (\"System Generated X\") also carries its own `margin-top:12px`, stacking to a real 32px gap where 20px (the value the gap was actually tuned for) was intended.\n\n**Bug 2**: every listing card's injected \"Configure and view guide\" link (`.component-open-link`, appended by `experience.js` to any `[data-detail-href]` card) carries `margin-top:16px`, but its parent `.system-card` already has its own `gap:8px` applying between every child uniformly - stacking to 24px where 16px was intended. This one is sitewide, not limited to the 55-component \"wide\" set.\n\nFixed both by removing the redundant margin rather than fighting the gap: zeroed `.dualicon-card-label`'s margin-top specifically within the 55 wide-card selectors (left completely untouched for every other card, where `.button-type-demo` is plain block flow and that margin is the correct, sole spacing mechanism); reduced `.component-open-link`'s margin-top from 16px to 8px sitewide, so `gap:8px + margin:8px` restores the original 16px total instead of compounding past it.\n\nVerified: measured both gaps directly on Cascader (32px → 20px, 24px → 16px), screenshotted the card to confirm it now reads as a tight, correctly-rhythmed card; clicked Copy prompt (still copies) and the Configure link (still navigates to cascader-default.html) to confirm nothing was broken structurally. Swept both bugs across every page that could possibly have them - 62 pages for the label gap, 62 for the link gap - 0 remaining issues on any page. Full 157-page regression sweep: 0 console errors."}, {"title": "Copy prompt / Copy code CTAs - centered within the card", "body": "Follow-up to the fixed-width fix: once the two buttons stopped stretching to fill `.card-copy-row` (still `width:100%` of its own card via the 55+ `*-type-card` rules), they sat left-aligned by flexbox's own default, leaving unused space to the right on any card wider than the buttons' own ~228px - asked to center them instead.\n\nAdded `justify-content: center` to `.card-copy-row`. The row itself is still `width:100%` of its card (untouched, and harmless now that it's just an invisible flex container), so centering its own two non-growing children centers them relative to the card.\n\nVerified: measured each card's own horizontal center against the button row's center on Add-on, Number, Tabs, Modal and Card - 0.0px difference on every one; screenshotted Add-on and Tabs to confirm visually; clicked Copy code on Add-on to confirm it still copies. Swept card/row centering across all 64 pages: 0 mismatches. Full 157-page regression sweep: 0 console errors."}, {"title": "Copy prompt / Copy code CTAs - fixed the actual root cause, not just the worst symptom", "body": "Reported a third time with a screenshot: Add-on's CTAs (a \"normal\" card, not one of the earlier Modal/Tabs/Tree outliers) still looked oversized. The previous fix only bounded `.card-copy-row`'s own max-width to 320px - it never addressed why the buttons were stretching in the first place: `.card-copy-row .btn{ flex:1 }` grows each button to fill whatever width the row resolves to, and the row's width is set to `100%` of its own card by 55+ `*-type-card` rules. Capping the row just capped the worst case (Modal/Tabs/Tree's 477px); every other card was still stretching its buttons out to fill its own particular width - 286px or 299px, well past what \"Copy prompt\"/\"Copy code\" actually need (measured unstretched: 103.5px / 89.4px).\n\nRemoved the stretch entirely instead of continuing to bound it: `flex: 0 0 auto; min-width: 110px` on both buttons - sized to (a hair over) the longer label's natural width, so both land on the exact same fixed width regardless of the row's own resolved size. The row itself is left at its existing width:100% (now visually inert - empty flex-container space to the right of two non-growing buttons has no background or border, so it renders as nothing).\n\nVerified: measured buttons on narrow (Number), medium (Add-on/Card/Table) and the previously-oversized wide (Tabs/Modal/Tree) pages directly - every single one now renders at exactly 110px, not just \"closer than before\"; screenshotted Add-on, Tabs and Number side by side to confirm all three now look like the same small, appropriately-sized action row; clicked Copy prompt on Add-on to confirm it still copies correctly. Swept the actual button width (not the row's, which was the wrong thing to measure once it stopped mattering) across all 64 pages and 150 button instances: 100% land on exactly 110px, zero exceptions. Full 157-page regression sweep: 0 console errors."}, {"title": "Copy prompt / Copy code CTAs - a real cross-page size inconsistency, not the earlier within-row one", "body": "Reported again with screenshots: Tabs' CTAs look dramatically wider than Number's. This was a genuinely different bug from the earlier \"Copy prompt wider than Copy code on the same card\" fix - within each screenshot the two buttons were already equal width; the actual problem was the whole row's absolute size varying page to page.\n\nMeasured before touching anything: every `*-type-card .card-copy-row` rule sets `width:100%` of its own card, and while most listing cards share a 4-column (~286px) or 3-column (~299px) grid, Modal/Tabs/Tree deliberately use a wider 2-column grid (documented in shell.css: \"single example cards need more room than the 3-column components above\") so their preview content isn't a narrow orphan - but that same extra card width was also stretching their Copy prompt/Copy code row to 477px, a real ~65% jump for what's meant to be the same small utility action everywhere.\n\nFixed with a `max-width: 320px` on the base `.card-copy-row` rule - bounds Modal/Tabs/Tree's outlier row back down to line up with the rest of the site, while every narrower card (already resolving under 320px at `width:100%`) is untouched.\n\nVerified 3 ways before considering it done: measured the row and both buttons on a narrow (Number), medium (Card/Table) and both previously-oversized wide (Tabs/Modal/Tree) page - all three outliers now cap at exactly 320px, everything else unchanged, buttons still equal within each row; screenshotted Tabs' card to visually confirm it now reads as a normal-sized action row instead of a stretched one; clicked Copy prompt on Tabs to confirm it still copies correctly. Then swept the width of every `.card-copy-row` across all 64 pages: the whole site now falls into exactly 3 tight buckets (286px / 299px / 320px, a 34px spread) with zero rows over 320px, down from a 245-477px spread (232px) beforehand. Full 157-page regression sweep: 0 console errors."}, {"title": "Copy prompt / Copy code CTAs - unequal widths on 2 of 64 pages", "body": "Reported: Copy prompt and Copy code buttons aren't equal width, inconsistent across listing pages. Checked all 64 pages that have this button pair before touching anything: 62 already wrap them in `.card-copy-row` (`display:flex` + `.btn{flex:1}`, giving equal width by construction regardless of label length) - measured and confirmed genuinely equal there. Only `patterns.html` and `templates.html` (5 button pairs each, 10 total) used a one-off inline `style=\"display:flex;gap:8px;margin-top:12px;\"` instead of the class, so they never got the `flex:1` rule - measured the real gap directly: \"Copy prompt\" rendered at 103.5px, \"Copy code\" at 89.4px.\n\nFixed by replacing the inline style with `class=\"card-copy-row\"` on all 10 instances, matching the other 62 pages exactly rather than inventing a new rule - both pages verified their tag balance stayed correct (`section`/`div` counts) after the scripted find-replace.\n\nFollowed the requested process per page: fixed patterns.html, then verified 3 ways (measured both buttons now render at an identical 439px, screenshotted a row to confirm visually, clicked Copy prompt and confirmed the clipboard still populates correctly - the class swap doesn't touch the `data-role` attributes the JS wiring keys off) before moving to templates.html and repeating all three checks there. Finished with a sitewide sweep across all 64 pages (not just the 2 fixed): 0 width mismatches, 0 Copy prompt/Copy code buttons found outside `.card-copy-row` anywhere. Full 157-page regression sweep: 0 console errors."}, {"title": "Dropdown - built the real interactivity its own docs already claimed", "body": "Asked to check whether Dropdown is properly implemented. Checked empirically, not by reading the code: loaded the real page in a browser, clicked the Live Preview's trigger, and read the live DOM. Result: the trigger had no `aria-haspopup`, no `aria-expanded`, and clicking it did nothing at all - yet Dropdown's own Accessibility section explicitly claims \"the trigger exposes aria-haspopup and aria-expanded, reflecting whether the menu is currently open,\" \"the opened menu is fully keyboard-navigable,\" and \"focus returns to the trigger when the menu closes.\" All three were false. Also confirmed Dropdown's demo classes aren't reused anywhere else sitewide - this is isolated to its own 2 pages, not a spread-out problem.\n\nRoot cause: the \"Open\" state shown in the Live Preview is just one more static button with an `.is-open` CSS class applied for comparison, part of the same Rest/Hover/Open/Focused/Disabled side-by-side matrix every component on this site uses - correct and consistent for showing what each state *looks like*, but incapable of proving a genuinely dynamic behavior (open/close, keyboard nav, focus management) actually works, because nothing there is wired to real events.\n\nRather than touch that established matrix pattern, added a new \"Interactive Example\" section: one real, working Dropdown, built as a proper WAI-ARIA menu button (`role=\"menu\"`/`\"menuitem\"`, real DOM focus moving between items via a roving tabindex - not `aria-activedescendant`, which is the listbox/combobox pattern Select uses instead, since Dropdown's items are actions to invoke, not a value to select). Reused the exact open/close/Escape/click-outside shape the page's own \"Copy Code as...\" utility dropdown already implements correctly, adapted for real menu semantics. Follows the same Size/Corner radius/Label property controls the static matrix above already offers, so it isn't a disconnected add-on.\n\nVerified via Playwright, checking every claim in the docs directly against live DOM state rather than assuming the new code was correct: `aria-haspopup=\"true\"`/`aria-expanded` toggles correctly; opening moves focus to the first item; ArrowDown/ArrowUp move focus between items; Escape closes the menu and returns focus to the trigger; a click outside closes it; arrowing to \"Delete\" and pressing Enter selects it, shows \"Selected: Delete\" in a live region, closes the menu, and returns focus to the trigger. Also confirmed the Label property control updates the interactive trigger's own text live. Full 157-page sweep: 0 console errors."}, {"title": "Component detail pages' own Corner radius default now tracks the sitewide Radius token", "body": "Follow-up to the sitewide Radius propagation work: asked to fix the caveat identified there - Button's (and, once checked properly, many other components') Live Preview has its own independent \"Corner radius\" property control with a hardcoded `FALLBACK_DEFAULTS.radius: \"8\"`, unrelated to `var(--radius-md)`.\n\nScoped it first rather than assuming Button was the only one: grepped every `*-detail.js` file for a `radius` field inside `FALLBACK_DEFAULTS` - 33 files, all but one (`checkbox-detail.js`, which uses its own smaller 2/4/6/Full option set) sharing the exact same 5-option scale (0/4/8/12/Full). Rather than a single shared helper (this codebase has no module system - every page-specific file is self-contained by convention), added the same small `liveRadiusDefault(fallback)` function to each of the 33 files: reads the live computed `--radius-md`, then snaps to whichever of *that file's own* `RADIUS_OPTIONS` is numerically closest - so a philosophy step a component doesn't offer as an exact preset (e.g. Compact's 6px, when a component only offers 0/4/8/12/Full) still lands on a sensible neighbor instead of ignoring the sitewide setting. `FALLBACK_DEFAULTS`'s `radius: \"8\"` (or `\"4\"` for the two that used that) became `radius: liveRadiusDefault(\"8\")` - the original literal kept as the fallback argument if the CSS variable is ever unavailable.\n\nWhile verifying, found that 13 of the 33 files' Live Preview matrix code has no matching mount element in their current HTML at all (`add-on`, `checkbox`, `drawer`, `input`, `menu`, `pagination`, `search`, `tabs`, `time`, `tree-select`, `card`, `collapse`, `table`) - the render function's own early-return (`if (!matrixContainer) return;`) means `liveRadiusDefault()` is computed but has nothing live to feed on that specific page. Pre-existing, unrelated to this fix - didn't attempt to rebuild 13 pages' matrix wiring under a \"fix the radius default\" task.\n\nVerified via Playwright on the 20 pages that do have a live mount (Button, Modal, Tag, Dropdown checked directly): default Corner radius reads 8px at Balanced (unchanged baseline) and correctly updates to 16px on Modal / 12px on Tag and Dropdown (snapped from Expressive's 16px, since those two don't offer 16 as an exact preset) the moment Radius philosophy is saved as Expressive - confirmed via computed style inside each page's own real matrix-container mount, not assumption. `node --check` passes on all 33 files. Full 157-page sweep: 0 console errors."}, {"title": "Verified whether saved Foundations actually reach real components - found only Colors did", "body": "Asked to verify that every Foundation category is actually applied across the real component pages, not just documented. Checked this empirically rather than by inspection: saved a loud test value on each Foundation page, then loaded real component pages and read their *computed* CSS. Result: only Colors propagated - `theme-init.js`/`shell.js` explicitly re-read the 5 color-related `localStorage` keys on every page load. Grid & Layout, Typography, Spacing, Radius, Borders, Shadows and Icons all wrote to their own `localStorage` key just fine, but nothing anywhere else in the codebase ever read those 7 keys back - a saved choice never left that one page.\n\nInvestigated each of the other 7 for whether they even *could* propagate before building anything, by grepping how widely their underlying CSS custom properties are actually consumed in `shell.css`:\n- **Radius** - `var(--radius-md)` alone is referenced 100+ times across real component classes. Broadly and consistently wired - safe to make live.\n- **Typography font family** - every single non-monospace `font-family:` declaration in `shell.css` (153+92 checked) already routes through `var(--font-display)`/`var(--font-body)` - 100% coverage, zero hardcoded stacks. Safe to make live.\n- **Shadows** - only Modal and Card actually reference `var(--shadow-*)`; ~40 other `box-shadow` declarations sitewide (Popover, Toast, Autocomplete, Cascader, TreeSelect, Tour, Mention...) are hardcoded raw values. Overriding the shadow tokens would visibly change 2 components and silently skip the rest - misleading, not done.\n- **Spacing** - `var(--space-N)` appears only ~17 times across the whole stylesheet; the vast majority of padding/gap values are raw pixel literals. Same shallow-adoption problem as Shadows.\n- **Borders (\"Configure your border\")** - no shared \"default border\" token exists at all; real components each reference `--line`/`--line-strong` contextually, not one common value this page's picker could override.\n- **Icons** - Line/Flat/Duotone/Isometric/3D are structurally different SVG markup, not a CSS-swappable property; picking a new style can't propagate without regenerating every icon instance sitewide.\n- **Grid & Layout** - \"product type\"/\"grid type\" are structural per-page layout decisions with no corresponding CSS custom property anywhere to override.\n\nBuilt sitewide propagation for the 2 categories that were actually ready (Radius, Typography font family), using the exact same architecture Colors already has: `theme-init.js` (the `<head>` boot script, shared by all 157 pages) reads `ads:radius`/`ads:typography` and overrides the relevant `:root` custom properties before paint; `shell.js` adds `applySavedRadius()`/`applySavedTypographyFont()` for live re-application without a reload plus a `storage`-event listener so an already-open other tab picks up a change too, mirroring `applySavedColorsForTheme()`'s exact pattern; `radius.js`/`typography.js`'s own persist and reset paths now call these directly so the current page reflects a change immediately, not just on next load.\n\nRadius needed a real design decision, not just a mechanical wire-up: the 7 non-fixed radius tokens (`--radius-2/sm/6/md/12/16/24`) needed a value per philosophy (Sharp/Compact/Balanced/Soft/Expressive). A first attempt mapped each philosophy's own documented scale onto these 7 slots by list position, which happened to put `--radius-md` (the single most-used slot, 102 sites) at 8px under both Balanced and Sharp - buttons never visibly changed. Caught via a live Playwright check (computed style before/after, not just reading the code), not by inspection. Replaced with an explicit per-philosophy table, still using only values each philosophy already documents, chosen so `--radius-md` reads distinctly at every step (4/6/8/12/16 across Sharp through Expressive).\n\nOne caveat, found and confirmed rather than assumed away: several component detail pages (Button confirmed, likely more) have their own independent \"Corner radius\" property-selector in their Live Preview matrix, which generates its own hardcoded CSS class (`.btn--radius-8{border-radius:8px}`) instead of reading `var(--radius-md)` - a separate, legitimate feature (letting someone preview one button at a specific radius) that happens to share the word \"radius\" with the Foundation-level philosophy picker. The sitewide override still correctly reaches everything that isn't behind one of these property controls (confirmed: sidebar search, nav links, static Do/Don't examples, settings icon, and every page's own chrome, all update correctly) - only each component's own interactive property-controlled preview stays on its own independent default, which is the same \"component-tier tokens only cover 4 of 63 components so far\" gap already tracked below, not a new one.\n\nVerified via Playwright across multiple real component pages (table.html, modal.html, input.html, card.html): Radius \"Expressive\" correctly changes the sidebar search's border-radius from 8px to 16px consistently on all of them; a custom Secondary font (JetBrains Mono) correctly changes nav-link font-family on table.html and card.html while leaving the Primary-font-driven H1 untouched; Reset on both correctly restores every page back to its true CSS defaults; cross-tab sync confirmed (a change saved in one open tab reaches another already-open tab live). Full 157-page sweep: 0 console errors."}, {"title": "Guidelines pages - the same Markdown export, adapted for pages with no editable state", "body": "Asked to do the same for Design Principles, Component Guidelines, Copywriting, Data Format and Accessibility. Checked first: none of these 5 have a dedicated JS file or any localStorage-backed state at all - they're pure reference/documentation pages, unlike every Foundations page so far. \"Export current editable values\" doesn't apply here; there's nothing to select or save.\n\nAdapted the pattern instead of forcing it: added a generic `initPageMarkdownExport()` to experience.js (only activates on a page that actually has the new button+output element) that exports the page's own real content as Markdown - mirroring the exact heading/paragraph/list-item conversion `tools/audit_pages.py` already uses to build `docs-catalog.js` (the source the sitewide MD Export page's per-page export already draws from), just run client-side against the live rendered DOM instead of raw HTML source. This gives each page its own local Copy Markdown button without loading the 500KB+ sitewide catalog file just to read one page's own entry, and without duplicating a second, differently-behaved extraction implementation.\n\nOne correctness detail worth calling out: an extractor that only reads text inside `<h1>`-`<h3>`/`<li>`/`<p>` would silently drop most of these pages' actual content, since Do/Don't examples, usage-row tables and similar structures hold their text in bare `<span>`s, not `<p>`. Matched audit_pages.py's own approach instead: walk every text node in document order and use tag boundaries only to insert structural breaks (heading markers, newlines, bullet prefixes), so nothing between two headings is skipped. The injected outline nav and setup-notice banner, plus the export panel's own placeholder text, are explicitly excluded so they don't pollute the output.\n\nVerified via Playwright on all 5 pages: each renders substantial, correctly-structured Markdown (2-6KB) with real headings and body text, Copy places the exact same text on the clipboard, and the export panel confirmed absent from its own output. Full 157-page sweep: 0 console errors."}, {"title": "Icons - same Markdown export added, after reading what this page actually saves", "body": "Asked to do the same MD-file fix for Icons, the last of the 8 Foundations pages. Read the page and icons.js first: Icon style (system-card), Configure your icon system (size/stroke/corner selects) and Bring your own icons (library radio pick + session-only uploaded reference icons) are the editable parts; Icon states is fixed reference content, excluded for the same reason as every other page's static sections. One wrinkle specific to this page: uploaded file names were never part of the persisted `ads:icons` payload (only the library choice is) - included them in the Markdown anyway, same as the existing Machine View JSON already does, since they're real current-session state even though they don't survive a reload.\n\nAdded an \"Icon tokens - Markdown export\" section covering all 3 editable parts, using each select's own descriptive option text (\"24px - standard default,\" \"1.8px - medium\") rather than raw values. Regenerated inside the same `updateMachineViews()` already called after every card click, select change, library pick and upload.\n\nVerified via Playwright: updates on an icon style pick, updates again on a stroke-width change, correctly reflects a library pick, and Copy places the exact current Markdown on the clipboard. Full 157-page sweep: 0 console errors. Shadows is the one Foundations page still without this - not done here, since it wasn't asked for."}, {"title": "Borders - same Markdown export added, after reading what this page actually saves", "body": "Asked to do the same MD-file fix for Borders. Read the page and borders.js first: 3 of its 4 sections are real editable state, not 1 or 2 like the previous pages - Border presets (a system-card picker), Configure your border (width/color/opacity/style/sides live config) and Border states (10 per-state light/dark color rows) all persist to localStorage; only Component guidance (Card and Table's fixed values) is static reference, excluded for the same reason as every other page's static sections so far.\n\nAdded a \"Border tokens - Markdown export\" section covering all 3 editable parts, called from all three of this page's existing Machine View update functions (`updatePresetsMachineView`, `updateConfigMachineView`, `updateStatesMachineView`) so it stays current regardless of which one last ran. One real bug caught before it shipped: `renderMarkdown()` reads `stateRows`/`stateDarkAuto`, but those aren't assigned until later in the same script, and `loadBorder()`'s own call chain reaches `updatePresetsMachineView()` (and therefore `renderMarkdown()`) before that point - would have thrown \"Cannot read properties of undefined\" on every page load. Fixed with a truthiness guard around the Border states section specifically.\n\nVerified via Playwright: picking a preset updates both Border presets and Configure your border sections, changing Sides updates just that field, editing a state's hex updates just that row, and Copy places the exact current Markdown on the clipboard. Full 157-page sweep: 0 console errors."}, {"title": "Radius - same Markdown export added, after reading what this page actually saves", "body": "Asked to do the same MD-file fix for Radius. Read the page and radius.js first: same product/system-card pattern as Grid & Layout and Spacing - \"What are you building?\" and \"Radius philosophy\" are the two real editable choices; \"Recommended global model,\" \"Radius by component role\" and \"Component defaults\" are fixed reference content (the three sections split out of one combined `<section>` during the earlier Human/Machine View pass), excluded for the same reason as every other page's static sections so far.\n\nAdded a \"Radius tokens - Markdown export\" section: selected product (plus its recommended philosophy/core radius/container radius), and the selected radius philosophy (plus its tagline, description and full scale) - regenerated inside the same `updateMachineViews()` already called after every card click, load and reset. Copy button matches the other four pages.\n\nVerified via Playwright: correct placeholder before any selection, updates on product pick, updates again after manually overriding the radius philosophy independent of the product's recommendation, and Copy places the exact current Markdown on the clipboard. Full 157-page sweep: 0 console errors."}, {"title": "Spacing - same Markdown export added, after reading what this page actually saves", "body": "Asked to do the same MD-file fix for Spacing. Read the page and spacing.js first: structurally identical to Grid & Layout's own product/system-card pattern - \"What are you building?\" and \"Spacing system\" are the two real editable choices; \"Recommended global model\" and \"Component defaults\" are fixed reference content (stat tiles and usage rows, already auto-populated as static Machine View JSON), left out for the same reason as every other page's static sections so far.\n\nAdded a \"Spacing tokens - Markdown export\" section: selected product (plus its recommended primary/secondary unit), and the selected spacing system (plus its tagline, description and full scale) - regenerated inside the same `updateMachineViews()` already called after every card click, load and reset. Copy button matches the other three pages.\n\nVerified via Playwright: correct placeholder before any selection, updates on product pick, updates again after manually overriding the spacing system independent of the product's recommendation, and Copy places the exact current Markdown on the clipboard. Full 157-page sweep: 0 console errors."}, {"title": "Typography - same Markdown export added, after reading what this page actually saves", "body": "Asked to do the same MD-file fix for Typography. Read the page and typography.js fully first: Font families (Primary/Secondary pickers) and Type scale (16 rows - H1-H6, Body, Paragraph, Caption, Active, Selected, Disabled, Error, Warning, Success, Info, each with family/size/line-height/weight/italic/color/dark color) are the real editable state; Additional semantic levels is fixed reference text with its own static Machine View JSON already, same category as Colors' \"Light & dark theme behavior\" and Grid & Layout's 3 static sections - left out for the same reason.\n\nAdded a \"Typography tokens - Markdown export\" section: Font families, the active platform preset (if any), then every Type scale row with its real option-list labels (e.g. \"Extrabold\", \"Display LG - 48/60px\") pulled straight from each row's own `<select>` rather than the raw numeric/token values Machine View's JSON uses - more useful to read as prose. Regenerated inside the same `updateMachineViews()` already wired to every input/change/click on the page, with a Copy button matching Colors and Grid & Layout.\n\nVerified via Playwright: updates immediately when a row's weight changes, correctly adds a \"Platform preset\" section once one is picked, and Copy places the exact current Markdown on the clipboard. Full 157-page sweep: 0 console errors."}, {"title": "Grid & Layout - same Markdown export added, after reading what this page actually saves", "body": "Asked to do the same MD-file fix for Grid & Layout. Read the page fully first rather than assuming it mirrors Colors: only 2 of its 5 sections are actually editable state (What are you building? / Grid type) - Breakpoints, Layout patterns and How this page fits together are fixed reference content with their own static Machine View JSON already, not user selections that need synchronizing anywhere. So the Markdown here covers only the 2 real choices, same as how Colors' export didn't include its own static \"Light & dark theme behavior\" section either.\n\nAdded a \"Grid & Layout tokens - Markdown export\" section: selected product type (plus its recommended grid type/columns/gutter) and the currently-selected grid type (plus its description) - regenerated inside the same `updateMachineViews()` already called after every card click, load and reset, with a Copy button matching Colors'.\n\nVerified via Playwright: renders correctly before any selection (\"No product type selected yet\"), updates after picking a product card, updates again after manually overriding Grid type independent of the product's recommendation, and Copy places the exact current Markdown on the clipboard. Full 157-page sweep: 0 console errors."}, {"title": "Colors - added the missing MD file, not just a JSON Machine View", "body": "Checked against a pasted product spec for the Colors detail page. Every described interaction already existed (Light/Dark per section, auto-derived Dark with manual override, Brand's derived scale, per-section Save) except one real gap the spec called out explicitly: \"there is no MD file available in the final output that users can copy.\" True - the Machine View toggle added earlier gives each section its own JSON, but nothing on the page produces an actual Markdown block, and nothing consolidates all 5 sections into one document.\n\nAdded a new \"Color tokens - Markdown export\" section at the bottom of the Colors tab: one Markdown document covering Brand, Background & surface elevation, Semantic & status, Neutral scale and Text colors (Light values, plus Dark values for whichever sections have their dark override enabled), with a Copy button. Reused the exact same field-reading logic the Machine View JSON already had (`collectTheme()` in `initColorMachineViews()`) rather than writing a second parallel reader, and hooked the Markdown render into the same sitewide input/change listener that JSON already uses - so it updates on every edit, not only after a Save click (the spec asked for \"on save,\" this is a superset of that).\n\nVerified via Playwright: the Markdown renders correctly on load, updates immediately when Brand's hex is edited, correctly reflects a newly-enabled Dark override on Status colors, and the Copy button places the exact current Markdown on the clipboard with a \"Copied to clipboard\" confirmation. Full 157-page sweep: 0 console errors."}, {"title": "All 8 Foundations pages now auto-save, so MD Export always reflects current values", "body": "Follow-up to the MD Export fix above: even with Grid & Layout and Icons added to `TOKEN_KEYS`, the export's \"Saved foundation values\" JSON stayed empty (`{}`) for anyone who had edited a Foundations page but never clicked that page's own Save button - by design, `savedTokens()` only reads what was explicitly saved. Asked to change that: the export should reflect what's actually on screen, not require a separate \"click Save on all 8 pages\" step.\n\nAdded a `persistX()` function to each of the 8 pages (foundations.js's 5 sections, plus grid-layout/typography/spacing/radius/borders/shadows/icons.js) that writes the exact same payload shape the Save button already wrote, called on every real edit - a card pick, a select change, a hex input, a checkbox - not only on Save's click. The explicit Save/Reset buttons are unchanged and still work exactly as before; auto-save just means there's no longer a gap between what you see and what's stored.\n\nThe one real subtlety: persisting had to be wired to genuine user edits specifically, not to the shared render/select functions those edits call - those same functions also run during page load (restoring a previous save) and Reset (restoring defaults), and persisting there would immediately overwrite Reset's own `localStorage.removeItem()` with the just-reset default values. Typography's Reset button surfaced a sharper version of this: its font-picker reset internally fires a *synthetic* click (`defaultBtn.click()`) that bubbles through the same page-wide listener used for auto-persist, mid-reset, before the row-level fields were back to their defaults - caught via a direct before/after localStorage read in Playwright, not just eyeballing the UI. Fixed by pairing `e.stopPropagation()` (blocks Reset's own click from bubbling) with a `suppressPersist` flag (blocks the nested synthetic click too) for the duration of Reset's handler.\n\nVerified via Playwright: edited one field on each of the 8 pages with no Save click anywhere, then opened MD Export fresh - all 8 categories present with the exact edited values. Separately confirmed Reset still leaves `localStorage` fully empty (not just at documented defaults) on grid-layout, typography and borders. Full 157-page sweep: 0 console errors."}, {"title": "MD Export - Grid & Layout and Icons never made it into the exported Markdown", "body": "Reported: the exported Markdown's \"Saved foundation values\" block was missing Grid & Layout and Icons entirely, no matter what. Root cause: `experience.js`'s `TOKEN_KEYS` list - the fixed set of localStorage keys the export reads back - was `['colors','bg-colors','status-colors','neutral-colors','text-colors','typography','spacing','radius','border','border-states','shadow']`. Grid & Layout and Icons save to `ads:grid-layout` and `ads:icons` respectively (confirmed in grid-layout.js/icons.js), but neither key was ever in that list, so their saved data was silently dropped even after clicking each page's own Save button - not a display bug, the values genuinely never reached the export.\n\nAdded `'grid-layout'` and `'icons'` to `TOKEN_KEYS`. Verified by scripting a save on all 8 Foundations pages' Save buttons in one Playwright session, then checking the exported JSON block: all 8 now appear (previously 6 of 8). Full 157-page sweep: 0 console errors.\n\nWorth noting for the other 6 (Colors, Typography, Spacing, Radius, Borders, Shadows): these were already correct, but only appear in the export once that page's own Save button has actually been clicked - unsaved edits fall back to \"documented defaults,\" by the export's own existing disclaimer. That part of the behavior is unchanged."}, {"title": "Human View / Machine View extended to all remaining Foundations pages", "body": "Following straight on from Colors, asked to add the same toggle to every section across Grid & Layout, Typography, Spacing, Radius, Borders, Shadows and Icons - 28 sections total, none of which had it. Two of those pages (Radius, Shadows) had 3 differently-titled sub-sections sharing one physical `<section>`; split each into its own real `<section>` so every title gets its own toggle, rather than one toggle covering three unrelated titles.\n\nAdded a third, generic way of populating Machine View to shell.js/experience.js's existing two (a component page's static usage doc, and Colors' page-specific live-JSON functions): `autoMachineViews()` in experience.js scans every `.component-guide` section for a `.usage-row` or `.stat-tile` reference table in its Human View and derives the JSON straight from that markup - zero per-page JS needed. It only touches a `<code>` block that has no `data-role` and is still the `{}` placeholder, so it never overwrites a page-specific live block. This covered about half the 28 sections outright (Additional semantic levels, Recommended global model, Radius by component role, Component guidance, Elevation hierarchy, Icon states, and more).\n\nThe other half - product/system card pickers (What are you building?, Grid type, Radius philosophy, Spacing system, Shadow philosophy, Border presets, Icon style), live config controls (Configure your border, Configure your icon system), and two genuinely complex cases (Typography's 16-row Type Scale table, Borders' 10-row state-color table) - got small page-specific `updateMachineViews()` functions added to their own `.js` file, following the exact pattern already established for Colors and Grid & Layout: read current DOM/dataset state, call on every relevant click/input/change, and once on initial load.\n\nVerified via Playwright across all 7 pages: all 28 toggles switch panels, all 28 Machine View blocks parse as valid non-empty JSON, and spot-checked live updates (clicking a product card, editing a border state's hex, changing a type-scale size, picking an icon library) all reflect immediately in their JSON. Full 157-page sweep: 0 console errors."}, {"title": "Borders - Border states swatches had a border drawn around the color box itself", "body": "Flagged from a screenshot of Border states (Default/Hover/Focus/etc.): each Light/Dark hex swatch had its own visible border ring drawn around the color box, inside the field's normal pill container. Root cause: `applyStateSwatch()` in borders.js set `swatch.style.border` to that row's own width/color/opacity spec, as a literal \"here's what this border looks like\" demo - clever in intent, but read as an unwanted stray outline in practice, especially since `.type-color-swatch` is styled `border: none` everywhere else on the site.\n\nRemoved the inline border - both the 20 `style=\"border:...\"` attributes baked into borders.html and the `swatch.style.border = ...` line in borders.js that was re-applying the same thing on every load/edit (so removing just the HTML wouldn't have stuck). Verified via computed style: all 20 swatches now resolve to `border: none` before and after editing a hex value. Full 157-page sweep: 0 console errors."}, {"title": "Colors - Human View / Machine View added to all 6 sections", "body": "Asked to bring the Human View / Machine View pattern - already used across all 70 component detail pages - to Colors' 6 sections (Brand color, Background & surface elevation, Semantic & status colors, Neutral scale, Text colors, Light & dark theme behavior). None of them had it; Colors was the one Foundations tab still missing the toggle entirely.\n\nReused the real markup exactly (`.component-guide`, `.panel-title-row` + `.guide-view-toggle`, `.guide-toggle-btn[data-view]`, `.guide-view-panel[data-view-panel]`) rather than a look-alike, so the already-generic `initGuideViewToggle()` in shell.js wires up all 6 sections with zero JS changes needed for the toggle itself.\n\nThe 5 editable sections aren't like a component's fixed usage docs, though - their whole content is live, user-editable hex values - so a static Machine View JSON would go stale the moment someone typed a new hex. Wrote `initColorMachineViews()` in foundations.js instead: recomputes each section's JSON from its actual current field values on the same delegated `input`/`change` listener pattern `initContrastBadges()` already uses, so editing a color updates that section's Machine View live, not just on save. Light & dark theme behavior has no editable fields at all (pure explanation of the light-is-source-of-truth rule), so it gets a static JSON describing that rule instead.\n\nVerified via Playwright: all 6 toggles switch panels correctly, all 5 live JSON blocks parse as valid JSON and reflect real field values, and editing Brand's light hex updates its Machine View JSON immediately even while that panel stays hidden. Full 157-page sweep: 0 console errors."}, {"title": "\"On this page\" outline - strip numbering, force single scrollable row", "body": "Two follow-ups on the Group Button restyle, from a screenshot of principles.html (whose 10 headings are themselves numbered, \"1. Proximity\" through \"10. React Immediately\"): the numbers doubled up oddly once the same text became a short nav-strip label, and wrapping to a second row (the previous behavior once a page had enough sections to overflow one line) didn't read as one cohesive segmented control anymore.\n\nStrips a leading \"N. \" ordinal from the nav label only - the heading itself is untouched, this only affects the short outline copy. Changed the outline from wrap-to-second-row to a single row with horizontal scroll (the same behavior a narrow viewport already had, now applied at every width) - segments no longer shrink or wrap their own text, verified via scrollWidth > clientWidth on a 10-segment page and confirmed scrolling reveals the cut-off segment.\n\nFull 157-page sweep: 0 console errors."}, {"title": "\"On this page\" outline - corrected against the real component, not just its source", "body": "Asked to check the actual rendered Group Button detail page and verify the previous restyle against it directly, rather than trust the earlier CSS read. Did a computed-style diff between the real component (group-button-default.html's live preview) and the outline: container background, gap, segment color/font, and selected-state red/white all matched exactly - but segment `border-radius` didn't. The real component uses `border-radius: inherit` on each segment (so every segment's corners match whatever radius the container is set to), not a fixed value; the earlier pass had hardcoded `var(--radius-sm)` (4px) instead, a real, visible mismatch against the container's 8px.\n\nFixed to `inherit`, confirmed it now resolves to the same 8px the real component's default example uses. Full 157-page sweep: 0 console errors."}, {"title": "\"On this page\" outline restyled as a real Group Button", "body": "Asked to turn the auto-generated \"On this page\" section links (the row that appears under any page with more than 2 headings) into a Group Button. Rather than reskin it to merely look like one, wired it to the actual component: each link gets the real `.group-btn-segment`/`.group-btn-segment--h36` classes group-button.html itself uses, inside a filled segmented-control container.\n\nWent one step further than a paint job, since a real Group Button's whole point is \"exactly one segment selected at a time,\" and a purely static restyle would leave that meaningless: added an IntersectionObserver that marks whichever section is actually scrolled into view as `.is-selected`, live, as you scroll - not just on click. Verified directly: scrolling to a specific section updates the highlighted segment correctly, clicking still jumps via the real anchor hrefs, and the container wraps cleanly onto a second row on a page with 10 sections at a narrow viewport (confirmed at 700px) rather than overflowing.\n\nFull 157-page sweep: 0 console errors."}, {"title": "A real AI Foundation", "body": "The last of the three deferred items. This is the Agentic Design System, but until now nothing in it visually distinguished AI-generated content, agent actions, or how confident the system is about either - `ai-generator.html` was pure \"Coming soon\" vision copy, and the audit's own AI-readiness section found essentially nothing shipped at the token or component level.\n\nAdded a real ninth Foundation page, `ai-foundation.html` (\"AI & Agents\"), linked from every page's sidebar, built entirely on new tokens rather than one-off styling: one new hue, `--color-ai-accent` (violet, the common cross-industry association for \"AI,\" distinct from brand red and every status color so it can never be confused with an error/warning/success state), plus `--color-ai-confidence-high/medium/low` and `--color-agent-success/failure` - both deliberately aliased onto the *existing* semantic status colors, since \"confidence\" and \"agent outcome\" are already exactly what those tokens mean.\n\nFour real, working components, not static mockups: an attribution badge (`.badge-ai`) marking AI-generated content; a three-tier confidence indicator (color and text together, never color alone); a streaming-content skeleton tinted with the AI accent instead of Skeleton's plain gray, so \"still generating\" doesn't look identical to an ordinary loading state; and a genuinely interactive approve/reject/undo pattern - a new `.btn-demo--reject` variant paired with the existing `.btn-demo--approve`, wired to a real demo card that shows the outcome and a working Undo link (verified via direct click-through: approve, undo, reject, all confirmed to actually transition state, not just declared in markup). Also added an explainability pattern using a native `<details>`/`<summary>` disclosure with citations - keyboard-operable by default, no bespoke JS needed for the interaction itself.\n\n`ai-generator.html`'s own copy now points here, rather than remaining a dead end with no connection to the rest of the system."}, {"title": "A real Changelog and an honest version number", "body": "Governance was a static \"Owner: Design System Team · Version: v3.4.0 · Changelog: not yet tracked\" string, identical on all 155 pages - the version never moved and the changelog claim was false (this file already existed and was already being maintained every session).\n\nAdded `tools/generate_changelog.py` (parses this file's own `## ` sections into `changelog.js`, the same file:// -compatible script-tag pattern as `docs-catalog.js`) and a real `changelog.html` page rendering it, newest-first, with a separate \"Known gaps\" section for the \"Known follow-ups\" heading below. Every component page's metadata line now links there instead of claiming \"not yet tracked,\" and the version bumped to v3.5.0 - an honest reflection of the accumulated work since v3.4.0, not an arbitrary increment. \"Owner: Design System Team\" is left as a static string deliberately: a single-team ownership model is a legitimate governance choice for a system this size, not a gap that needs a multi-owner/RBAC model invented to fill it."}, {"title": "Component-tier tokens, established on 4 real components", "body": "Extended the token architecture with a third tier (primitive -> semantic -> component), demonstrated on Button's Primary variant, Table, Modal and Card rather than attempted across all 63 components at once - a component token is a named alias for one specific component/state, so restyling \"the Card\" becomes one edit in theme.css instead of a hunt through shell.css for every rule that happens to reference `--shadow-sm` for an unrelated reason too.\n\nAdded `--button-primary-bg-default/hover/active/text/focus-ring/disabled-opacity`, `--table-cell-padding-y-default/compact` plus header/border-color aliases, `--modal-scrim/surface/shadow/border-color`, `--card-surface/border-color/shadow-rest/hover/pressed` - each aliasing the existing semantic/primitive layer, plus one new semantic token (`--color-on-brand`, fixing a hardcoded `#fff` text-on-brand-background gap flagged during the audit). Verified byte-identical rendering before/after on all four components - this reorganizes what already existed, it doesn't change how anything looks. Extending this tier further is real, valuable follow-up work."}, {"title": "Full Foundation-layer production-readiness audit, then closed almost every finding", "body": "Asked for a comprehensive audit of all 8 Foundation tabs (Color, Grid & Layout, Typography, Spacing, Radius, Borders, Shadows, Icons) against design-token architecture, responsive design, WCAG 2.2 AA, enterprise/data-heavy SaaS needs, and AI-readiness - then asked to implement the findings by priority, testing each one before moving to the next.\n\nDelivered a 26-section audit (grounded in direct reads of every foundation page plus theme.css/shell.css/experience.js, not assumption) that found the system's real gap: every foundation documented a rich scale in prose, but only Color and (partially) Radius backed that scale with real CSS custom properties - Spacing and Shadows had zero tokens despite each documenting a full scale, so every component needing spacing or shadow values had to hardcode literals.\n\n**P0 (10 items)**, each implemented and verified: added the full `--space-*` (17 steps) and `--shadow-*` (7 levels) token scales, wiring them into Button/Table/Modal/Drawer (and correcting a real drift found in the process - Modal's actual shadow didn't match its own documented XL default); fixed a real bug in Icons (`icons.js` wired a 7-option style picker to markup that didn't exist in `icons.html` at all - dead code, always silently falling back to a hardcoded default - fixing it exposed a second bug, the same click-hijack regression from earlier in this session, now fixed identically); added `aria-hidden` to every generated icon SVG; corrected two real documentation-contradicts-implementation issues (`values.html` claimed a live density/direction toggle that didn't exist; `grid-layout.html` claimed to define breakpoints but contained none - replaced with real breakpoint documentation); shipped `design-tokens.json` (W3C Design Tokens format, generated by `tools/generate_tokens.py` from theme.css); added a 3:1 non-text contrast check for Control Border (was being held to the wrong 4.5:1 text threshold); shipped a real sitewide density mode (`data-density` attribute, Settings toggle, wired into `.app-main`/`.panel`).\n\n**P1 (15 items)**: completed the Radius and Border-width token scales and migrated 206 real call sites to reference them; differentiated Borders' Active/Selected states (were byte-identical); wired a real monospace font (JetBrains Mono, replacing \"Inter\" masquerading as `--font-mono`) and a letter-spacing token scale (48 sites); added `--opacity-40/50` and `--color-focus-ring` semantic tokens (103 sites); documented Icons' interaction states and icon-to-text spacing; closed Radius/Shadows/Borders navigation and card/table guidance gaps; fixed Card's shadow to actually match its own documented SM/MD/XS spec; added Grid & Layout's missing Master-Detail pattern and a real overflow policy; added Spacing's Component Defaults table; added a real responsive H1 step-down and tabular figures to Typography; shipped a real, live RTL direction toggle (the existing icon-mirroring CSS had never actually been exercised before - verified it fires correctly now).\n\n**P2 (9 items)**: distinguished Typography's Body/Paragraph levels and added the missing semantic-level reference table and AI Rule callout; added Grid & Layout's closing section; documented Radius's nested-corner formula and Borders' \"when not to use one\" guidance; added an alpha slider (preview/copy only, doesn't touch the hex-only data model) and a color-blindness (CVD) simulation to the color-picker popover; clarified Danger/Brand's coincidental color match; shipped a dedicated Accessibility documentation page consolidating real, already-shipped infrastructure with an honest \"known gaps\" list.\n\nTwo CSS-parsing bugs caught and fixed mid-flight via direct computed-style verification, not assumed from source: a `*/`-truncation from writing two token names back-to-back in a comment (now in this project's own persistent memory), and the same gotcha inside `tools/generate_tokens.py`'s theme.css parser.\n\nDeliberately deferred, as each needs real design decisions rather than a fix-and-verify pass: a full **AI Foundation** (tokens, attribution/confidence/streaming components, explainability), **component-tier tokens** across the full 63-component library, and a **real governance/versioning model** - see below for those."}, {"title": "Colors - popover's format dropdown had its native arrow flush against the border", "body": "Follow-up, from a close-up screenshot: the chevron inside the \"Hex\" dropdown had almost no breathing room from the field's right edge. This is a known limitation of a native `<select>`'s own arrow, not something padding can fix - `.form-field select` elsewhere in this codebase already has a comment about exactly this (\"the native one sits flush against the edge on some browsers/OSes with no way to control its position\"), solved there by turning the native arrow off and drawing a custom one. `.color-popover-format-select` had been styled before that pattern was applied here, so it still had the unfixable native arrow.\n\nApplied the same fix: `appearance: none` to remove the native arrow, a small inline SVG chevron as the background image, positioned a fixed 8px from the right edge, with the field's own right padding increased to keep the chevron clear of the text. Reused the exact same SVG markup already used for `.form-field select`, for visual consistency across the site rather than introducing a second slightly-different chevron.\n\nVerified visually (chevron now sits with clear, even spacing on both sides in a cropped screenshot) and functionally (all five format options still selectable and still convert correctly). Full 154-page sweep: 0 console errors."}, {"title": "Colors - popover's format dropdown wasn't level with its value field", "body": "Follow-up, pointed at a screenshot of the popover's format row: the \"Hex\" dropdown sat visibly lower than the value field beside it, not flush with it the way a dropdown-plus-field control normally reads. Cause: every value field has a small uppercase label above it (\"#\", \"R\", \"H\", ...), but the format `<select>` had no label of its own - with the row's `align-items: center`, that extra label-height on one side and not the other centered the shorter select against the taller (label + field) column next to it, leaving it hanging below where it should sit.\n\nFixed by wrapping the select in the exact same label+control structure as every value cell, with an invisible (`visibility: hidden`, not removed - it still needs to hold its height) spacer standing in for the label. Both columns are now identically tall regardless of format, so the select and field align on their own without needing any special-cased vertical offset.\n\nVerified: select and input top/bottom edges now measure within 1px of each other (the native OS select renders its own border a hair differently than the styled text input) in Hex mode, and the same holds after switching to RGB, HSL, HSB and CSS. Full 154-page sweep: 0 console errors."}, {"title": "Colors - alignment/spacing pass: a stray popover and a misaligned grid row", "body": "Asked for a general review of alignment, spacing, margins, padding and gutters on the Colors tab. Rather than restyle things that already measured out consistently, checked first and found two real, specific bugs.\n\nThe bigger one: the new color-picker popover from the previous change rendered on every page load, pinned at the top-left corner and overlapping the sidebar, whether or not anyone had clicked a swatch. Cause: `.color-popover` sets its own `display: flex` for when it's open, and that - being an author-stylesheet rule - overrides the browser's built-in `[hidden]{ display: none }` default at equal specificity, so toggling the element's `hidden` property stopped actually hiding it the moment that class carried a `display` value. Fixed with one rule, `.color-popover[hidden]{ display: none; }` (an attribute+class selector, so it wins regardless of source order): confirmed via computed style that `display` now correctly reads `none` while `hidden` is true, and `flex` once it's open.\n\nThe other was a real grid inconsistency, not a false alarm: the AA/AAA badge under each field wraps its ratio text and two pills differently depending on how much room the field has. In the 4-up Semantic & status colors row, Success and Warning's badges fit on one line while Danger and Info's AAA pill spilled onto a lonely second line - so in the very same row, two columns' description text (\"Destructive actions...\", \"Neutral, non-urgent context.\") sat 24px lower than the other two's, breaking the row's shared baseline. Fixed by always giving the ratio text its own line (`flex-basis: 100%`), so every badge wraps the same way regardless of field width. Verified with exact measurements before and after: Danger/Info's badge height dropped from 42px to the same 38px as Success/Warning, and all four columns' description text now starts at the identical 2079.78px top offset.\n\nAlso verified, to confirm they weren't part of the complaint: the 6 section panels' padding (24px, all six) and the vertical gap between them (24px, all five gaps) were already exactly uniform - nothing to fix there. Full 154-page sweep: 0 console errors."}, {"title": "Colors - replaced the OS-native color picker with one that has a format switcher and inline AA/AAA", "body": "Reported with two reference screenshots: the color picker (the small square swatch next to each hex field) doesn't show AA/AAA accessibility guidance while a color is actually being chosen, and only offers Hex - no RGB, HSL, HSB or CSS. Confirmed: every one of the 32 swatches was a bare `<input type=\"color\">`, so clicking it opened whatever picker the OS/browser provides (on this Mac/Chrome combination: a saturation/brightness square, a hue slider, and RGB-only number fields - no Hex readout, no format switcher, no accessibility feedback at all, and not something this codebase can add anything to since it's rendered outside the page entirely).\n\nBuilt a custom popover to replace it: the same saturation/brightness square and hue slider, plus a format dropdown (Hex/RGB/HSL/HSB/CSS - CSS renders as a copyable `rgb(r, g, b)` string with its own Copy button) and the exact same live AA/AAA readout as the badge under the field, updating as you drag. The underlying native `<input type=\"color\">` for each swatch is kept exactly as-is under the hood (still the one thing every other function in `foundations.js` reads and writes) - just visually hidden, with the visible swatch button driving it by dispatching a real `input` event on it. That one choice meant wireHexPair, the Save-state dirty tracking, and the contrast badge below the field all react to a popover-driven change exactly as they already did to someone typing a hex value, with no changes needed to any of that existing code.\n\nFound and fixed one real bug during verification: the popover closed itself the instant it opened. Root cause was a `window` \"scroll\" listener meant to close the popover if the page scrolled out from under it - but focusing the picker square right after opening (for keyboard users) can itself trigger a scroll, which that same listener caught and treated as \"user scrolled away,\" closing the popover before the click that opened it had even finished. Fixed by repositioning the popover on scroll instead of closing it, which also means it now correctly tracks its swatch if the page scrolls while it's open, rather than drifting or disappearing.\n\nVerified all 32 swatches (both light-column, visible by default, and dark-column, revealed via each section's \"customize dark separately\" checkbox): popover opens with the right starting color, all five formats convert correctly and can be edited directly (confirmed RGB/HSL/HSB/CSS math against known values, e.g. brand red FF031A -> rgb(255,3,26) -> hsl(355,100,51) -> hsb(355,99,100)), editing any format updates the hex text field, the below-field badge and the trigger's own swatch color together, dragging the hue slider changes the color, arrow keys nudge it, Escape and outside-click both close it, the chosen format is remembered across reopening a different swatch, and it stays open and repositions correctly through a scroll instead of closing. Full 154-page sweep: 0 console errors."}, {"title": "Colors - AA/AAA contrast checker was missing entirely, not hidden", "body": "Reported as: \"the AA and AAA color contrast checker is not visible\" on every color picker below its hex input. Checked first rather than assuming it was a display bug - a sitewide grep for \"contrast\", \"AA\" and \"AAA\" across `foundations.html`/`foundations.js` turned up nothing relevant at all. It wasn't hidden or broken; it never existed. (An old, already-superseded audit doc referenced AA/AAA badges, but those belonged to a much earlier, since-removed color-picker UI - unrelated to the current one.)\n\nBuilt it from scratch: three small WCAG helper functions (sRGB-to-linear conversion, relative luminance, contrast ratio) plus a badge under all 32 color fields across Brand, Background, Status, Neutral and Text, showing the ratio and a pass/fail pill for both AA (4.5:1) and AAA (7:1). Each field is checked against the color it would realistically sit next to: Brand/Status/Neutral/Text swatches are checked against their own theme's page background (light fields vs. the light Background section's current Primary color, dark fields vs. the dark one), recomputed live on any edit anywhere on the page - so changing the background color immediately updates every other section's badges, not just its own.\n\nBackground's own three swatches (Page/Panel/Raised) needed different treatment: checking a background color's contrast against \"the page background\" is checking it against itself, which is always a meaningless ~1:1 fail. Those three are checked against the page's own primary text color instead (i.e. \"would your body text still be readable on this surface\"), labeled \"vs primary text\" instead of \"vs page background\" so it's clear what's actually being compared.\n\nFirst pass anchored each badge to `.color-rgba`, which turned out to exist on only 6 of the 32 fields (Brand shows a live `rgb()` string there; Status's light column reuses the class for static descriptive text; the other 22 fields have no such element) - only 6 badges rendered instead of 32. Fixed by anchoring to `.hex-input-wrap` instead, which every field actually has.\n\nVerified all 32 badges render with sane values (e.g. brand red 3.68:1 vs. the light page background, correctly failing AA - matches known real-world behavior of saturated reds on near-white; body text-hi 16.60:1, correctly passing both; text-dim 3.29:1, correctly failing both, consistent with an old audit's independent finding that the \"dim\" text tier was under-contrast). Confirmed live recompute by editing the background hex directly and watching an unrelated section's badge update. Full 154-page sweep: 0 console errors."}, {"title": "Colors - each section's Save button now shows whether it's actually saved", "body": "Reported as: the Save button on each Colors section (Brand, Surface, Status, Neutral, Text) always just says \"Save,\" with no way to tell whether that section currently holds a saved value or is still showing defaults - and no way to tell it apart from \"saved, but you've since changed something and need to save again.\" The only existing feedback was a transient \"Saved just now\" message next to the button that disappeared after 2.5 seconds, so that information was gone the moment you reloaded the page or moved on.\n\nAdded persistent save-state tracking, per section, independent of the other four: the button itself now reads \"Save brand color\" when the fields on screen don't match what's saved (nothing saved yet, or edited since the last save) and \"Saved brand color\" (visually distinct - muted, like the Reset button next to it - instead of staying red like an action still waiting to happen) the moment they do. This isn't a value-by-value comparison against the saved JSON (hex casing and optional dark-override keys make that more fragile than it's worth) - it tracks the simpler, equivalent fact directly: the fields start in sync with storage right after a page load or a successful save, and fall out of sync the instant anything in that section is actually edited. A single delegated `input`/`change` listener on each section's own container catches every field in it - hex text, the native color pickers, and the \"customize dark separately\" checkbox - without having to wire each one by hand.\n\nApplied identically to all 5 sections (`grid-layout.js` untouched - this is Colors-specific). Verified: a fresh page load with nothing saved shows \"Save ...\" on all 5; saving one flips it to \"Saved ...\" and persists across a reload; editing any field in that section immediately reverts it to \"Save ...\"; saving again restores \"Saved ...\"; Reset always returns to \"Save ...\" (correct, since Reset means nothing is saved anymore); editing one section never affects another's state. Full 154-page sweep: 0 console errors."}, {"title": "Grid & Layout, Spacing, Radius, Borders, Shadow - selecting a card did nothing at all", "body": "Reported as \"when I click on the selection, values are not changing\" across Grid & Layout, Colors, Typography, Spacing, Radius, Borders, Shadow and Icons. Tested each one directly rather than assuming all eight had the same problem. Colors (typing a hex value updates the RGBA text and scale chips correctly), Typography (choosing a category then a font correctly updates the live preview's font-family) and Icons (changing the size/stroke/corner selects correctly updates the preview SVG; clicking a library card correctly checks its radio) all worked exactly as expected once tested the way a real user actually interacts with them.\n\nThe other five - Grid & Layout, Spacing, Radius, Borders, Shadow - were genuinely, completely broken: clicking any product or system card did nothing whatsoever, silently, with no console error. Root cause: on these five pages specifically, the clickable \"card\" is itself a `<button class=\"product-card\">`/`<button class=\"system-card\">` element (every other card-based page on the site - all 60+ component listing pages - uses a `<div class=\"system-card\">` instead). The click-hijack guard added earlier this session (`if (e.target.closest(\"button, input, select, textarea, a, [role=combobox]\")) return;`, meant to stop a card's own navigate/select handler from also firing when a click actually landed on a nested interactive element like a Copy button) matches the *card itself* whenever the card is a real `<button>` - so every single click on these cards, no matter where on the card, was being silently swallowed by a guard meant only to catch clicks on something *nested inside* it.\n\nFixed by checking that the closest interactive ancestor isn't the card itself: `var hit = e.target.closest(...); if (hit && hit !== card) return;`. Applied to all 8 affected click handlers across `grid-layout.js` (×2: product cards, system cards), `spacing.js` (×2), `radius.js` (×2), `borders.js` (×1) and `shadows.js` (×1) - confirmed via a sitewide grep that these were the only 5 HTML pages using a `<button>`-tag card (every `*-detail.js` file's own guard is untouched and doesn't need this, since none of their cards are buttons).\n\nVerified each of the 5 pages individually after the fix (clicking a product card now correctly activates its matching system and shows the recommendation callout; clicking a system card directly now correctly switches which one is marked active) and re-ran the full 154-page sweep: 0 console errors."}, {"title": "MD Export - actually fixed the file:// case, not just explained it", "body": "Follow-up to the previous entry. The earlier fix (a clearer error message when opened via `file://`) was a real improvement over the misleading one before it, but it was still just explaining a failure, not fixing it - and the user correctly called that out. Reopened it and fixed the actual cause instead.\n\nRoot cause: two of MD Export's three scopes called `fetch(\"docs-catalog.json\")`, and `fetch` is blocked against the `file:` protocol by every browser - a hard platform restriction, not a bug in this code, but one this code didn't need to be subject to. A `<script src>` tag, unlike `fetch`, is *not* restricted under `file://` - every other script on every other page of this 154-page site already loads that way and works fine opened directly. So the fix was to stop fetching the catalog and start loading it the same way: `tools/audit_pages.py` now also writes `docs-catalog.js` (`window.ADSDocsCatalog = [...]`) alongside the existing `docs-catalog.json`, `md-export.html` loads it via a plain `<script src=\"docs-catalog.js\">` before `experience.js`, and `experience.js`'s `generate()` reads `window.ADSDocsCatalog` directly - no `fetch`, no async wait, no `file://` special-case error message needed at all anymore. Kept `docs-catalog.json` as-is for `tools/browser-audit.js`, which already runs against a live server and has no reason to change.\n\nVerified by reproducing the user's exact scenario (`file:///.../md-export.html`, opened directly, no server): all three scopes now generate real content (958 chars / 427 KB / 543 KB, matching what serving over HTTP produces), Copy Markdown copies the full generated text, Refresh Preview works, and the deep-link \"Export this guide\" flow (`?page=table-default.html`) still resolves to that page's real content. Re-ran the full 154-page sweep over HTTP too: 0 console errors, all three scopes still correct there as well."}, {"title": "MD Export - two of the three scopes silently failed when the page wasn't served over HTTP", "body": "Reported as \"Project brief and saved foundations, Brief + all component detail guides, Brief + complete documentation is not working, and generating Markdown preview, Copy Markdown and Refresh Preview [don't work].\" Measured before assuming anything about the code: all three scopes, plus Copy/Refresh/Download, worked correctly when the portal was served via `python3 -m http.server` (confirmed in both Chromium and WebKit). The difference showed up when reproducing the page the way a user opening a local HTML file directly would - `file:///.../md-export.html`, no server. Under that condition, the default \"Project brief and saved foundations\" scope still worked (958 characters, no problem), but switching to either \"Brief + all component detail guides\" or \"Brief + complete documentation\" failed outright, leaving the preview blank - both scopes call `fetch(\"docs-catalog.json\")` to pull in the component documentation text, and browsers refuse to run `fetch` at all against the `file:` protocol. Since Copy Markdown and Download both just act on whatever is currently in the preview textarea, they \"stopped working\" too the moment the preview went blank - not independently broken, just downstream of the same failure.\n\nThe failure itself was real but the *message* shown for it was actively misleading: the raw browser error (\"Failed to fetch\") got a generic suffix stitched onto it (\"... Check saved foundation values or try again.\"), which points at completely the wrong thing - nothing about saved foundation values was the problem. Fixed by detecting `location.protocol === \"file:\"` before attempting the fetch and showing a specific, actionable message instead: that this scope needs the portal served over `http(s)://`, gives the exact command to do that (`python3 -m http.server` in the project folder, then open the page from `http://localhost`), and offers the one scope that doesn't need a server as an immediate alternative. Also dropped the same generic \"Check saved foundation values or try again.\" suffix from the other two error paths on this page (a failed catalog fetch, and an empty result set) - both of those already end in a complete, specific sentence of their own and didn't need it either.\n\nVerified the new message reproduces correctly under the exact `file://` condition that caused the original report, confirmed all three scopes still generate correctly when properly served (958 / ~427 KB / ~543 KB, matching the page count each scope claims), and re-ran the full 154-page sweep: 0 console errors."}, {"title": "Cataloged a parallel Codex editing session, then finished what it left open", "body": "A parallel Codex session had edited this same working copy directly (226 files, +1298/-13739 lines, all still uncommitted) - de-duplicating every page's inline theme-bootstrap script into `theme-init.js`, splitting styles across `theme.css`/`shell.css`/`experience.css`, and building a genuinely new feature layer (`project-model.js` + `experience.js`): a real 3-step New Project setup wizard replacing the old static mockup and the welcome modal, a working MD Export page, a populated Settings hub, sitewide accessibility injections (skip-link, page-outline nav, `aria-current`), and a real fix for clickable cards swallowing clicks meant for a button/input/link nested inside them (the exact bug class the Copy prompt/Copy code buttons and the welcome modal's Select combobox were exposed to). Full writeup, including everything read and verified to build this picture, is in `CODEX-CHANGES.md`.\n\nRather than only documenting it, went looking for what Codex hadn't finished. Its own after-the-fix browser audit (`audit/browser-second-pass.json`, 308 checks across every page at two widths) flagged exactly one thing: `modal-default.html` overflowing at 390px. Chased it four different ways - the plain check, with the Component Guide toggles clicked first, inside an actual iframe matching the audit's own harness, and with web fonts blocked to test a late-font-swap theory - zero reproductions each time, and no fixed-width element anywhere near that range in the page's own CSS. Concluded it was a one-off flake in that specific audit run rather than a real bug, and said so plainly instead of manufacturing a fix with nothing to point at.\n\nFound and finished two real, smaller loose ends instead:\n- **`theme.css` was loading twice on every page** - Codex's new explicit `<link>` alongside `shell.css`'s own pre-existing `@import url(\"theme.css\")` at the top of that file. Removed the redundant `<link>` from all 154 pages; kept the `@import`, since that's the path every page already depended on before this session. Verified `--red-500` and other theme tokens still resolve correctly afterward.\n- **`welcome.js` was orphaned** - Codex's new wizard replaced the modal it powered, but the file itself, and nothing else, was left with zero remaining references anywhere in the codebase. Confirmed that with a full-site grep before deleting it.\n\nAlso ran a direct functional pass across the new feature surface that its own audit doesn't cover end-to-end: wizard validation (empty-submit blocking, \"Other\" reveal fields), Settings with no saved profile, the skip-link/page-outline/component-open-link accessibility injections, the \"Export this guide\" deep link from a detail page (confirmed it pre-selects the right scope and the exported Markdown actually contains that page's content), and the click-hijack fix specifically against this session's own Copy prompt/Copy code buttons. Nothing further found. Re-ran `python3 tools/audit_pages.py`, `node --test tests/project-model.test.js`, and the full 154-page live sweep after both fixes - catalog regenerates clean (0 source issues), all 4 tests pass, 0 console errors, 0 failed requests."}, {"title": "Design Principles - all 10 Do/Don't illustrations were centered instead of left-aligned, plus one duplicate-attribute bug", "body": "Reported as \"alignment, spacing, padding, margin and gutter issues\" across every section. Measured before touching anything: `.guide-dodont-demo` defaults to `justify-content:center` (correct for a typical single small component swatch, which is what it's used for on the other 60+ component pages), but every one of these 10 illustrations is a real, multi-element composition (a form, a file row, a button-plus-caption) - centering that kind of content leaves it floating in a sea of empty space on both sides, and for any row with more than one child, the \"first child\" isn't the same as \"the whole visual group,\" so the actual content silently drifted left or right by a different amount in every section depending on how many children it had. Confirmed by measuring each of the 10 Do/Don't pairs' content inset from its box's left edge before the fix: values ranged from 12px to 177px depending on the section, with no consistency even between a Do and its own Don't.\n\nFixed by adding `justify-content:flex-start` and a consistent `20px` padding to all 20 `.guide-dodont-demo` instances on this page (matching the same override already used for Patterns/Templates' own wider illustrations) - re-measured after: every single one of the 20 boxes now insets its content by exactly 20px, Do and Don't lining up at the same left edge in all 10 sections.\n\nFixing the horizontal drift surfaced two more real bugs it had been hiding:\n- **\"Keep it Lightweight\"'s Don't example** - \"Step 1 of 5\" was a flex-row sibling next to the two stacked input fields, so centering (previously) or the row's own cross-axis behavior placed it floating vertically mid-height instead of sitting above them as an actual step indicator. Wrapped it and the fields in their own column so it now reads correctly as a wizard step label.\n- **\"Provide an Invitation\"'s Don't example** - a fixed `width:200px` row couldn't fit both the filename and its long caption, so \"Q1 Report.pdf\" was wrapping mid-word onto two lines. Removed the fixed width and added `white-space:nowrap`, letting the row grow to fit both.\n- **\"Alignment\" section was not actually illustrating its own point** - every field in both the Do and Don't examples had two `style=\"...\"` attributes on the same element (a leftover from an earlier edit). HTML silently keeps only the *first* `style` attribute on a duplicate and discards the second - so every field rendered at a flat 140px with no offset, regardless of the intended per-field width/`margin-inline-start` overrides. This made the Don't example accidentally render identically to the Do example, both looking \"aligned\" - the exact opposite of what the section is teaching. Merged each pair of duplicate attributes into one; the Don't side now genuinely shows fields starting and ending at different points, matching its own caption.\n\nSite-wide grep for the same duplicate-`style`-attribute pattern came back with zero other instances. Verified all 10 sections visually after every fix and re-ran the full 154-page sweep: 0 console errors."}, {"title": "Audited Design System/Guidelines sidebar tabs - all 7 kept, two real content gaps fixed", "body": "Asked to check whether Overview, Design Values, New Project, Design Principles, Component Guidelines, Copywriting and Data Format are actually needed, and remove whichever aren't. Read every one of these 7 pages in full (not just skimmed) rather than assuming the \"lots of tabs\" complaint meant more of them were as thin as Code/Documentation/Governance turned out to be. Conclusion: all 7 are real, substantial, non-redundant content (each 300-475 lines, none of it boilerplate) - Design Principles' 10 Do/Don't pairs, Component Guidelines' 6 \"which one, when\" decision matrices, Copywriting and Data Format's rule tables, Design Values' 4 values plus where each is already enforced, and Overview/New Project as the portal's own entry points - none overlaps with anything else in the portal the way Code/Documentation/Governance did. Kept all 7 as-is; nothing removed.\n\nWhile reading in depth, found and fixed two real content gaps:\n\n- **Four missing descriptions**: \"Numbers and currency,\" \"Dates and times\" and \"Missing and sensitive data\" on Data Format, plus \"Capitalization and punctuation\" on Copywriting, each had an empty `<p class=\"get-library-copy\"></p>` where every sibling section on both pages has a one-sentence description. Wrote one for each, matching the page's own established voice.\n- **36 inert filename references on Component Guidelines**: every \"which component to reach for\" row ends with the target's filename (`toast.html`, `modal.html`, ...) styled as a plain label - none of them were actual links, on a page whose entire purpose is pointing you to the right component page. Wrapped all 36 in real `<a href>` tags; verified every target resolves and a sample click actually navigates.\n\nVerified no other page in the 7 has the same gaps (site-wide grep for the same empty-description pattern came back clean), confirmed no broken `.html` links anywhere in these 7 pages, and re-ran the full 154-page sweep: 0 console errors. Not yet pushed - awaiting the go-ahead."}, {"title": "Removed 3 sidebar tabs that duplicated functionality already built elsewhere", "body": "Reported as \"lots of tabs, but they don't have any good information - remove any unnecessary ones.\" Audited all 10 tabs in the sidebar's bottom group rather than acting on the description alone: 6 of them (AI Generator, Code, Figma, Documentation, Governance, Settings) turned out to be the same genre - a one-line pitch plus a \"Not built yet\" placeholder with a chip-list of planned sub-features, not broken pages, just intentionally thin (matching this portal's own established \"static mockup, be honest about what's real\" ethos). The other 4 (Patterns, Templates, MD Export, NPM Package, History) are already fully built out.\n\nChecked each placeholder's own pitch against what already exists elsewhere in the portal before deciding anything, and confirmed the result with the user (with selectable options, one tab or group at a time) before removing or keeping each:\n\n- **Code** - promised \"copy production code per component.\" Every one of the 62 components already has its own real \"Copy code\" button. Removed - a dedicated tab for this would only duplicate what's already on every component page.\n- **Documentation** - promised \"usage guidelines, do/don't rules, accessibility notes per component.\" Every component page already has exactly this in its own Component Guide section (Human View/Machine View toggle). Removed for the same reason.\n- **Governance** - promised versioning (already real, in **History**) and component status (already real, as the `.component-status` badges every component page already carries) alongside two genuinely new ideas (Approvals, Compliance audit) that weren't enough on their own to justify keeping a dedicated tab. Removed.\n- **AI Generator** and **Figma** - kept as-is. Neither duplicates anything else in the portal - AI Generator is this system's actual core premise (an agent reusing existing components before generating anything new), and Figma is a standard, non-redundant integration promise.\n- **Settings** - kept as-is. It already correctly defers to Foundations · Colors for the one piece of it that's real today, and honestly labels the rest (team access, integrations) as not built yet.\n\nRemoved `code.html`, `documentation.html` and `governance.html` entirely, their sidebar `<a class=\"nav-link\">` entries across all 157 pages (byte-identical markup, removed via a scripted pass), and the one other cross-reference to any of them (a \"Governance\" row in Overview's own \"Guidelines & governance\" panel, which also linked to `governance.html`). Verified afterward: no remaining `.html` href pointing at a file that no longer exists anywhere in the codebase, and a full sweep of all 154 remaining pages - 0 console errors, 0 failed asset/page requests."}, {"title": "Patterns and Templates, round 2 - icons and structure that didn't match the real components they claimed to be built from", "body": "Reported again after the previous pass (\"the components are not matching our design system\") with a screenshot of the KPI row. That specific screenshot turned out to be the real Card component's own documented hover effect (verified: identical `border-color`/`box-shadow` change reproduces on Card's own listing page at `card.html`, not a bug), but re-auditing all 10 illustrations against their real counterparts instead of stopping there turned up four genuine mismatches, all from the same root cause: several illustrations used a plain Unicode/emoji character or skipped a piece of markup instead of the real component's own SVG icon or structure.\n\n- **Search icon** (Filter bar, Search + results): was the Unicode character `⚲` (U+26C2, Misc. Technical block - not a magnifying glass in most fonts), not the real Search component's SVG (`circle` + diagonal `line`). Both instances swapped in the real SVG.\n- **Date Range's calendar icon** (Filter bar): was 📅 (an emoji, OS/font-dependent rendering), not the real component's SVG (`rect` + tick lines). Swapped in the real SVG.\n- **Select's chevron was missing entirely** (Filter bar's \"Status\" field, Settings template's \"UTC+1\" field) - both rendered as a bare box with no dropdown affordance at all, unlike every real Select instance elsewhere in the portal. Added the same chevron SVG Select uses everywhere else.\n- **Statistic's trend arrows** (KPI row): were the Unicode characters `▲`/`▼`, not the real component's SVG arrows (`line` + `polyline`). Swapped both in.\n\nAlso found two structural gaps in `templates.html` while re-checking each \"Built from\" credit against what the markup actually contains (three of the ten were listing a component that wasn't actually there - `Search, Table, Empty` when no table existed, `... Divider` when no divider existed - corrected to match reality):\n\n- **Dashboard's \"Recent activity\" table** had plain text status cells (`Active`/`Invited`) instead of the real Table component's own status-dot treatment (a colored `badge-demo-status-dot--*` dot, reused from Badge, precedes the label in every real usage). Added the dots (success/warning).\n- **List + detail's \"list\" half was never actually the List component** - two plain inline-styled `<div>`s standing in for it, which is exactly why its own credit line was wrong. Rebuilt using the real `.list-demo-panel`/`.list-demo-row`/`.list-demo-body`/`.list-demo-title` markup, with the selected row highlighted the same way the sidebar marks its own active link (`--red-tint` background, red left border) - and its neighboring Description pairing (`STATUS`/`Paid`) was missing the `.description-demo-item` wrapper the real component requires to stack label over value, so the two were running together on one line; wrapped it correctly.\n\nVerified all four icon swaps and both structural fixes visually, re-ran all 20 Copy prompt/Copy code buttons (content changes automatically since both scripts read the live DOM, not a duplicated copy), and the full 157-page sweep: 0 console errors."}, {"title": "Patterns and Templates - real alignment bugs plus a structural gap against the rest of the portal", "body": "Reported as \"two tabs not coming properly, not aligned with the design system, a lot of things missing.\" Measured each of the 10 illustrations (5 Patterns + 5 Templates) rather than reacting to the description alone, and found both a real content gap and four concrete rendering bugs, all on `patterns.html` (`templates.html`'s own 5 illustrations had none of these).\n\n**Rendering bugs, found by direct measurement:**\n- **Filter bar**: the \"Any date\" Date Range control had no width of its own (`.daterange-demo-control` is `width:100%` by design, meant for a field sitting alone in its own column) - next to Search/Select's explicit fixed widths in this shared row, it stretched to fill the remaining line and forced the \"Clear\" button onto its own row below instead of sitting inline. Fixed by giving it an explicit `width:170px` here, matching its neighbors' scale.\n- **KPI row**: `$84.2k`/`▲ 6%` and `2.1%`/`▼ 0.4%` were visibly clipping to `$8...`/`6%` and `2....`/`0.4%` (confirmed the underlying text was correct - `$84.2k` in the DOM - and that this was purely a layout clip, not a truncated string). Root cause: the trend badge was nested *inside* `<p class=\"statistic-demo-value\">` alongside the value text, competing for the same cramped flex row - but the real Statistic component's own generator (`statistic-detail.js`) explicitly renders trend as a **sibling below** the value, specifically so it doesn't compete with the number for space (a comment in `shell.css` already documents this: \"Its own line below the value now... not a flex child inside .statistic-demo-value\"). Patterns.html just hadn't been built to match that. Fixed by moving each trend span out to be a sibling, matching the real component's own structure exactly - which incidentally resolves the \"not reusing the real component's own layout\" complaint at the same time.\n- **Two Empty-state illustrations** (\"Search + results\" and \"Empty state layout\") had a completely blank icon - `.empty-demo-icon-wrap` existed but nothing was ever put inside it, unlike the real Empty component (`empty-detail.js`), which always renders an icon SVG inside that wrapper. Added the same inbox icon SVG the real component uses to both.\n\n**Structural gap:** every other section of the portal - down to a single-type component like Alert - lets you get a ready-to-paste AI prompt and the actual working code via \"Copy prompt\"/\"Copy code\" buttons. Patterns and Templates had neither; they were pure static pictures. Confirmed scope with the user before building anything (add Copy actions to the existing 10 illustrations vs. a full click-through-to-its-own-page rebuild matching Components' architecture) - chose the lighter option, keeping today's single flat page per tab.\n\nAdded `patterns.js` and `templates.js`: each wires a \"Copy prompt\" and \"Copy code\" button under every illustration. Deliberately reads each illustration's own title, description and \"Built from\" text straight out of the DOM at click time (rather than duplicating that content into the JS as separate strings), and \"Copy code\" reads the demo `<div>`'s actual live `innerHTML` (stripping the `readonly`/`tabindex=\"-1\"` attributes used to keep these static illustrations non-interactive) - so the copied prompt and code can never drift out of sync with what's actually shown on the page. Matches the existing `copyTextFull`/`fallbackCopyFull` clipboard pattern already duplicated per-page across every other `*-detail.js` file, for consistency with the rest of the codebase.\n\nVerified all 20 buttons (10 illustrations × 2 actions) via Playwright - correct \"Copied!\" feedback, correct clipboard contents, no leftover `readonly`/`tabindex` artifacts in copied code - and re-ran the full 157-page sweep afterward: 0 console errors."}, {"title": "Sidebar - collapsible group chevron was a squeezed, off-center Unicode glyph", "body": "Reported as \"the chevron icon is not coming properly - spacing/alignment/padding/margin gutter issue, looks squeezed.\" Measured before touching anything: the Foundations/Guidelines/Components group toggles' `<span class=\"chev\">` held a literal Unicode character (`&#8964;`, U+2304 \"⌄\") styled only with `font-family`/`font-size` - no explicit box, sizing, or centering of its own, unlike every other icon in this shell (search, back-link, theme toggle, Settings gear from an earlier fix), all of which are sized, explicitly-centered SVGs. U+2304 is from Unicode's phonetic/diacritic block, not designed as a standalone UI icon, and rendered thin, undersized and sitting low in its own line box in this font - reproduced and measured (chevron's vertical center sat visibly below the label text's own center before the fix).\n\nFixed by replacing the character with the same SVG chevron path already used by the Select component's own dropdown icon (`<path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/>`, one consistent chevron look across the whole portal now), across all 471 instances (157 pages &times; 3 collapsible groups each - markup was byte-identical everywhere, confirmed before a scripted replace) and giving `.chev` an explicit 14&times;14px flex-centered box. Verified: chevron's vertical center now matches the label text's center exactly (measured, not eyeballed), the existing collapse/expand rotation still works unchanged, and a full 157-page sweep confirms exactly 3 chevron SVGs on every page with 0 console errors.\n\n**Also found while verifying, unrelated to the chevron itself:** the same class of bug already fixed once this session for `.select-demo-panel` (an author CSS rule's unconditional `display` winning a same-specificity tie against the browser's own `[hidden]{ display:none }` rule, regardless of source order) turned out to affect two more elements. `.welcome-modal-overlay` - dismissing the welcome modal via Escape or a backdrop click (the only two dismissal paths that don't immediately navigate to another page) left it fully visible and blocking every click on the page underneath, invisible only in the sense that its `hidden` DOM property read `true`; every *other* dismissal path masked this because the page unloads a moment later anyway. And `.theme-pair-col` on Foundations - the 5 \"use a separate dark-theme value\" override columns (Brand/Background/Status/Neutral/Text) were permanently visible regardless of their own enable checkbox, always showing extra dark-mode input fields that should only appear once opted into. Fixed both with the same `.classname[hidden]{ display:none; }` pattern, then re-swept all 157 pages checking every `[hidden]` element's actual computed style (not just its DOM property) - zero remaining conflicts."}, {"title": "Dogfooding audit - the portal's own live UI wasn't reusing its own component library", "body": "Prompted by \"are we actually using our own components, or reinventing them\" - checked, rather than assumed. Most of the site does dogfood correctly (History's table reuses the real Table classes; every \"Copy prompt/Copy code\" action correctly uses a plain generic button rather than pulling in the full Button component's variant system, since a utility action isn't a component sample). But three real, live pieces of UI had each quietly built their own miniature stand-in for a component that already exists and is already documented, instead of reusing it. Confirmed each with the user (with selectable options) before touching anything, given the range of effort involved.\n\n**Sidebar global search** (every page) used its own `.sidebar-search`/`.search-icon` classes - structurally identical to the real Search component's `.search-demo-control`/`.search-demo-icon`, just duplicated under different names. Restyled the markup (all 157 pages, via a scripted find/replace - the search bar's HTML was byte-identical across every one of them) to use `.search-demo-control search-demo-control--filled search-demo-control--h36` + `.search-demo-icon` + `.search-demo-input` directly, and trimmed `.sidebar-search`'s own CSS down to the one thing still genuinely sidebar-specific: a visible resting border, since `--filled`'s own default is transparent and would otherwise blend invisibly into the sidebar's own background. Picked `--filled` over `--outlined` specifically because its default background (`var(--graphite-800)`) is what the sidebar search already looked like - `--outlined`'s own background would have matched the sidebar's surface color and disappeared. Bonus: the search box now also gets the Search component's own real focus ring (`outline: 2px solid var(--red-400)`) instead of the old plain border-color-only focus style.\n\n**Welcome modal's onboarding form** (Name/Designation/Email + Portal Type - live, functional, not a mockup) used a generic `.form-demo-input`/`.form-demo-select` pair. Investigated what those classes actually were before reusing anything under them: turned out to be dead CSS left over from a \"Form\" component (`form-default.html`) that was never actually built as a page in this library - so the welcome modal was reusing an *unbuilt* component's leftover styling, not a real shipped one. Switched the three text fields to the real Input component's actual markup (`.input-demo-field` > `.input-demo-label` + `.input-demo-control--outlined--h40` > `.input-demo-input`). The Portal Type field needed a bigger correction mid-task: initially planned to \"reuse Select's real open/close behavior,\" but `select-detail.js` turned out to have none - its Live Preview matrix only ever renders each state (default/filled/open/disabled/...) as a separate static markup string, picked by the page's own property controls, never wired to an actual click. Flagged this to the user before proceeding rather than quietly doing less than promised or silently over-building; they chose to have a real interactive combobox built from scratch on Select's own visual classes. Built: click-to-toggle, arrow-key highlight navigation (via `aria-activedescendant`, not real DOM focus - matching how a native `<select>`'s own listbox behaves), Enter/Space to choose, click-outside to close, and an Escape that closes just the panel (via `stopPropagation`) rather than falling through to the modal's own Escape-closes-everything handler. Found and fixed two latent CSS bugs this surfaced: `.select-demo-panel{ display:flex }` had the same specificity as the browser's own `[hidden]{ display:none }` rule and, being an author style, silently won regardless of the `hidden` attribute - added `.select-demo-panel[hidden]{ display:none }` (higher specificity, attribute + class) so toggling `hidden` actually works now that a panel persists in the DOM instead of only ever being conditionally printed once, open, in the static matrix; and `.select-demo-field:has(.select-demo-panel)`'s permanent 128px bottom margin (meant to reserve room in that always-open static illustration) needed `:not([hidden])` added so it doesn't apply while the now-real, normally-closed panel just sits there hidden.\n\n**Static mockup pages** (New Project, NPM Package, Patterns' Do/Don't card) had the same generic `.form-demo-*` fields in their read-only preview fields - aligned all of them to the real Input component's markup too, for the same reason: these are static illustrations (no working backend, an already-accepted genre per the earlier \"New capability mockups\" entry above), but there's no reason for a static illustration to invent its own field styling when the real one is sitting right there. Removed the now-fully-dead `.form-demo-*` CSS block entirely (confirmed zero remaining references in any `.html` or `.js` file first) rather than leave orphaned rules for a component that was never built.\n\nVerified each piece independently (search bar in both themes; the combobox's click/keyboard/outside-click paths and its effect on the Copy Link/Export MD actions' output; the three mockup pages' visual layout with the gap bug fixed) and re-ran the full 157-page sweep after every step: 0 console/page errors throughout."}, {"title": "UX pass - findings from testing the portal as a user, not just reading the code", "body": "Ran an actual hands-on session with Playwright (search, the welcome modal, phone-width viewport, keyboard/focus behavior) instead of only auditing markup, then confirmed each finding with the user (with selectable options) before implementing anything. Four real gaps found and fixed; one initial finding about the welcome modal was itself wrong and corrected before implementing - see below.\n\n**Welcome modal reappeared on every visit.** `welcome.js` never recorded that a visitor had already seen and dismissed it - reloading or revisiting `overview.html` always reopened it, with no way to make it stop. Fixed by writing `ads:welcome-seen` to localStorage inside `closeModal()` (which already runs on every dismissal path - choosing a project path, Escape, or backdrop click) and skipping `openModal()` entirely when that flag is already set.\n\n*Correction made before implementing:* Escape-to-close and backdrop-click-to-close were initially reported as broken too, based on an automated check that looked for a `hidden` attribute on the wrong element (`.welcome-modal` itself, a non-hidden child, instead of its parent `.welcome-overlay`, which is what actually gets `hidden`). Re-tested against the right element and both already worked correctly (`welcome.js` already had them wired up) - left untouched, only the persistence gap was real.\n\n**Global \"/\" search shortcut broke the welcome modal's focus trap.** The modal correctly traps Tab/Shift+Tab inside itself while open, but `shell.js`'s document-level \"/\" handler only skipped itself for a native `dialog[open]` - the welcome modal is a plain overlay div toggled via a `hidden` attribute, not a `<dialog>`, so it wasn't recognized and \"/\" could still yank focus out to the sidebar search behind an `aria-modal=\"true\"` dialog. Fixed by also checking for `[data-role=\"welcome-overlay\"]:not([hidden])`. Deliberately did *not* generalize this to \"any `[aria-modal=\"true\"]` element anywhere\" - Modal/Drawer/Tour's own Do/Don't illustrations on their detail pages use `aria-modal=\"true\"` permanently as static, always-visible example markup (not real toggleable overlays), so a broader check would have permanently disabled the \"/\" shortcut on those pages instead.\n\n**Mobile navigation was a dead end.** Below 900px, `.app-sidebar` was `display:none` with no toggle anywhere to bring it back - landing on any page on a phone left no way to reach any other page except a \"Back to X types\" link or the browser's own back button. Turned it into a slide-in drawer instead of building a separate mobile nav: `.app-sidebar` keeps the exact same search/collapsible-groups/active-link-scroll behavior it has on desktop, it's just `position:fixed` and translated off-screen until `.is-open` is toggled. `shell.js` injects a hamburger button into the topbar and a backdrop element at runtime (only below 900px; both are `display:none` above it) rather than duplicating that markup into all 157 pages' own topbar HTML. Backdrop click, Escape, and clicking a nav link (so navigating doesn't leave the drawer sitting open over the destination page) all close it.\n\n**Empty search results gave no explanation.** Searching for something matching zero components left the sidebar blank under the group headers with no indication why. Added a `.sidebar-search-empty` message (\"No results for '...'\"), built once and shown/hidden alongside the existing filter logic in `initSidebarSearch()`.\n\nVerified each fix individually (persistence across real navigations, not Playwright's `reload()` which can restore from bfcache and mask a missing localStorage write; drawer open/close via toggle/backdrop/Escape/nav-click; empty state appearing and clearing correctly) and re-ran the full 157-page sweep afterward: 0 console/page errors, every page still has exactly one `.nav-link.is-active`."}, {"title": "Global — Settings icon in the top bar didn't match its own sibling button", "body": "Reported as part of a page-by-page pass checking icon line-height/weight/spacing across the nav bars, listing pages, and detail pages. Compared the Settings gear button (`.icon-btn`, present on all 157 pages) against the theme toggle button right next to it (`.theme-toggle`) - a CSS comment on `.theme-toggle` already documents that the two are meant to read as a matched 32×32 pair, but only `.theme-toggle` actually had `display:flex; align-items:center; justify-content:center` and an explicit icon size. `.icon-btn` had none of that: no flex centering, no font-size, no line-height, so the gear glyph (`&#9881;`) fell back to the browser's default `<button>` styling - UA-default font-size (~13.3px), `line-height: normal`, and `padding: 1px 6px` - leaving it visibly smaller than the theme toggle's 18px icon and off-center within its box instead of centered.\n\nFixed by giving `.icon-btn` the same centering treatment as `.theme-toggle` plus an explicit `font-size: 18px; line-height: 1` sized to match the theme toggle's icon, and `padding: 0` so the flex centering isn't fighting the UA default padding. Verified with a full 157-page sweep (every page's `.icon-btn` renders at its intended 32×32 box) and a zoomed screenshot confirming the gear now matches the theme toggle's size and centering. Surveyed every other icon class in the codebase (`*-demo-icon`, `search-icon`, `back-link-icon`, `chev`, etc.) for the same anti-pattern (missing flex-centering on a glyph/SVG icon) - all of them already had explicit `display:flex` + sizing, so `.icon-btn` was the only offender."}, {"title": "Breadcrumb — listing card's preview never rendered", "body": "Reported as \"the breadcrumb isn't visible on the listing screen,\" with a screenshot of `breadcrumb.html` showing the type card's \"System Generated Breadcrumb\" label but no actual breadcrumb trail underneath it. `breadcrumb-default.html` (the detail page) rendered its own Live Preview matrix correctly, so this was isolated to the listing page's card specifically.\n\nRoot cause: `breadcrumb-detail.js`'s single `DOMContentLoaded` handler fills the listing card's preview (`buildBreadcrumbDemo(\"chevron\", ...)`, independent of any detail-page-only controls) in the same function as the detail page's Live Preview matrix setup - and an early `if (!matrixContainer) return;` guard, meant only to skip the matrix setup on `breadcrumb.html` (which has no matrix container), sat textually *before* the listing-card-preview code. Since `breadcrumb.html` never has a `matrixContainer`, that return fired immediately and skipped the listing-card preview too, leaving only the card's static label visible.\n\nFixed by moving the listing-card preview fill to before the guard (relying on `buildBreadcrumbDemo` being a hoisted function declaration, so calling it earlier in the same scope still works). Verified via `node --check`, a direct DOM check confirming the card's `innerHTML` now contains the full \"Home › Products › Shoes\" trail, a regression check that the detail page's own matrix still renders (5579 chars, 0 errors), and a clean screenshot."}, {"title": "Global — sidebar had no active item at all on every component's own type/detail page", "body": "Reported as \"links are not working, pages aren't linked to each other\" and separately \"when I'm scrolling and selecting a tab, its position in the left-side nav isn't fixed/updating.\" Rather than trust either description literally, audited the actual link graph first: every `<a href>` in every one of the 157 pages resolves to a real file, every `href=\"#hash\"` resolves to a real `id` on its target page, every dynamically-constructed `window.location.href` navigation (the `.system-card`/`.button-type-card` click handlers used by all 62 listing pages) resolves to a real file, and every \"← Back to X types\" link on all 69 detail pages points at its own correct listing page. No actual broken or misdirected links anywhere - the link graph itself is sound.\n\nThe real bug was in `shell.js`'s `markActiveLink()`, which powers both complaints at once. It only ever matched the current page's own filename against the sidebar's `<a class=\"nav-link\">` hrefs - but the sidebar only links to each component's *listing* page (`table.html`, `components.html#button`), never to its type/detail pages (`table-default.html`, `button-primary.html`, `select-dropdown.html`, etc.), since those are only reachable via the dynamic card-click navigation above. So on all 69 of those pages - exactly the pages a user actually lands on to configure and read about a component, i.e. most of the time spent on this site - `markActiveLink()` matched nothing at all: no sidebar item was ever marked `.is-active`, and the page-load `scrollIntoView` that's supposed to bring the current page's item into view never ran either, since it's gated on a match existing. The sidebar just sat at whatever scroll position it last had, with nothing highlighted - which reads exactly like \"the tab I selected isn't shown, and its position doesn't update.\"\n\nConfirmed with a full 157-page sweep before fixing: `.nav-link.is-active` existed on 88 pages and was completely absent on the other 69 (every `-default.html` page plus the 6 Button variants, Select Dropdown, and Input Text Field).\n\nFixed by adding a third fallback to `markActiveLink()`: when no sidebar link matches the current page directly, read the current page's own `.back-link` href (verified above to always be correct) and match the sidebar link against *that* instead - reusing an already-correct value rather than re-deriving the listing filename from the current page's own name, which isn't safely reversible (both the component name and the type key can contain hyphens, e.g. `date-picker-default.html`). Re-swept all 157 pages after the fix: every page now has exactly one `.is-active` sidebar link, correctly scrolled into view (verified on `table-default.html`, `button-primary.html`, `select-dropdown.html`, `date-picker-default.html`), and this holds even when the containing nav group was previously collapsed by the user (tested by forcing `ads:nav-collapsed:components=1` in storage first) - `initCollapsibleGroups` force-expands whichever group the now-correctly-matched active link lives in."}, {"title": "Table — listing page's own preview card overflowed and clipped", "body": "Reported as \"the detail page isn't matching the listing page.\" Compared `table.html` (the component's listing/index page, which embeds a small 3-column preview inside a ~330px-wide grid card) against `table-default.html` (the full page) with Playwright rather than guessing from a screenshot. The detail page rendered correctly; the listing page's card was the broken one - its preview table was clipped on both the left and right edges, with the Name column's left edge starting outside the card entirely.\n\nRoot cause: `.table-demo-table{ width:630px }`, added earlier this session so the detail page's Live Preview matrix has room for all 5 columns (Name/Status/Role/Email/Mobile), is the same class the listing page's simpler 3-column preview reuses. That fixed width forced the listing card's table to 630px regardless of its actual ~300px available space, and being a flex item inside `.table-type-card` (`display:flex`), it didn't shrink to fit the way a normal block child would - it overflowed the card on both sides instead. Measured before fixing: card was 332.6px wide, table rendered at 630px, starting 149px left of the card's own left edge. Fixed by scoping the listing card's table to `width:auto` (`.table-type-card .table-demo-table`), leaving the detail page's matrix untouched. Re-measured after: table renders at 294px, fully inside the 332.6px card, confirmed visually via screenshot.\n\nAlso surveyed all other 58 listing pages with a paired detail page (automated check for any preview-card content clipping past its card's bounds, plus zero-size/overflow checks) - Table was the only one affected. Ran the full 157-page HTTP-200 sweep and tag-balance sweep afterward; both pass clean."}, {"title": "Global — full audit of every \"Do and Don't\" section (74 pages)", "body": "Requested a page-by-page walkthrough of every component's Do/Don't illustration, checking why the layout and embedded components \"were not coming properly.\" Rather than guess from screenshots alone, ran an automated diagnostic across all 74 pages with a `.guide-dodont` section first - checking every descendant for clipping (extending outside its `.guide-dodont-demo` box, which has `overflow:hidden`) or collapsed zero-size elements - then did a full manual visual pass over every page's rendered screenshot to catch issues the automated pass couldn't (wrong/missing icons, inert CSS classes, content that contradicted its own caption). Found and fixed 14 genuine bugs, confirmed the rest (~60 pages) render correctly, including several that looked suspicious at first glance but checked out clean on measurement (Group Button's \"blue\" selected segment turned out to be a text-selection screenshot artifact, not a real color bug; Date Picker and Number both measured zero issues).\n\n**Real bugs found and fixed, one by one:**\n\n1. **Cascader** - the dropdown panel's own `min-width:360px` (correct for the real, wider component) didn't fit a Do/Don't card's ~465px box, clipping the second column's content (measured 483px content in a 465px box). Scoped `min-width:0` override.\n2. **Badge** - the \"Do\" example's count badge (`<span class=\"badge-demo-count\">3</span>`) was missing its required `.badge-demo-badge` wrapper, so it had no background color and no absolute positioning - rendered as invisible white-on-white text floating below the icon instead of a red badge on its corner.\n3. **Drawer** - the same footer-button-truncation bug as Card's earlier fix: \"Cancel\"/\"Discard\" clipped to \"Can...\"/\"Disc...\" in the 200px-wide Small drawer panel. Added a `--has-checkbox`-style scoped fix (tighter padding + `flex-shrink:0`).\n4. **Image** - the caption \"Warehouse floor plan, Level 2\" (meant to demonstrate a *complete, useful* caption) was silently truncated to \"Warehouse floor plan, L...\" by the shared 150px truncation rule. Let it wrap instead, scoped to the Do/Don't context.\n5. **Button (Primary + Icon)** - the \"Don't\" example used a literal color emoji (`&#128190;`, a floppy disk) instead of a monochrome SVG icon, rendering in jarring full color unlike every other icon in the system. Replaced with a proper stroke-based save icon.\n6. **Empty** - both examples' `.empty-demo-icon-wrap` divs were completely empty - no inbox icon inside, just a plain gray circle. Added the real component's icon SVG.\n7. **Time** - both examples used a bare Unicode stopwatch character (`&#9201;`) instead of the real component's proper SVG clock icon, rendering small and thin. Replaced with the real `ICON_CLOCK`.\n8. **Text Area** - the control wrapper was missing its required size class (`--h40`), so it had zero padding - text sat flush against the box border instead of the intended 16px inset.\n9. **Add-on** - the \"Don't\" example's field was wide enough (280px) that the long \"Set the amount\" prefix never actually starved the input's width, undermining its own caption (\"steals the width the value needs\"). Scoped the Don't case to 220px so the input visibly cramps.\n10. **Tree Select** - all 5 carets/chevrons used a bare Unicode triangle (`&#9662;`) instead of the real component's SVG chevrons (which distinguish expanded/collapsed with down vs. right icons). Replaced with the real down-chevron SVG.\n11. **Upload** - both dropzones were missing the upload-cloud icon the real component always shows above \"Drop files or browse\". Added it.\n12. **Search** - both examples used the wrong Unicode character entirely (`&#9906;`, not even a magnifying glass) instead of the real component's SVG search icon. Replaced with the real icon.\n13. **Skeleton** - the \"Do\" example's avatar+text-lines layout collapsed to just the avatar circle - `.skeleton-demo-row` has no `flex-grow` of its own, so nested inside `.guide-dodont-demo`'s flex layout it shrank to fit its content instead of filling the available width, starving its child lines to 0px. Scoped `width:100%` fix.\n14. **Radio and Checkbox** - `is-checked` was a fully inert class in both components: every other state (hover/focus/disabled) has both a real `:checked`-sibling-selector rule *and* a class-based fallback for static illustrations with no real `<input>`, but `checked` was missing its fallback entirely, so radios/checkboxes marked `is-checked` in any Do/Don't example never visually showed as checked. Added the missing `.is-checked` class rules to `shell.css` **and** synced both components' own Copy Code emission (`radio-detail.js`, `checkbox-detail.js`), since this is a real gap in the reusable component CSS, not just the Do/Don't pages. Also added the missing inner dot/checkmark child elements the markup needed regardless.\n15. **Timeline** - both examples' dots had no `.timeline-demo-marker` wrapper and no `.timeline-demo-connector` at all, rendering as two disconnected floating dots instead of a connected timeline.\n16. **Tag** - the \"Don't\" example's deliberately-long sentence (meant to demonstrate text that \"forces the row to wrap\") was instead silently ellipsis-truncated to \"Last edited 3 days ago b...\" by the shared 150px truncation rule - the opposite of its own point. Let it wrap instead, scoped to the Do/Don't context.\n17. **Result** - both examples were missing the semantic status icon (error circle-X) the real component always shows above the title. Added it with the correct `--error` type class.\n18. **Toast** - both examples were missing the semantic status icon (checkmark for success, warning triangle) the real component always shows. Added both, matching each card's own type.\n\nEach fix was verified individually before moving to the next: measured the actual DOM (widths, colors, computed styles) to confirm the root cause, applied the fix, re-measured to confirm it resolved, then confirmed visually via screenshot - the same discipline used throughout this entire session. Ran the full 157-page HTTP-200 sweep, the tag-balance sweep, and `node --check` on every touched JS file after each fix and again at the end. All pass.\n\n**Also investigated and explicitly ruled out as non-bugs** (to avoid \"fixing\" things that already work): Group Button's apparently-blue selected segment (was a text-selection artifact in the user's own screenshot; measured and screenshotted clean red); Date Picker and Number (zero DOM issues found on either); List's single-line description truncation (a real, dedicated design rule for list rows, not an accidental collateral hit like Image/Tag's bugs); Checkbox/Menu/Avatar/Card/Button/etc.'s intentional truncated or empty states where the truncation *is* the illustration's point (Card's wrapped title, Checkbox's long double-negative label, Splitter's cramped panel, Tabs' overflowing row, Password's missing reveal icon, Avatar's overflowing initials)."}, {"title": "Global — topbar brand color swatch didn't say which color it was showing", "body": "Reported via screenshot as the topbar's color swatch (next to the theme toggle) not telling the user what color shade is currently active. Checked first: the swatch's own background color was already correct (`var(--red-500)`, which does track a saved custom brand color) - the actual gap was that its only affordance was a static, generic `title=\"Active brand color\"` tooltip with no actual value in it anywhere, on any of the 157 pages.\n\nAdded `updateBrandColorDotTitle()` to `shell.js`, called at the end of `applySavedColorsForTheme()` (which already runs on every page load and on every theme toggle) - reads the live computed `--red-500` value and sets the swatch's title to `Active brand color: #HEXVALUE`. Exposed it on `window` so `foundations.js`'s own Save button handler (the only place that applies a newly-saved brand color to the current page without a reload) can refresh the same tooltip immediately too, instead of it only picking up the change on the next page load.\n\nVerified: the tooltip correctly reads `Active brand color: #FF031A` with no saved override, and `Active brand color: #F28B82` after saving that exact pink from the user's own screenshot - checked via direct DOM inspection (`title` attribute), since native `title` tooltips render at the OS level and don't appear in a screenshot. Also verified the Foundations page's own Save-button flow updates the tooltip immediately, with zero console errors on either page. `node --check` passes on both `shell.js` and `foundations.js`, and the full 157-page HTTP-200 + tag-balance sweep passes."}, {"title": "Global — topbar theme toggle restyled as a plain icon button", "body": "Requested: a light/dark theme icon on the top navigation bar \"like fonts.google.com's,\" with an attached reference image of a plain sun icon. The system already had a theme toggle in the topbar (next to the Settings gear), and it already used this exact sun icon internally (plus a moon icon for dark) - just wrapped in a small track/thumb pill switch with a \"Dark\"/\"Light\" text label, which read as a different, less prominent control style than the reference. Confirmed the intended scope with the user before touching 157 files: restyle the existing toggle as a plain icon-only button matching `.icon-btn` (the Settings button's own style), not add a second/different toggle elsewhere.\n\nRewrote `.theme-toggle` in `shell.css` from a `display:flex; gap:8px` row (track + thumb + label) into the same 32x32 bordered box as `.icon-btn`, with a single `.theme-toggle-icon` child. Switched the icon from `background-image` (the old thumb's approach, which baked two different literal fill colors - `#15171A` and `#FFFFFF` - directly into the SVG data URIs since the thumb sat on two different colored backgrounds) to `mask-image` with `background-color: currentColor`, so the icon now inherits `.theme-toggle`'s own `color`/`:hover` color the same way the Settings gear's icon does, rather than needing separate hardcoded colors. Kept the exact same sun/moon SVG paths and the same current-theme-representing semantics (moon shown while dark is active, sun while light is active) - only the packaging changed, not the icons or the interaction logic. `shell.js`'s `applyTheme()` needed no changes - its `themeToggleLabel` lookup already guards with `if (label)`, so removing that element is a silent no-op.\n\nSince the toggle's markup was byte-for-byte identical on all 157 pages, updated it with a single scripted pass (regex matching the full `<button id=\"themeToggle\">...</button>` block) rather than 157 manual edits.\n\nVerified: clicking the toggle correctly flips `aria-pressed` and `data-theme` (tested light→dark) with zero console/page errors. Confirmed visually via screenshot in all 3 relevant states - default, hover (red border + brighter icon, matching the Settings button's own hover treatment), and the opposite theme - the toggle now reads as a clear, consistent icon button next to Settings instead of a separate pill-switch style. Confirmed zero remaining references to the old `.theme-toggle-track`/`-thumb`/`-label` classes anywhere in the codebase, and the full 157-page HTTP-200 + tag-balance sweep passes."}, {"title": "Global — replaced the \"← Back to X types\" arrow character with a real chevron icon", "body": "Follow-up: the `&larr;` Unicode arrow character read as too small/faint even after the spacing fix above, since it's just a text glyph at the link's own 12px font-size with no independent sizing control. Replaced it with this system's own established chevron icon style - the same stroke-based SVG pattern already used by Breadcrumb's separator (`viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"`), mirrored to point left and sized at a fixed 14x14 independent of the surrounding text.\n\nSame approach as the spacing fix - the `&larr;` markup was byte-for-byte identical inside every one of the 69 `.back-link-icon` spans, so replaced it with a single scripted pass rather than 69 manual edits. Left two unrelated `&larr;` usages alone (the Welcome modal's own \"Back\" button in `overview.html`, and an illustrative Do/Don't example caption in `principles.html`, about arrows rather than being one) - neither is an instance of the `.back-link` component the report was about.\n\nVerified: every sampled page (Table, Avatar, Card, Popover, Timeline) renders an identical 14x14 chevron, still with the exact 4px gap and vertical centering from the prior fix. Confirmed visually via screenshot - the chevron is now clearly visible and proportionate next to the label, instead of a small, faint arrow character. Confirmed zero remaining `&larr;` instances inside `.back-link-icon` via `grep`, and the full 157-page HTTP-200 + tag-balance sweep passes."}, {"title": "Global — the \"← Back to X types\" link's arrow had no real gap or alignment control", "body": "Reported via screenshot as a spacing/alignment/margin/padding/gutter issue on the back-arrow link, asked to be fixed globally across every page. Measured before touching anything: `.back-link` is `display:inline-flex; align-items:center; gap:4px` - clearly written assuming the arrow and label are two separate flex items - but the actual markup on all 69 detail pages was a single plain-text node, `&larr; Back to X types`, with nothing to apply that flex/gap to. `childNodes.length` measured 1, confirming it. The gap the user saw was just whatever a literal space character happens to render as in that font, with no real control over the arrow's vertical position relative to the label - exactly matching a report of inconsistent spacing and alignment.\n\nSince the pattern was 100% uniform across all 69 files (`<a class=\"back-link\" href=\"X\">&larr; Back to Y types</a>`, only the href/label text differing), fixed it with a single Python regex pass rather than 69 manual edits: wrapped the arrow in its own `<span class=\"back-link-icon\">&larr;</span>`, making it a real second flex item so `.back-link`'s existing `gap:4px`/`align-items:center` rules finally take effect. Added `.back-link-icon{ flex: none; line-height: 1; }` in `shell.css` so the glyph doesn't stretch or pad out the row's height.\n\nVerified: `childNodes.length` is now 2 on every sampled page, the icon-to-text gap measures exactly 4px (Table, Avatar, Card, Popover, Timeline, Statistic all checked individually), and the icon's vertical center matches the link's vertical center exactly - confirmed visually via screenshot. Confirmed zero remaining instances of the old `&larr; Back to` pattern via `grep`, and the full 157-page HTTP-200 + tag-balance sweep passes (covering all 69 touched files plus every untouched one)."}, {"title": "Avatar — new \"Show as: Avatars / Names only\" toggle for Avatar.Group", "body": "Requested feature: a \"user profile name\" section with a dropdown to choose whether the avatar graphic shows or not. Took two rounds of clarification to locate, since nothing by that name existed yet - landed on Avatar.Group (the overlapping-circle example for a list of people), which currently shows no names at all, only initials in circles.\n\nAdded a `name` field to each person in `GROUP_PEOPLE` (`avatar-detail.js`) and a new \"Show as\" dropdown (`avatar-group-view-select`) in `avatar-default.html`, next to the Avatar.Group example. Refactored the group-rendering code into `renderAvatarGroup(viewMode)`, wired to the select's `change` event:\n- **Avatars** (default): the existing overlapping-circle stack, unchanged, except the overflow tile's tooltip now shows full names (\"Morgan Diaz\") instead of initials (\"MD\").\n- **Names only**: a new vertical list of full names with no avatar circle at all, using the identical \"+N more\" overflow pattern so both modes represent the same underlying list.\n\nAdded `.avatar-demo-group-names`/`.avatar-demo-group-name-item` to `shell.css`, and updated the Copy Prompt's Avatar.Group description in `avatar-detail.js` to document the new property.\n\n**Found and fixed a real, pre-existing bug while verifying**: the overflow tile (\"+1\") rendered in the avatar's own red instead of its intended muted gray. `.avatar-demo-circle--overflow` and `.avatar-demo-circle--initials` are both single-class selectors of equal specificity, and `--initials` happens to be declared later in `shell.css`, so it silently won the cascade regardless of the overflow tile's own class - undermining the \"styled distinctly, not a real person\" intent already described in the Copy Prompt text. Fixed by raising `--overflow`'s specificity to a compound selector (`.avatar-demo-circle.avatar-demo-circle--overflow`), which now wins regardless of declaration order.\n\nVerified: switching the dropdown correctly re-renders between the two modes (confirmed the Names-only list text: \"Jordan Lee, Sam Ortiz, Priya Nair, Alex Kim, +1 more\"), the overflow tile now renders in the correct muted gray instead of red, and the Copy Prompt output includes \"Show as\"/\"Names only\". No console errors when exercising the Copy Prompt button (clipboard-stubbed). `node --check` passes on `avatar-detail.js` and the full 157-page HTTP-200 + tag-balance sweep passes."}, {"title": "Table — Email/Mobile underline moved to hover-only", "body": "Follow-up on the new columns: drop the permanent underline, show it only on hover as interaction feedback. This is exactly the pattern this system's own Link-type Button already uses (`.btn-demo--link:hover{ text-decoration: underline; }`, no underline at rest) - reused it directly rather than inventing a new one. Changed `.table-demo-link-cell` to `text-decoration: none` at rest with a `:hover{ text-decoration: underline; }` rule, in `shell.css` and synced into `table-detail.js`'s Copy Code emission.\n\nVerified: `text-decoration-line` computes `none` at rest and `underline` on `:hover` for the link cells. Confirmed visually via screenshot in both states - no underline on any cell until hovered, then only the hovered cell shows one, matching the requested \"gives feedback that the user is interacting with it\" behavior. `node --check` passes on `table-detail.js` and the full 157-page HTTP-200 + tag-balance sweep passes."}, {"title": "Table — added Email and Mobile columns", "body": "Requested addition: two new columns (Email, Mobile), rendered in this system's defined \"link\" color. Checked first rather than assuming blue - this system has no blue link token anywhere; the actual defined link color (confirmed via the Link-type Button's own Copy Code output, and reused by Toast's inline \"View\" action) is `var(--red-400)`. Asked the user directly whether to use that or a literal blue as requested; they confirmed `var(--red-400)`, staying consistent with the rest of the system.\n\nAdded `email`/`mobile` fields to `ROWS_DATA` in `table-detail.js`, extended the header row and every row-building branch (data rows, the Loading state's skeleton rows, and the Empty state's `colspan`, updated from 3 to 5) to include the two new columns, and rendered Email/Mobile as real `mailto:`/`tel:` links with a new `.table-demo-link-cell{ color: var(--red-400); text-decoration: underline; }` class - the same color + underline treatment already used by Toast's own inline link.\n\nGiven this session's repeated table-width-squeeze bug (Steps/Avatar/Badge/Calendar/Statistic's rowhead, and Table's own checkbox column earlier today), pre-emptively sized the table wide enough for the new columns instead of waiting for a wrap report: `.table-demo-table`'s fixed width grew from 320px to 630px (Email alone needs ~165px for \"jordan.lee@company.com\"), and `.table-demo-table--has-checkbox` grew from 360px to 670px, keeping the same +40px checkbox-column allowance on top of the new base width. Synced both into `shell.css` and `table-detail.js`'s Copy Code emission, and updated the stale \"Name / Status / Role example columns\" line in the Copy Prompt description.\n\nVerified: every row across all 5 states and both types measures a uniform 33px height (single-line, no wrapping) in a direct DOM check. Confirmed visually via screenshot - Email/Mobile render in red-400 with an underline, consistent with Toast's own link style. Also exercised the actual Copy Code and Copy Prompt buttons (clipboard-stubbed) to confirm the new columns, the `.table-demo-link-cell` rule, and `mailto:`/`tel:` links all appear in the copied output with no console errors. `node --check` passes on `table-detail.js` and the full 157-page HTTP-200 + tag-balance sweep passes."}, {"title": "Table — data cells were centered, only the header row was left-aligned", "body": "Follow-up report on the same component: Role (and every other data cell) still read as center-aligned. Measured directly: `.table-demo-table th` had an explicit `text-align: start`, but the matching `.table-demo-table td` rule never set `text-align` at all - it was silently inheriting `text-align: center` from the outer Live Preview matrix cell's shared default, so every row's Name/Status/Role text was centered while the column headers above them stayed correctly left-aligned.\n\nAdded `text-align: start` to `.table-demo-table td` in `shell.css`, synced into `table-detail.js`'s Copy Code emission.\n\nVerified: `td` now computes `text-align: start`, matching `th`. Confirmed visually via screenshot across every state (Default/Striped/Selected/Loading/Empty) and both types (Default/Bordered) - all cell text now lines up on the same left edge as its column header. Double-checked the Empty state's own centered icon/title/description block (which sets its own explicit `text-align: center`) still renders centered as intended, since an element's own explicit style always wins over an inherited one regardless of this change. `node --check` passes on `table-detail.js` and the full 157-page HTTP-200 + tag-balance sweep passes."}, {"title": "Table — Selected state's checkbox column squeezed Name/Role into wrapping", "body": "Reported as an alignment issue plus \"Jordan Lee\" wrapping onto two lines when it should stay on one. Measured before touching anything: `.table-demo-table` has a fixed `width:320px` used by every state, but the Selected state adds a leading checkbox column - without any extra width budget for it, that column's own ~40px had to come out of Name/Role's existing share instead. Name shrank from 98px to 85px and Role from 140px to 120px, both narrow enough that \"Jordan Lee\" and \"Product Designer\" wrapped onto a second line - which in turn made that row visibly taller than the others and threw off the checkbox's vertical alignment relative to the surrounding rows, matching the \"not aligned on the left-hand side\" part of the report.\n\nAdded a `table-demo-table--has-checkbox` modifier (applied only when the Selected state's checkbox column is present) that widens the table to 360px - exactly the checkbox column's own measured need - so Name/Role keep their normal widths instead of being squeezed. Wired the class in `table-detail.js`'s `buildTableField()` and added the matching rule to both `shell.css` and the Copy Code CSS emission.\n\nVerified: Name and Role are back to within 1-2px of their un-squeezed widths (97px and 138px, versus 98px/140px with no checkbox column), and \"Jordan Lee\"/\"Product Designer\" both render on one line in every Selected-state table across the matrix. Confirmed visually via screenshot - the checkbox now sits aligned with a single-line row, matching the unchecked rows below it. `node --check` passes on `table-detail.js` and the full 157-page HTTP-200 + tag-balance sweep passes."}, {"title": "Statistic — left-aligned content, trend moved below the value", "body": "Two follow-up requests on the same component: left-align the Title/Value text (previously centered), and move the trend indicator to its own line below the value instead of sitting inline next to it.\n\nFor the alignment: measured before assuming anything, since simply adding `text-align:start` without understanding *why* it looked centered could have missed the real cause. `.statistic-demo-title`/`.statistic-demo-value` share a `max-width:260px` truncation rule (meant for the Properties panel's narrower context) that capped their boxes at 260px even though the column itself measured 326-370px wide - the box was already left-anchored, but `text-align:center` (inherited from the matrix's shared default) was centering the text *within that narrower box*, landing it visibly off the true column center rather than looking genuinely centered or left-aligned. Added the same scoped override already used for Rating/Upload/Calendar: `[data-role=\"statistic-matrix-container\"] .button-matrix-colhead, .button-matrix-cell{ text-align: start; }` in `shell.css`.\n\nFor the trend: restructured `buildStatisticField()` in `statistic-detail.js` so the trend `<span>` is now a sibling of `.statistic-demo-value` inside `.statistic-demo-field`, instead of a flex child packed onto the same line inside `.statistic-demo-value`. `.statistic-demo-field`'s own `gap:6px` (already used for the title-to-value spacing) is now the single source of the value-to-trend spacing too - no new margin needed. Updated the stale Copy Prompt line that described the trend as \"next to the value\" to \"on its own line below the value,\" and fixed an outdated code comment left over from the earlier redundant-margin fix (which referenced the trend still being inside `.statistic-demo-value`).\n\nVerified visually via screenshot: \"Active Users\" and \"112,893\" now sit flush against the column's left edge (matching the column headers, which the same override also left-aligns) instead of looking off-center, and the trend arrow + percentage now render as a clear second line under the number in the \"With Trend\" column, reading like a real KPI card. `node --check` passes on `statistic-detail.js` and the full 157-page HTTP-200 + tag-balance sweep passes."}, {"title": "Statistic — two real bugs behind \"spacing, margin, padding, and alignment\"", "body": "Reported via screenshot as general spacing/margin/padding/alignment issues on the Statistic detail page. Measured every dimension before assuming anything - title-to-value gap, row heights, and vertical alignment all came back consistent and correct, so those weren't the problem. Found two separate, genuine bugs instead:\n\n1. **A redundant/doubled gap** - `.statistic-demo-value` is a flex row (`gap:4px`) holding the prefix, value text, suffix and trend badge as siblings, but `.statistic-demo-trend` also had its own `margin-inline-start:4px` - stacking on top of the parent's gap between the same two elements, measured at 8px instead of the intended 4px. Same redundant-spacing bug class as Timeline's Horizontal layout (item gap stacking with content's own padding) found earlier this session.\n2. **The same table auto-layout rowhead bug as Steps/Avatar/Badge/Calendar** - the sticky row-head column (\"Default\"/\"Positive\"/\"Negative\"/\"Loading\") measured 199.7px wide against a 1206px table, well beyond what any of these labels need. \"Negative\" is the longest at 80px content width plus the column's own 40px of side padding; 130px covers every label with room to spare.\n\nFixed both in `shell.css` (removed the trend's redundant margin; added scoped `table-layout:fixed` + explicit rowhead width) and synced the trend fix into `statistic-detail.js`'s Copy Code emission (the rowhead fix is generic matrix scaffolding, not component-specific, so it needs no JS-side change, same as Avatar/Badge/Calendar).\n\nVerified: the value-to-trend gap now measures exactly 4px (down from 8px), and the rowhead measures exactly 130px (down from 199.7px). Confirmed visually via screenshot - the trend arrow sits tight against the value, and the row labels no longer eat a large dead gutter. `node --check` passes on `statistic-detail.js` and the full 157-page HTTP-200 + tag-balance sweep passes."}, {"title": "Popover — bubble forced to a fixed 180px width regardless of content", "body": "Reported (still on the popover, called \"tooltip\" again) as \"a lot of spacing coming in the content.\" Measured before touching anything: `.popover-demo-bubble` had `min-width:180px`, but a short title like \"Storage usage\" only needs ~91px of actual text - leaving ~65px of dead blank space inside every bubble regardless of how little content it held. Tooltip never has this problem (no min-width at all, just `white-space:nowrap`, so it hugs its own content); Popover picked up the same min-width bug pattern independently.\n\nDropped `min-width:180px` from `.popover-demo-bubble` so it sizes to its own content, capped only by `max-width:220px`, in `shell.css` and synced `popover-detail.js`'s Copy Code emission.\n\nThat surfaced a second, latent issue while testing: the close button is absolutely positioned (so it reserves no layout space of its own) - with the bubble now shrinking tightly to content, a long title could run the close (x) button over. First attempt used `padding-right:20px` on the title when a close button is present, which looked right in computed styles but didn't actually work - `overflow:hidden`/`text-overflow:ellipsis` clip at a box's own outer edge, not its content edge, so truncated text can still render straight through a padding reservation. Switched to `.popover-demo-bubble:has(.popover-demo-close) .popover-demo-title{ max-width: calc(100% - 20px); }` instead, which actually shrinks the title's box (verified: title's rendered width dropped from 196px to 172px with a long test title, \"Cloud storage usage summary\"), so the ellipsis genuinely clips before reaching the button.\n\nVerified: blank space next to short content now measures exactly 0px (down from 65px) across all placements and states. Stress-tested with a long custom title typed into the page's own Title field - confirmed visually via screenshot that it truncates cleanly to \"Cloud storage usage su…\" with a real gap before the (x), no overlap, in the \"With close\" state specifically (the only one with a close button). `node --check` passes on `popover-detail.js` and the full 157-page HTTP-200 + tag-balance sweep passes."}, {"title": "Popover — same \"different column heights, one centered baseline\" bug as Skeleton/Card", "body": "Reported as an alignment issue with \"blank space over\" some cells in the Popover Live Preview matrix. Measured before touching anything: Top/Bottom placements stack the bubble and trigger vertically (~78px tall), while Left/Right placements sit side by side in a single short row (~36px tall) - `vertical-align:middle` (every matrix's default) centered each independently within the shared row height, so Left/Right ended up floating 37.5px below where Top/Bottom start, versus Top/Bottom's own 16.5px offset - the same bug class already found and fixed for Skeleton and Card.\n\nFixed with the identical scoped override: `[data-role=\"popover-matrix-container\"] .button-matrix-cell{ vertical-align: top; }` in `shell.css`. No JS sync needed - `.button-matrix-cell` is generic matrix scaffolding shared by every component, not something Popover's own Copy Code output emits (same reasoning as the Avatar/Badge/Skeleton/Card fixes).\n\nVerified: all 4 placements now measure the identical top offset (751.5px, previously 751.5px for Top/Bottom vs 772.5px for Left/Right). Confirmed visually via screenshot - every column now sits flush at the same top edge across all 3 content states. Full 157-page HTTP-200 + tag-balance sweep passes."}, {"title": "Popover — applied the same inverted-overlay fix as Tooltip/Tour/Pop Confirm", "body": "Reported as the popover bubble rendering with a plain white background in both themes, the same bug already fixed for Tooltip/Tour/Pop Confirm earlier this session. Confirmed directly: `.popover-demo-bubble` used `var(--graphite-900)` (the user-customizable token), so it just matched the page's own theme instead of always contrasting with it - a near-invisible white-on-white card in light theme.\n\nThe user also referenced Toast (\"the toaster\") as an example of this already being fixed - checked directly and that's not the case: Toast currently uses a plain theme-matching card (`var(--graphite-850)`), same pattern as Notification, and does not invert. Asked the user whether Toast should be converted too; they confirmed Popover only, since Toast and Notification are persistent/dismissible surfaces rather than momentary floating overlays like Tooltip - left both unchanged.\n\nApplied the identical treatment used for Tooltip/Tour/Pop Confirm: `.popover-demo-bubble` switched from `var(--graphite-900)`/`var(--text-hi)` to the fixed `var(--overlay-invert-bg)`/`var(--overlay-invert-text)` tokens, its border removed (box-shadow alone gives separation - a border reads as an unwanted outline against the inverted background, the same lesson from Tour/Pop Confirm's own fix), body/close-button color switched to a dimmed `color-mix()` of the invert tokens, and the arrow given the same half-hidden-diamond treatment (`z-index` + per-placement physical margins, bubble-side `-12px`/trigger-side `-4px`) so it reads as a clean triangular pointer instead of a floating square. `.popover-demo-trigger` was deliberately left alone - it sits on the ordinary page background outside the bubble, same as Tooltip's own trigger. Updated `shell.css` and synced `popover-detail.js`'s Copy Code emission and Copy Prompt description (which previously described the old bordered design as intentionally \"distinct from Tooltip's flat single-color bubble\" - rewritten to describe the current shared inversion technique).\n\nVerified visually in both themes (near-black bubble in light theme, near-white in dark theme, in all 3 content states x 4 placements) and proved robustness with the same adversarial test used for Tooltip - wrote deliberately wrong custom colors to `localStorage` before reload; the bubble still rendered the correct fixed color (`rgb(21, 23, 26)`), completely unaffected. `node --check` passes on `popover-detail.js` and the full 157-page HTTP-200 + tag-balance sweep passes."}, {"title": "Timeline — Horizontal layout had double the intended connector-to-heading gap", "body": "Reported as the Horizontal column having noticeably more space than Default/Alternate between the connector line, the heading, and the supporting text. Measured before touching anything: the gap between the connector's bottom and the content's top was 12px, plus `.timeline-demo-content`'s own deliberate `padding-top:10px` for this layout - 22px combined, versus the intended 10px.\n\nRoot cause: `.timeline-demo-item{ gap:12px }` is the base rule shared by every layout, sized for the vertical layouts where the item is a flex row and 12px is the *horizontal* space between the marker and content sitting side by side. The Horizontal layout flips the item to `flex-direction:column` (so marker stacks above content instead), which silently turned that same 12px into a second, unintended *vertical* gap - stacking directly on top of content's own already-deliberate 10px `padding-top`.\n\nFixed by adding `gap:0` to `.timeline-demo-list--horizontal .timeline-demo-item` in `shell.css`, overriding the base gap for this layout only, since content's `padding-top:10px` is already the real, intentional spacing here. Synced into `timeline-detail.js`'s Copy Code emission with a comment explaining why.\n\nVerified: the connector-to-content gap now measures exactly 0px (content's top sits flush against the marker's bottom), with content's own 10px padding-top providing the sole, correct gap to the heading - down from 22px combined. Confirmed visually via screenshot; Horizontal now reads with the same density as Default and Alternate. `node --check` passes and the full 157-page HTTP-200 + tag-balance sweep passes."}, {"title": "Timeline — the sixth missing-margin-reset bug this session, with a twist", "body": "Reported as two things in one screenshot: the gap between the heading and the supporting time text, and the dot still not lining up with the heading even after the Alternate-layout row fix above. Measured before touching anything, and both turned out to share one root cause - the same missing-margin-reset bug class found five times already this session (Alert, Notification, Result, Card, Empty), but with a wrinkle unique to Timeline: `.timeline-demo-title` and `.timeline-demo-time` are `<p>` tags with no margin reset, sitting inside `.timeline-demo-content` (a plain block, not a flex/grid container like the earlier cases).\n\nBecause `.timeline-demo-content` is itself a flex item (of the row's own flex/grid layout), it establishes its own formatting context, so title's default top margin (~13px, font-size-relative) couldn't collapse *out* through content's top edge the way it would in normal block flow - it stayed trapped inside, pushing the title text 13px below content's (and the row's, and the dot's) true top. That's why the dot looked misaligned with the heading even though the row-level top alignment fixed above was already correct - the row's top was right, but the title had invisible padding pushing it down from that point. Title's default bottom margin then collapsed with time's own explicit `margin-top:2px` (ordinary adjacent-sibling collapsing, since both are normal block children within content), inflating the intended 2px heading-to-time gap to 13px.\n\nFixed by adding `margin:0` to `.timeline-demo-title` and rewriting `.timeline-demo-time` as `margin:2px 0 0` (keeping the deliberate 2px top gap while zeroing everything else) in `shell.css`, synced into `timeline-detail.js`'s Copy Code emission.\n\nVerified: `titleTop` now exactly equals `markerTop`/`contentTop`/`dotTop` for every item in both the Default and Alternate layouts (was 13px off before), and the heading-to-time gap now measures a consistent 2px everywhere (was 13px). Confirmed visually via screenshot across all 3 state rows. `node --check` passes and the full 157-page HTTP-200 + tag-balance sweep passes."}, {"title": "Timeline — Alternate layout's left-side items landed on the wrong grid row", "body": "Reported via screenshot as alignment/spacing issues on the Timeline detail page - \"Payment confirmed\" (a left-side item in the Alternate layout) had its dot sitting well above its own title text, unlike every right-side item where the dot and title line up correctly. Measured before touching anything: the left item's `.timeline-demo-content` rendered 46px below its own grid row's top, while every right-side item measured 0px offset.\n\nRoot cause is a genuine CSS Grid auto-placement quirk, not a simple missing style: `.timeline-demo-item` is a 3-column grid (`1fr 16px 1fr`) with the marker always in DOM order before the content, pinned to the fixed center column (2). Content's column flips per item - column 3 for right-side items, column 1 for left-side items - but neither cell had an explicit `grid-row`. For right-side items, content's column (3) sits *ahead* of the marker's auto-placement cursor (which advanced to column 2), so the browser's sparse-packing algorithm keeps it on row 1. For left-side items, content's column (1) sits *behind* that cursor, and sparse auto-placement won't place an item backward within the same row - so it silently wrapped to row 2 instead, taking the whole item's visual height down with it. This only became obvious once a title wrapped to 2 lines (\"Payment confirmed\"), which is exactly the case in the screenshot.\n\nFixed by pinning both cells to `grid-row: 1` explicitly in `shell.css`, removing the ambiguity that let the browser guess wrong, and synced the same rule into the Copy Code emission in `timeline-detail.js` (with a comment explaining why it's required, not decorative). Also checked the Default and Horizontal layouts for the same class of bug while in there - both measured perfectly consistent (Default: an identical 16px dot-to-title offset on every item, which is the deliberate line-height difference between a 10px dot and 13px text, not a bug; Horizontal: dots and content exactly centered on the same X-axis, 0px diff) - no changes needed there.\n\nVerified: all 3 Alternate-layout items now measure 0px offset between their dot and their own content, confirmed visually via screenshot across all 3 state rows (Default/With events/Pending) - every dot now lines up exactly with its own title regardless of which side it's on or whether the title wraps. `node --check` passes on `timeline-detail.js` and the full 157-page HTTP-200 + tag-balance sweep passes."}, {"title": "Empty — the fifth missing-margin-reset bug this session", "body": "Reported via screenshot as an alignment issue between the heading text, button, and icon on the Empty detail page. Measured before touching anything: icon/title/description turned out to already be perfectly horizontally centered (`align-items:center` on `.empty-demo-panel` was working correctly, confirmed by comparing each element's own center X to the panel's) - the real problem was vertical, and it's the same missing-margin-reset bug found four times already this session (Alert, Notification, Result, Card). `.empty-demo-title` and `.empty-demo-description` are `<p>` tags with no margin reset, so the browser's default paragraph margins (14px/12px) stacked on top of `.empty-demo-panel`'s intentional `flex; gap:10px` - measured a 24px icon-to-title gap (should be 10px) and a 36px title-to-description gap (should be 10px).\n\nAdded `margin:0` to both in `shell.css` and the matching Copy Code emission in `empty-detail.js`.\n\nVerified: icon-to-title and title-to-description gaps now both measure exactly 10px, and description-to-button measures 14px (the 10px panel gap plus the button's own deliberate 4px margin-top, unchanged) - consistent across both types (Default, Simple) and all 3 content variants (Title only, With description, With action). Confirmed visually via screenshot. `node --check` passes on `empty-detail.js` and the full 157-page HTTP-200 + tag-balance sweep passes."}, {"title": "Card — the fourth missing-margin-reset bug this session", "body": "Reported via screenshot as a title/body spacing, margin, padding and alignment issue on the Card detail page. Measured before touching anything: `.card-demo-title` and `.card-demo-text` are `<p>` tags with no margin reset, so the browser's default paragraph margin (~14px/13px, font-size-relative) stacked on top of `.card-demo-body-wrap`'s intentional `flex; gap:6px`, producing a 33px gap between title and body text instead of 6px - the same bug already found and fixed for Alert, Notification and Result, just never caught on Card until now.\n\nAdded `margin:0` to both in `shell.css` and the matching Copy Code emission in `card-detail.js`.\n\nVerified: the title-to-text gap now measures exactly 6px (down from 33px), matching `.card-demo-body-wrap`'s intended gap. Confirmed visually via screenshot across all 3 types and all 5 states - spacing is now tight and consistent everywhere. `node --check` passes on `card-detail.js` and the full 157-page HTTP-200 + tag-balance sweep passes."}, {"title": "Card — two independent bugs behind \"spacing, margin, padding, and text alignment\"", "body": "Reported via screenshot as spacing/margin/padding/text-alignment issues on the Card detail page. Measured before touching anything and found two separate real bugs:\n\n1. **The same \"columns of very different height shouldn't share one centered baseline\" bug already fixed for Skeleton** - Default/With media/With actions cards measured 139px/219px/198px tall in the Live Preview matrix, and `vertical-align:middle` (every matrix's default) centered each independently within its row instead of anchoring all 3 at the same top edge - the Default and With actions cards visibly sat lower than With media, stair-stepping down the row exactly as shown in the screenshot.\n2. **A genuine footer-button truncation bug** - the \"With actions\" card's footer reuses Modal's `.modal-demo-footer`/`.modal-demo-btn` classes directly, inheriting Modal's 20px side padding (sized for Modal's much wider dialog). In Card's 240px-wide panel, \"Cancel\" + \"View details\" + the 8px gap + that padding left only ~8px of slack - thin enough that ordinary font-rendering variance (this reproduced with no viewport-width change involved) tipped the buttons' shared \"truncate at 150px\" rule (a `shell.css` selector list built for looser contexts like breadcrumbs and badges) into clipping real button text down to \"Can...\"/\"View deta...\".\n\nFixed both in `shell.css` (and synced the Copy Code emission in `card-detail.js`):\n- `[data-role=\"card-matrix-container\"] .button-matrix-cell{ vertical-align: top; }` - same scoped-override pattern as Skeleton, so only Card's own matrix is affected.\n- `.card-demo-footer{ padding: 12px 16px; }` (down from Modal's inherited 20px sides, freeing real margin) plus `.card-demo-footer .modal-demo-btn{ flex-shrink: 0; }` so the buttons can never shrink below their own text width again - worst case they'd overflow visibly, never silently truncate.\n\nVerified: all 3 card types now share one exact top edge (773.5px) in every state row instead of three different heights, and both footer buttons render their full text (\"Cancel\"/\"View details\") with `flex-shrink:0` guaranteeing that holds regardless of font metrics. Confirmed visually via screenshot across all 5 states (Rest/Hover/Selected/Disabled/Loading). `node --check` passes on `card-detail.js` and the full 157-page HTTP-200 + tag-balance sweep passes."}, {"title": "Calendar — two independent bugs behind \"spacing, alignment, margin, and padding\"", "body": "Reported via screenshot as a general spacing/alignment/margin/padding issue on the Calendar detail page. Measured before touching anything, and found two separate real bugs, same as several earlier rounds this session (Notification, Divider):\n\n1. **The same table auto-layout rowhead bug as Steps/Avatar/Badge** - the sticky row-head column (\"Default\"/\"With events\"/\"Range preview\") measured 238px wide against a 1206px table, handing the Full/Compact columns an inflated 544px/424px share neither actual calendar card came close to using. \"Range preview\" is the longest label at 119px content width plus the column's own 40px of side padding; 170px covers every label with room to spare.\n2. **The same text-align-doesn't-center-a-block-element bug as Rating/Upload/Steps/Divider** - `.calendar-demo-grid` is a fixed-width (`width:fit-content`), `display:block` box with no margin, so the matrix cell's `text-align:center` (which only affects inline content) never centered it; it sat flush against the cell's own left padding instead, leaving the calendar visibly shoved to one side with a large blank margin on the right - measured 242px/192px of dead space to the right of the Full/Compact cards respectively, which is what the screenshot's highlighted gap actually was.\n\nFixed both in `shell.css`:\n```css\n[data-role=\"calendar-matrix-container\"] .button-matrix{ table-layout: fixed; }\n[data-role=\"calendar-matrix-container\"] .button-matrix-rowhead{ width: 170px; }\n.calendar-demo-grid{ /* ...existing rules... */ margin-inline: auto; }\n```\nand synced the matching Copy Code emission in `calendar-detail.js` (`.calendar-demo-grid` gets `margin-inline:auto` there too; the rowhead-width fix is generic matrix scaffolding, not component-specific, so it needs no JS-side change, same as Avatar/Badge).\n\nVerified: rowhead now measures exactly 170px (down from 238px), both calendar cards sit centered in their columns with equal left/right margin (116px/116px for Full, 151px/151px for Compact) instead of flush-left with a lopsided gap, and all 3 rows (Default, With events, Range preview) now look visually consistent. Confirmed via screenshot. `node --check` passes on `calendar-detail.js`, `calendar.html` (the listing page) doesn't use the matrix pattern, and the full 157-page HTTP-200 + tag-balance sweep passes."}, {"title": "Badge — same blank-gap bug as Avatar", "body": "Reported by the user as the same issue on Badge's detail page. Measured before touching anything, same discipline as every round this session: the sticky row-head column (\"Default\"/\"Success\"/\"Processing\"/\"Warning\"/\"Error\") had been handed 415px of a 1206px table by the browser's default `table-layout:auto`, squeezing Count/Dot/Status into the remainder and leaving the same dead gap the user reported for Avatar.\n\nMeasured the true minimum content need the same way (temporary `table-layout:fixed; width:1px` on the rowhead): the longest label, \"Processing,\" needs 95px plus the column's existing 40px of side padding, so 135px minimum. Used 150px for a comfortable buffer, scoped to Badge's own matrix only in `shell.css`:\n```css\n[data-role=\"badge-matrix-container\"] .button-matrix{ table-layout: fixed; }\n[data-role=\"badge-matrix-container\"] .button-matrix-rowhead{ width: 150px; }\n```\n\nVerified: rowhead now measures exactly 150px (down from 415px), and Count/Dot/Status split the remaining space evenly at 352px each. Confirmed visually via screenshot - no gap between the row labels and Count. No JS files touched (same reasoning as Avatar), `badge.html` (the listing page) doesn't use the matrix pattern either, and the full 157-page HTTP-200 + tag-balance sweep passes."}, {"title": "Avatar — large blank gap in the Live Preview matrix", "body": "Reported via screenshot (a highlighted blank rectangle between the row labels and the \"Initials\" column) as \"spacing coming on the screen.\" Recognized immediately as the same root cause already found and fixed for Steps earlier in this session: every matrix in this file shares `.button-matrix` with the browser's default `table-layout:auto`, which distributes column width by its own content heuristics rather than by what each column actually needs. Measured the live table before touching anything - the sticky `.button-matrix-rowhead` column (holding just \"Default\"/\"Online\"/\"Away\"/\"Offline\") had been handed 369px of a 1206px-wide table, squeezing the 3 real content columns (Initials/Icon/Image) into whatever was left and creating the large dead gap the user highlighted.\n\nTemporarily forced `table-layout:fixed; width:1px` on the rowhead to measure the column's true minimum content need (68px `scrollWidth` for \"Default\" plus the existing 40px of horizontal padding), then scoped a permanent fix to `shell.css`:\n```css\n[data-role=\"avatar-matrix-container\"] .button-matrix{ table-layout: fixed; }\n[data-role=\"avatar-matrix-container\"] .button-matrix-rowhead{ width: 140px; }\n```\n140px comfortably fits every state label with room to spare, matching the buffer used for Steps' own 150px fix. Scoped to Avatar's own matrix only, so every other component's matrix keeps its default proportional-to-content split.\n\nVerified: rowhead now measures exactly 140px (down from 369px), and the 3 data columns split the remaining space evenly at ~355px each instead of being squeezed unevenly. Confirmed visually via screenshot - Initials/Icon/Image now sit directly after the row labels with no gap, consistent across all 4 states. No JS files were touched (Avatar's Copy Code output doesn't emit `.button-matrix` structural CSS - that's generic scaffolding shared by every component, not Avatar-specific), and `avatar.html` (the listing page) doesn't use the matrix pattern at all, so there was nothing to check there. Full 157-page HTTP-200 + tag-balance sweep passes."}, {"title": "Divider — removed the Vertical form", "body": "Removed the \"Vertical\" divider form (the short inline rule shown between \"Settings | Logout\") entirely, at request - it was a full third TYPES entry, not just a static card, so removal touched:\n- **`divider.html`** — deleted its listing card (`data-role=\"copy-prompt/code-divider-vertical\"` buttons and all); the 2-column `.system-card-grid` already tolerates a single remaining card (same pattern used for Modal's own type removal earlier).\n- **`divider-detail.js`** — removed it from `TYPES`, the `buildDividerField()` branch that rendered the \"Settings | Logout\" example, the Copy Code CSS emission for `.divider-demo-vline` and its size-scaling rules, the \"Forms (3)\"/\"3 forms\" Copy Prompt text (now \"Forms (2)\"), the Size property's Vertical-specific description clause, the `<!-- Example usage -->` line that instantiated it, and the second gallery card's entire click-wiring block.\n- **`divider-default.html`** — removed the stale \"Use the vertical orientation for inline separators...\" Best Practices bullet (and its Machine View JSON twin), and updated 3 \"horizontally or vertically\" descriptions (the page lede, the guide body text, and the JSON summary) to \"with a horizontal rule,\" since the component genuinely no longer has a vertical form to describe.\n- **`shell.css`** — removed the entire `.divider-demo-vline`/`.divider-demo-inline-context` rule block, including the vertical-only entries in the Size (3) scaling rules.\n\nVerified zero remaining references to `divider-demo-vline`/`divider-demo-inline-context` anywhere in the repo, the listing page shows exactly 1 card with no half-empty grid cell, the Live Preview matrix shows exactly the 2 remaining column headers, Copy Prompt/Copy Code output is clean of any \"vertical\" mention, no console errors on either page, and the full 157-page HTTP-200 + tag-balance sweep passes."}, {"title": "Divider — heading vs. content alignment mismatch", "body": "Reported via screenshot (a Divider matrix, with the column-header row highlighted) as a \"heading alignment issue.\" Checked the header row directly first - background, position, and jump-target state were all exactly as expected, so the headers themselves were never the problem. The real mismatch was one column down: `.divider-demo-line` (the plain \"Horizontal\" form) and `.divider-demo-line-text` (the \"Horizontal with text\" form's \"___ OR ___\" pattern) are both fixed-width `<div>`s - block-level, so the `text-align:center` every matrix cell sets doesn't center them the way it centers inline content (the same text-align-vs-block-positioning distinction behind the earlier Rating/Upload/Steps alignment bugs this session). Left unset, each sat flush against its column's left edge while the centered header text floated above the middle of the same column, reading as the heading not lining up with its own content. The Vertical form (\"Settings | Logout\") never had this problem, since `.divider-demo-vline` is already `display:inline-block` - an inline-level box, correctly centered by the ancestor's text-align all along.\n\nAdded `margin-inline:auto` to both in `shell.css` and the matching Copy Code emission in `divider-detail.js`. Verified the Live Preview matrix (all 3 forms now centered consistently under their headers, matching Vertical's already-correct look), the listing card, and the Do/Don't guide demo - no regression in either of the latter two."}, {"title": "Skeleton — inconsistent vertical alignment across layouts", "body": "Reported as \"not properly aligned,\" referring to the Live Preview matrix where Text, Avatar+text, and Card render side by side. Root cause was different from every other alignment bug this session: not a missing margin reset, but the shared `.button-matrix-cell{ vertical-align:middle }` every matrix in this file relies on, which works fine when a row's columns are similar heights but actively hurts here - Skeleton's 3 layouts have wildly different natural heights (a tall image block vs. a single compact avatar+line row), so the shortest one (Avatar+text) ended up mathematically centered in a row sized for the tallest (Card), floating well below where Text and Card both visually start. Confirmed this by measuring computed positions first: all three were in fact perfectly centered on the same Y-coordinate - the \"misalignment\" was real, just not a coordinate bug, a design-intent one: skeletons represent content loading in from the top, so all three should anchor there consistently regardless of shared row height, not center independently.\n\nScoped `vertical-align: top` to `[data-role=\"skeleton-matrix-container\"] .button-matrix-cell` in `shell.css` - the same scoping pattern already used for Rating's and Upload's own `text-align` overrides, so every other component's matrix (where columns are usually similar heights and centering is correct) is untouched. Verified visually: all 3 layouts now start at the same top edge in both the Animated and Reduced-motion rows."}, {"title": "Result — the same missing-margin bug, a fourth time", "body": "Reported as \"space inconsistency\" - measured before touching anything, same as every prior round: `.result-demo-title` and `.result-demo-description` are `<p>` tags with no margin reset, so each kept its own default browser paragraph margin stacked on top of `.result-demo-panel`'s intentional `gap:8px`. Icon-to-title measured 28px, title-to-description 37px, description-to-actions 33px - three different numbers where the design called for one consistent baseline (this is the same underlying bug as Alert and Notification, just never caught here since this component hadn't been reported on yet).\n\nAdded `margin:0` to both in `shell.css` and the matching Copy Code emission in `result-detail.js`. Verified: icon-to-title now measures 12px (the 8px gap plus the icon's own deliberate 4px margin-bottom), title-to-description a clean 8px, description-to-actions 20px (8px gap plus the actions row's own deliberate 12px margin-top) - consistent across all 4 semantic types and all 3 states (Default, With secondary, Compact) in one screenshot. (Also noticed the \"Done\" button renders blue instead of its coded `var(--red-500)` in a browser carrying a saved custom brand color - confirmed this is correct, expected behavior here, unlike Pop Confirm's bug above: Result's primary CTA is meant to follow the brand-customizable accent, not a fixed danger token.)"}, {"title": "Pop Confirm — Destructive CTA used the wrong red token", "body": "Reported as inconsistent CTA colors (some blue, some white) - a fresh check showed correct gray/red with no customization saved, which pointed at the user's own browser carrying a saved customization mine didn't have. Found the real bug: `.popconfirm-demo-btn--confirm.is-destructive` used `var(--red-500)` - the brand-customizable primary accent (set on Foundations) - instead of `var(--danger-500)`, the fixed token dedicated to destructive/error semantics. Confirmed this against the established convention elsewhere in the same codebase: Modal's own Destructive type explicitly documents \"the primary action button uses the fixed danger-red token,\" and Button's destructive variant references `--danger-500` too - Pop Confirm's had drifted onto the wrong one. Any custom brand color (blue, in the reported case) would correctly leave every other destructive CTA in the system red, while this one alone shifted color - reading as a random inconsistency rather than what it was.\n\nFixed in `shell.css` and the matching Copy Code emission in `pop-confirm-detail.js`. Verified two ways: computed color is `rgb(255,3,26)` (real red) with no customization, and - the test that actually matters here, since the bug was invisible without one - stayed exactly that same red after saving a simulated custom brand color (blue) to `localStorage`, proving the destructive button no longer reacts to brand customization at all."}, {"title": "Drawer — dead space at the bottom of the panel", "body": "Reported via screenshot: a large blank gap between the footer and the bottom edge of Right/Left placement panels. Different root cause from the earlier Top/Bottom footer-clipping bug (that was too little height; this was too much, going unused): Right/Left panels are pinned to `height:100%` of the 260px scrim, but the Live Preview's body reuses Modal's own `.modal-demo-body` - which, unlike the real code recipe's `.drawer-body` (already `flex:1`), never grows to fill a taller container, since Modal's own dialog always sizes to its content and never needs to. Measured the header+body+footer's own natural height at ~179px against the panel's fixed 228px - a 49px gap that just sat dead below the footer instead of the footer sticking to the bottom the way a real drawer's footer should.\n\nFixed by scoping `.drawer-demo-panel .modal-demo-body{ flex:1 1 auto; overflow:auto; }` in `shell.css` - specific to a modal-body nested inside a drawer panel, so Modal's own (intentionally auto-height) dialogs elsewhere are untouched. No JS changes needed - the real Copy Code recipe (`drawer-detail.js`) already had `flex:1` on its own `.drawer-body` from the start; only the Live Preview's reused markup was missing it. Verified all 12 combinations (4 placements × 3 states, including \"No footer\" - confirming the body itself grows to absorb the space rather than a gap reappearing after a shorter footerless state): trailing gap dropped from 49px to ~1px everywhere."}, {"title": "Tour and Pop Confirm — removed an unwanted outline/stroke", "body": "Reported as an outline/stroke visible on \"the tooltip\" - turned out to be Tour's card and Pop Confirm's bubble, not Tooltip itself (confirmed Tooltip has no border anywhere, computed and visually, before looking elsewhere). Both had picked up a `border: 1px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--overlay-invert-text) 20%, transparent)` as part of their earlier color-inversion fix - a deliberate choice at the time to keep the card readable against its own new inverted background, but in practice it read as an unwanted outline around the whole card rather than a deliberate edge; Tooltip's own bubble never had a border at all and looks fine without one, box-shadow alone providing enough separation from the page.\n\nRemoved the border from `.tour-demo-card`, `.tour-demo-arrow`, `.popconfirm-demo-bubble`, and `.popconfirm-demo-arrow`, in both `shell.css` and the matching Copy Code emission in `tour-detail.js`/`pop-confirm-detail.js`. Left the ghost/Cancel buttons' own borders untouched - those still need to read as buttons, unlike the card/bubble's own outer edge. While in there, also fixed a Copy Prompt text description in `tour-detail.js` that had never been updated with the original color-inversion fix at all (still described the pre-fix `var(--graphite-900)` background and border verbatim), and updated Pop Confirm's own Copy Prompt text to match the border removal. Verified both computed to `0px none` and confirmed visually via zoomed screenshots - clean cards with shadow-only elevation, no visible edge."}, {"title": "Pop Confirm — applied the same inverted-overlay color fix", "body": "Requested by reference (\"fix the issues you've previously done, but for this screen\"): Pop Confirm's bubble still used the plain, user-customizable `var(--graphite-900)`/`var(--text-hi)` pair, same gap Tour had before its own color fix. Applied the identical `--overlay-invert-bg`/`--overlay-invert-text` treatment to `.popconfirm-demo-bubble`, `.popconfirm-demo-question`, and the arrow, in both `shell.css` and the matching Copy Code/Copy Prompt output in `pop-confirm-detail.js`.\n\nSame two exceptions as Tour, for the same reasons:\n- `.popconfirm-demo-trigger` sits on the ordinary page background outside the bubble (like Tooltip's trigger, Tour's target) and correctly keeps its normal, non-inverted `var(--text-hi)` - left untouched.\n- `.popconfirm-demo-btn--cancel`'s border/text were rebuilt from `color-mix(in srgb, var(--overlay-invert-text) N%, transparent)` instead of `var(--line-strong)`/`var(--text-hi)`, which would have rendered near-invisible (near-black-on-near-black in light theme) against the new inverted bubble. `--confirm` (both the neutral and destructive variants) needed no change - both already carry their own fully opaque background, so they stay legible regardless of what the bubble around them inverts to.\n\nVerified computed colors in both themes (light: bubble `rgb(21,23,26)` / Cancel text `rgb(255,255,255)`; dark: bubble `rgb(243,242,239)` / Cancel text `rgb(21,23,26)`), confirmed the listing card in `pop-confirm.html` picked up the fix automatically, and re-ran the same adversarial `localStorage` test used to catch Tooltip's original bug (deliberately wrong custom \"Text Hi\"/\"Background Secondary\" colors saved for both themes) - the bubble still rendered correctly inverted, confirming this fix doesn't depend on what's saved there either."}, {"title": "Pop Confirm — same arrow fix as Tooltip/Tour", "body": "Reported by reference to the Tooltip fix: Pop Confirm's trigger/arrow/bubble structure is another near-exact structural clone (same DOM order - bubble/arrow/trigger - the same per-placement flex-direction trick, the same half-cancelled `margin:-4px` on a `gap:8px` wrap, even the same border/box-shadow-matching-the-bubble arrow styling Tour has), so the same diagnosis and fix applied directly, in both `shell.css` and the matching Copy Code emission in `pop-confirm-detail.js`:\n- `z-index` so the bubble paints above the arrow (DOM order alone would otherwise paint the arrow over the bubble).\n- Bubble-side margin `-4px` → `-12px` per placement (pulling the arrow's center, not just its tip, onto the bubble's edge - half hidden, half a clean triangular point); trigger-side margin left at the original `-4px`.\n- The listing card in `pop-confirm.html` shares the same CSS classes, so it picked up the fix automatically.\n\nVerified all 4 placements: bubble-side gap measures -5.66px (the arrow's bounding-box half-height, confirming its center sits exactly on the bubble's edge) with the trigger-side gap unchanged at the original 2.34px - identical to Tooltip's and Tour's own verified numbers."}, {"title": "Notification — two real bugs behind \"spacing and alignment\"", "body": "Measured every gap directly rather than guessing, and found two independent issues:\n\n- **Description-to-action gap was 20px, not the intended 8px.** `.notification-demo-title` and `.notification-demo-description` are both `<p>` tags; title resets its own margin fully (`margin:0`), but description only ever overrode `margin-top`, leaving its default bottom margin (~1em of its own 12px font) still active. That stray margin stacked with `.notification-demo-action`'s own `margin-top:8px` into a real ~20px gap - nearly 3x the deliberate 2px gap between title and description right above it, and it was inflating every card's height by ~12px even in states with no action link at all, not just the \"With action\" state. Added `margin-bottom:0`.\n- **The close (×) button was completely invisible.** `.notification-demo-close` had no explicit width/height, and its SVG icon has no intrinsic size either (relies on `viewBox` alone) - so the button collapsed to `0×0` and the icon simply never rendered, on every single card, in every state. This wasn't a spacing tweak so much as a missing control entirely. Confirmed by checking computed style directly (`width: 0px`) rather than assuming from the screenshot. Fixed by giving the button an explicit `16px × 16px` box - the same pattern already working correctly on `.alert-demo-close` - which is what lets a sizeless SVG child fill to match.\n\nBoth fixed in `shell.css` and the matching Copy Code emission in `notification-detail.js`. Verified: gap before \"Undo\" now measures exactly 8px, card height dropped by the stray ~12-24px it no longer needs, and the × button now renders and sizes correctly (computed `width: 16px`) across every type/state combination in the Live Preview matrix."}, {"title": "Drawer — all 4 corners now round", "body": "Reported as \"corners are not rounded.\" Investigated first rather than assuming: the code was deliberately rounding only the panel's 2 *inner* corners (the edge flush against the screen stayed square, matching a real edge-anchored drawer) - this was already documented in a code comment and confirmed working exactly as written for all 4 placements. Asked the user to confirm which they wanted rather than guess; they confirmed they want all 4 corners rounded, overriding that original design.\n\nSimplified `innerRadiusStyleFor()` (renamed `radiusStyleFor()`, since \"inner\" no longer means anything) to a flat `border-radius`, replacing the per-placement two-corner logic. Found and fixed the same inner-corner logic duplicated in 3 other places that would otherwise have kept the old look inconsistently: the Copy Code CSS emission (4 placement-specific rules collapsed into one shared `.drawer-panel--radius-X`), the Copy Code HTML example generator (had its own separate, and actually incomplete, version of the same logic - it only special-cased Left vs \"everything else,\" silently giving Top and Bottom the wrong corners), and a hardcoded inline style on the static listing card in `drawer.html`. Updated the now-inaccurate \"inner edge only\" copy in the Properties panel tooltip and the Copy Prompt text output. Verified computed `border-radius` on all four corners of all four placements now matches the chosen value."}, {"title": "Drawer — Top/Bottom footer clipped at Small/Medium size", "body": "Reported via screenshot: the \"Cancel\"/\"Save\" footer buttons were visibly cut off mid-button on the Drawer detail page's Live Preview, specifically for the Top and Bottom placements. Measured the real cause directly (`scrollHeight` vs assigned `height`) rather than guessing: `.drawer-demo-panel--top`/`--bottom` used a flat `height` per size (120px Small, 170px Medium, 220px Large) that was never validated against what the actual header + 2-line body + footer content needs - measured 178px at Small (58px short) and 195px at Medium (25px short); only Large (needs 218px against its 220px) happened to fit, which is why the bug wasn't visible there. Combined with the panel's `overflow:hidden`, the shortfall silently clipped the footer clean off rather than showing any visible warning.\n\nFixed by switching `height` to `min-height` (180px/200px/220px, each now comfortably covering the measured content) in both `shell.css` and the matching Copy Code emission in `drawer-detail.js` (a parallel `.drawer-panel` recipe using the same real padding/font-size values, confirmed to match the Live Preview's measurements exactly). `min-height` instead of a flat value means Size still sets a visible floor (so the \"No footer\" state, which has less content, still looks different at each size) while the panel can never again clip content taller than its guessed size - the header/body/footer set the real floor from here on, not a guess. Right/Left placements were never affected (their `height:100%` always had the full 260px scrim to work with) and were left untouched. Verified all three sizes across all three states (Default/No footer/No close) - `scrollHeight` no longer exceeds the assigned height anywhere, and the full Live Preview matrix screenshot shows every footer fully visible with no clipping."}, {"title": "Tour — applied the same inverted-overlay color fix as Tooltip", "body": "Requested by reference: Tour's card still used `var(--graphite-900)` for its background - in light theme that resolves to pure white (`#ffffff`), so the card looked like a plain white box instead of matching Tooltip's now-inverted look. Applied the exact same `--overlay-invert-bg`/`--overlay-invert-text` tokens (see the Tooltip entries above for why not `--graphite-900`/`--text-hi`) to `.tour-demo-card`, `.tour-demo-title`, `.tour-demo-description`, `.tour-demo-step-counter`, and the arrow, in both `shell.css` and the matching Copy Code emission in `tour-detail.js`.\n\nTwo things needed extra care that Tooltip's simpler bubble didn't:\n- **`.tour-demo-target`** (the red-outlined \"New feature\" pill) sits on the ordinary page background *outside* the card - like Tooltip's own trigger circle, it correctly keeps the normal, non-inverted `var(--text-hi)`, and was left untouched.\n- **The card's border and the `.tour-demo-btn--ghost` (Back) button's border/text** used `var(--line-strong)`/`var(--text-hi)` - tokens calibrated for this system's *normal* surfaces. Against the new inverted black-in-light-theme card, a `var(--text-hi)` ghost-button label would have rendered near-black-on-near-black (invisible), and `var(--line-strong)`'s dark-theme tint would have nearly vanished against the black card. Rebuilt both from `color-mix(in srgb, var(--overlay-invert-text) N%, transparent)` instead, so they scale correctly with whichever color the card itself ends up inverted to.\n\nVerified computed colors in both themes (light: card `rgb(21,23,26)` / ghost-button text `rgb(255,255,255)`; dark: card `rgb(243,242,239)` / ghost-button text `rgb(21,23,26)`) and confirmed the listing card in `tour.html` (which shares the same classes) picked up the fix automatically - it was the plain-white card the user's screenshot was pointing at."}, {"title": "Tooltip color fix, round 2 - the real root cause", "body": "The user reported the same \"tooltip needs to be dark in light mode / light in dark mode\" issue twice more after the fix above, despite it verifying correct on every fresh check. Root cause: `var(--text-hi)` and `var(--graphite-900)` - the pair the first fix reused for the inversion, because their *default* values happen to already form an inverted near-black/near-white pair - are both wired into this site's real, working per-theme brand color customization (`shell.js`'s `applySavedColorsForTheme`, driven by `localStorage[\"ads:text-colors\"]`/`[\"ads:bg-colors\"]`, saved from Foundations). A custom \"Text Hi\" or \"Background Secondary\" saved for either theme silently overrides those variables and breaks the inversion this component depends on - invisible to any fresh/clean browser check (which is exactly why my Playwright verification kept passing), but very real in an actual browser carrying saved customizations.\n\nFix: added two new tokens to `theme.css`, `--overlay-invert-bg`/`--overlay-invert-text`, holding the same default values as before but declared independently of the brand customization system - `applySavedColorsForTheme` never touches them. Switched `.tooltip-demo-bubble`, `.tooltip-demo-description`, and `.tooltip-demo-arrow` (in `shell.css` and the matching Copy Code/Copy Prompt emission in `tooltip-detail.js`) to the new tokens. Also caught and fixed a real leftover bug while in there: the Copy Prompt text still described the pre-fix colors (`Background var(--graphite-700), color var(--text-hi)`) from before the very first color fix - never updated at the time.\n\nVerified with an adversarial test a clean check can't catch: saved deliberately-wrong custom colors to `localStorage` (a light-theme \"Text Hi\" of light gray, a dark-theme one of near-black, plus unrelated magenta/green \"Background Secondary\" values) and reloaded - the tooltip still rendered correctly inverted in both themes, proving the fix no longer depends on what's saved there at all."}, {"title": "Tour — applied the same arrow fix as Tooltip", "body": "Requested by reference to the Tooltip fix above: Tour's target/arrow/card structure is a near-exact structural clone of Tooltip's trigger/arrow/bubble (same DOM order - card/arrow/target - the same per-placement flex-direction trick, the same half-cancelled `margin:-4px` on a `gap:8px` wrap), so the same diagnosis and fix applied directly:\n- Added `z-index` so the card paints above the arrow (again, DOM order alone would otherwise paint the arrow over the card).\n- Card-side margin `-4px` → `-12px` per placement (pulling the arrow's center, not just its tip, onto the card's edge - half hidden, half a clean triangular point); target-side margin left at the original `-4px`.\n- Fixed in `shell.css` and the matching Copy Code emission in `tour-detail.js`; also brought Copy Code's `.tour-demo-arrow`/`.tour-demo-card` output back in sync with the live CSS along the way - it was missing the arrow's `border`/`box-shadow` and the card's now-needed `position:relative`.\n- The listing card in `tour.html` shares the same CSS classes, so it picked up the fix automatically.\n\nVerified all 4 placements: card-side gap measures -5.66px (the arrow's bounding-box half-height, confirming its center - not just its tip - sits exactly on the card's edge) with the target-side gap unchanged at the original 2.34px, matching Tooltip's own verified numbers exactly."}, {"title": "Tooltip — arrow/bubble gap and inverted background", "body": "Two issues reported together, both fixed in `shell.css` and the matching Copy Code emission in `tooltip-detail.js`:\n\n- **Arrow not merging with the bubble.** `.tooltip-demo-wrap`'s `gap:8px` applied on both sides of the arrow (bubble side and trigger side), and the arrow's old symmetric `margin:-4px` only cancelled half of that, leaving a real ~2px gap on both sides - measured, not eyeballed. DOM order is always bubble → arrow → trigger regardless of placement (only `flex-direction` changes which side is visually which), so closing just the bubble-side gap needed a different physical margin per placement (which side is \"toward the bubble\" swaps between top/bottom and between left/right). Set that side to `-7px` (fully cancels the gap, small intentional overlap) while leaving the trigger-side `-4px` untouched, preserving the original trigger-to-arrow spacing exactly. Verified all 4 placements now overlap by ~0.66px (merged) on the bubble side while keeping the original ~2.34px gap on the trigger side.\n- **Background not inverted.** The tooltip used `--graphite-700`, the same non-inverted surface scale as normal UI elements (lightens in light theme, darkens in dark theme, same direction as the page) - so it never popped as an overlay the way a real tooltip should. Switched to `background:var(--text-hi)` (which already holds the correct inverted value in both themes - near-black on light, near-white on dark) with `color:var(--graphite-900)` for legible inverted text, no new literal colors needed. The description's dimmer text used to reference `--text-mid`, calibrated for normal surfaces and too low-contrast against the new inverted background, so it now mixes toward the bubble's own background instead (`color-mix(in srgb, var(--graphite-900) 65%, var(--text-hi) 35%)`). Verified computed colors in both themes (light: `rgb(21,23,26)` bg / white text; dark: `rgb(243,242,239)` bg / near-black text) and confirmed the listing card (which shares the same CSS classes) picked up both fixes automatically with no separate edit needed.\n\n**Follow-up: half the arrow needed to disappear.** The bubble-touching fix above still left the arrow as a complete floating diamond sitting *below* the bubble's edge - correct in the sense that it touched, but not what a real tooltip arrow looks like (a triangular point half-swallowed by the bubble, not a whole rotated square resting beside it). Two more things were needed: `z-index` so the bubble paints above the arrow (flex items paint in DOM order by default, and arrow comes after bubble in markup, so without this the arrow painted *over* the bubble rather than being hidden behind it), and pulling the arrow's bubble-side margin in further (`-7px` → `-12px`) so its *center*, not just its tip, lands on the bubble's edge - putting exactly half the diamond behind the now-opaque bubble. Trigger-side margin was untouched. Verified all 4 placements now show a clean triangular point with no visible square edge, in both themes, on both the Live Preview matrix and the listing card."}, {"title": "Modal — removed the Prompt type", "body": "Removed the \"Prompt\" modal type (the amber SLDS-style warning dialog - centered icon+title header, no close \"x\", centered footer) entirely, at request. It was a fully-integrated 3rd type (Default/Destructive/Prompt), not just a static card, so removal touched:\n- **`modal.html`** — deleted its listing card (`data-type=\"prompt\"`, its own Copy prompt/Copy code buttons); the 2-column `.system-card-grid` already tolerates a single card (same pattern Tabs uses), so no layout fix was needed.\n- **`modal-detail.js`** — removed it from `TYPES`/`FULL_SPEC`, the amber-color branch in `liveCssFor()`, its 5-line Copy Code CSS emission, the prompt-specific icon/close-button branching in both the Copy Code example generator and the Live Preview row-builder, and the second gallery card's entire wiring block. Left the generic \"jump to type\" deep-link mechanism in place (it's TYPES-driven and shared with other components) but reworded its comment off the now-nonexistent Prompt example. Carefully preserved every unrelated \"Copy Prompt\" (AI-prompt-text feature) reference - `buildFullPrompt`, `propertyPromptLine`, `copyPromptContainer` - since \"prompt\" means two different things in this file.\n- **`modal-default.html`** — updated the \"All 3 types\" copy to \"All 2 types\", removed the Prompt \"When to use\" chip/bullet and its Machine View JSON twin, and trimmed the Prompt exception clause out of the Accessibility bullet (and its JSON twin).\n- **`shell.css`** — removed the entire `.modal-demo-dialog--prompt` rule block and `.modal-demo-prompt-icon`.\n\nVerified zero remaining references anywhere in the repo (`modal-dialog--prompt`, `modal-demo-dialog--prompt`, `modal-demo-prompt-icon`), the Live Preview matrix now shows exactly Default/Destructive, Copy Prompt/Copy Code output is clean of any Prompt-type mention, no console errors, and the full 157-page HTTP-200 + tag-balance sweep passes.\n\n**Confirm-type shortcuts layout** — the 5 Modal.info/.success/.warning/.error/.confirm shortcut cards used `display:flex; flex-wrap:wrap`, so how many fit per row depended entirely on viewport width (4 across on a wide screen). Switched to `display:grid; grid-template-columns:repeat(2, minmax(0,1fr))` to hold exactly 2 per row regardless of viewport, per request. Verified via measured row groupings (2, 2, 1 - the 5th card alone on its own row, as expected for an odd count)."}, {"title": "UI polish (screenshot-reported bugs)", "body": "A batch of pixel-level fixes reported via screenshot/DevTools HTML over the course of this session, each verified with Playwright before being reported done:\n\n- **Sidebar search** — fixed placeholder text clipping, made the search box sticky to the top of the sidebar on scroll, then gave it its own padded sticky wrapper (`.sidebar-search-sticky`) after the first attempt left it touching the top edge. Applied across all 157 pages.\n- **Group Button focus ring** — Filled type: `outline-offset` was clipping the ring; changed `2px` → `-3px`. Outlined type (a second, harder bug found only after the user supplied exact DevTools HTML): first/last segments were missing `border-radius: inherit`, so the focus ring square-cornered past the pill's rounded ends.\n- **Password fields** — the reveal-icon toggle defaulted to unchecked (so the icon never rendered) and the static Do/Don't demo used a text label instead of the real eye icon; both fixed.\n- **Number stepper** — the up/down chevrons themselves were never miscentered (confirmed across Chromium/WebKit); the real bug was the control's own trailing `padding-inline-end` not accounting for the stepper as the last child, sized per height variant (24–56px).\n- **Component status badge** — `.component-status` was missing `line-height: 1`, so it inherited a 38px line-height from `.page-title` and rendered nearly 2.5x too tall.\n- **Rating / Upload matrix alignment** — labels were centered to match centered column headers; user wanted left alignment instead. Scoped `text-align: start` to each component's own matrix container so the shared `.button-matrix-*` classes used by other components weren't affected.\n- **Tabs underline** — a 1px seam between the gray baseline and the active tab's colored indicator, caused by fractional (`1.5px`) border/margin values rounding inconsistently. Switched to whole-pixel values with genuine overlap.\n- **Menu divider spacing** — two rounds. First: the divider above the destructive \"Delete\" item was a separate flex child, so the panel's `gap: 2px` applied on both sides of it (2px + line + 2px ≥ 5px), always more than the uniform 2px between any other two items — no margin value could close that gap without fractional-pixel hacks. Fixed by drawing the divider as a `border-top` directly on the destructive item itself; `box-sizing: border-box` means it draws inside the item's existing height, so the gap is now the exact same shared 2px as everywhere else. Removed the now-dead `.menu-demo-divider` class and its markup from `menu.html`/`menu-default.html`, and updated `menu-detail.js`'s Copy Code/Copy Prompt output to match. Second (found only after the gap measurement — 2px everywhere — still didn't match what the user kept seeing): every `.menu-demo-item` inherits the *panel's* full corner radius on **all four** corners, not just the ones touching the panel's own edge (intentional, so a rounded hover pill never clashes with the panel shell). That same blanket rounding curled the new border-top divider away from the left/right edges at any real radius, reading as a disconnected floating line rather than a flush divider — reproduced live via real `:hover`/`:focus-visible` (not just the `.is-hover`/`.is-focus` demo classes) and confirmed with zoomed pixel crops. Fixed by zeroing just the destructive item's own top-left/top-right radius (`border-start-start-radius`/`border-start-end-radius: 0`), leaving its bottom corners — and every other item's full rounding — untouched. Verified flush at every radius option including the 9999px \"pill\" extreme. Third (the actual \"blank space on the right when I hover\" bug, distinct from the divider): `.menu-demo-item` was caught in an unrelated shared selector, `.statistic-demo-value, .form-demo-help, .menu-demo-item, .transfer-demo-item, .addon-demo-segment, .splitter-demo-panel { max-width: 260px; ... }`, added earlier for other components' long free-text truncation. That flat 260px cap silently overrode the item's own `width: 100%` any time its panel was wider than 260px (the Live Preview matrix cells, and any demo card wider than that), leaving a blank strip down the right side of every item in every state (Rest/Hover/Focused/Disabled × Default/With-icons) - confirmed by inspecting computed width directly (item stuck at exactly 260px regardless of a much wider panel). Removed `.menu-demo-item` from that shared rule and gave it its own scoped truncation instead (`max-width: 100%`, so it tracks its own panel's real width and only ever truncates a label that's actually too long for that panel, never as a side effect of another component's truncation budget). Verified the hover pill and focus ring now span full width edge-to-edge in every state, and that a deliberately very long label still truncates correctly.\n- **Steps — removed the \"Simple\" type** (label-only, no circles) at request, leaving Numbered as the only type on both the horizontal and vertical detail pages (they share one `steps-detail.js`, deep-linked via `?orientation=vertical`). Removed the dead `.steps-demo-row--simple` CSS from `shell.css` (including one rule that had drifted to a separate part of the file, well outside the main Steps section), the `simple` entry from `TYPES`/`FULL_SPEC`, and the now-unreachable `typeKey === \"numbered\"` branches in `steps-detail.js`'s row builder (only \"numbered\" is ever passed now). The Live Preview matrix, Copy Prompt, and Copy Code all derive from `TYPES`, so they now show/emit only the one type with no leftover text; updated the one hardcoded description (\"Both types compared side by side...\") that assumed two. **Follow-up (reported as \"alignment is not coming properly\"):** removing the second column doubled the width the sole remaining \"Numbered\" column got from the matrix table's even-split layout. `.steps-demo-connector` uses `flex: 1 1 auto` (correct for a real product header, where it should fill available space) - so with double the room, connectors between circles stretched ~15x wider (measured 16px → 239px), and the whole row, now much narrower than its cell, sat flush left under a horizontally-centered column header. Scoped a fix to the Live Preview only (`[data-role=\"steps-matrix-container\"] .steps-demo-row { max-width: max-content; margin-inline: auto; }`), leaving the actual component recipe (Copy Code output, listing cards) untouched - confirmed the row's rendered width now matches the listing card's own proportions almost exactly (181px vs. 183px) and is centered under its header, in both Horizontal and Vertical orientation. **Second follow-up (\"a lot of blank space\"):** centering the row fixed its own position but didn't shrink the column around it - `.button-matrix{ width:100%; min-width:820px }` (shared by every component's matrix in this file) assumes the table's width gets divided across 2+ columns, so with only one column left it all still went to that one column, leaving a ~500px empty gutter between the row-head labels (Upcoming/Current/etc.) and the now-compact steps content sitting centered inside it. Scoped `width:auto; min-width:0` to Steps' own matrix only (every other component's matrix still gets the original 820px floor, which they need for comparing multiple types side by side) - the table now sizes itself to its actual content, confirmed compact in both orientations and confirmed it still grows properly (with `.button-matrix-wrap`'s `overflow-x:auto` as a fallback) for a deliberately long 5-label test case.\n- **Steps — added a dedicated \"Step count\" control.** More steps were already possible by typing more comma-separated values into the existing Labels field (step count always followed label count), but the user wanted an explicit counter rather than relying on that. Added a `type=\"number\"` input (min 2, max 10, matching the precedent already set by Skeleton's own \"Rows\" control) next to Labels. Bumping it up pads with generic \"Step N\" labels for just the new slots - existing custom text (e.g. \"Cart, Shipping, Payment\") is never overwritten; bumping it down trims from the end. Wired two-way: editing Labels directly also updates what Step count displays, so the two controls can't silently disagree. Verified the full range (2 through 10, including clamping an out-of-range 11 down to 10 and a 1 up to 2) renders correctly and stays compact/centered at every count.\n- **Steps — reversed the \"compact matrix\" fix above, per explicit follow-up request (\"make it responsive... auto-fill blank space\").** The compacting fix solved the row/header alignment problem by shrinking the row away from the available width, which traded one complaint (misalignment) for another (a large blank gutter next to a now-tiny row, not using the page). Removed both matrix-scoped overrides (`.steps-demo-row{ max-width:max-content }` and `.button-matrix{ width:auto }`) for Horizontal, letting the table and row return to the site-wide default (full width, `.steps-demo-connector`'s own `flex:1 1 auto` filling the resulting space) - this is standard behavior for a full-width product stepper header, and it happens to resolve the header/content alignment on its own too, since both now span the same 100% width. Vertical orientation needed to keep the centering fix, though: its connector is a fixed 1.5px x 20px tick, not a flex-growing line, so there's nothing for it to stretch into - left alone, an uncompacted vertical row would sit flush-left under the still-centered column header, reintroducing the exact mismatch Horizontal just fixed by filling. Scoped the max-content/centering rule to `.steps-demo-row--vertical` specifically so the two orientations each get the fix that actually applies to them. Verified both orientations at 3 and 6 steps: Horizontal now stretches full-width with proportionate connectors, Vertical stays compact and centered under its header.\n- **Steps — fixed the Step count field rejecting keyboard input.** It clamped and rewrote its own value on every keystroke (`input` event), which fought any two-digit target: typing \"10\" hit \"1\" first, and 1 is below the field's minimum, so it snapped straight to \"2\" before \"0\" could ever be typed - worse, that wrong intermediate count also silently rebuilt the Labels field around it on every keystroke, discarding real label text along the way. All my earlier verification of this control had set `.value` directly and dispatched one synthetic event with the finished number, which never exercised real per-keystroke typing and so never caught this. Switched the listener from `input` to `change` (fires on blur, Enter, or a spinner-arrow click - a natural \"commit\" point for a numeric field, not every keystroke), so any number can be typed freely and only the finished value clamps and updates Labels. Verified this time with actual simulated keystrokes (not a single dispatched value): typing \"1\" then \"0\" now reads \"1\", then \"10\" at each step with no snapping, commits to 10 correctly on blur, and the spinner arrows still apply instantly as before.\n- **Steps — fixed the real source of the \"blank space,\" and made the layout stable across step counts.** It wasn't blank space next to the row at all: the matrix table uses the browser's default `auto` layout (like every other matrix in this file), which splits a table's width across its columns using its own content-based heuristics. With only one data column, that heuristic handed the sticky row-head column (just \"Upcoming\"/\"Completed\"/etc.) a wildly disproportionate share - measured over 400px wide at the default 3-step count. The single data column got whatever was left, and - because auto-layout re-weighs columns from each row's actual content - that split reshuffled unpredictably every time the step count changed, which is what read as \"it depends\" / \"not responsive.\" Scoped `table-layout: fixed` to Steps' own matrix (`[data-role=\"steps-matrix-container\"] .button-matrix`) with an explicit `150px` row-head width (comfortably fits every state label plus its padding, measured directly rather than guessed) - fixed layout makes column widths deterministic instead of content-reshuffled, so the data column now gets the same, full, correct remainder at every count. Also raised `MAX_STEPS` from 10 to 12 to cover the full range the user tested. Verified programmatically across every count 1 through 12 (1 clamping to the 2 minimum) that the row-head stays exactly 150px and the data column exactly matches the table's remaining width every single time, confirmed Menu's own matrix (`table-layout: auto`) is untouched, and confirmed Vertical orientation still centers correctly under its header.\n- **Alert — fixed icon/title/description/action-link misalignment.** `.alert-demo-title` and `.alert-demo-description` are `<p>` tags with no `margin: 0` reset, so each kept the browser's own default paragraph margin on top of `.alert-demo-body`'s intentional `gap: 4px`. That pushed the title ~13px below the icon and the close (X) / Undo action - both plain flex children of the banner, not `<p>` tags, so unaffected - leaving title visibly lower than everything beside it, and left a much bigger gap between title and description than the 4px the gap was meant to set. Measured before/after with real pixel offsets rather than eyeballing it: icon/close/action were already sitting within 1px of each other pre-fix (top ≈ 14-16px), title was the outlier at 28px; after adding `margin: 0` to both `<p>` rules (in `shell.css` and the matching Copy Code emission in `alert-detail.js`), title lines up with the rest at 14px and the title-to-description gap is exactly the intended 4px. Fixed once at the shared CSS class, so it applies automatically to the listing card and Do/Don't demos too, not just the Live Preview matrix. Verified across all 16 type × state combinations."}, {"title": "New capability mockups (build brief)", "body": "A second spec document (\"Build Brief — Onboarding, NPM, MD Export & History\") asked for 4 new capability areas: a project creation flow, NPM package publishing, Markdown export, and a History page. All 4 assume real backend infrastructure this site doesn't have (async job runners, npm registry auth, encrypted credential storage, an audit-log database) — built as **static mockup pages** instead, matching this site's existing genre (real markup/CSS/copy for every state described, no working backend), per explicit confirmation before starting.\n\n- **`new-project.html`** — entry modal (Create/Import choice) + all 5 wizard steps (project details, brand with live contrast validation, foundations, context, review) + generation progress + success/partial-failure outcome states.\n- **`npm-package.html`** — the 6-state machine (Not configured → Configured → Building → Built → Published, plus Failed), configuration screen, published state with install command, version-drift warning, publish-safety rules.\n- **`md-export.html`** — export dialog (scope + tier selection with token estimates), file structure/naming, required front-matter, the immutability rule (stored snapshots, never regenerated), post-export state, and AI Sync (synced/stale).\n- **`history.html`** — Versions tab and Activity tab (reusing the site's existing Human/Machine View toggle mechanism in `shell.js` — no new JS needed), version detail, compare, restore, and the corrected empty state (\"no published versions yet, draft in progress,\" not \"create first version\").\n\nAdded 7 new `.component-status` modifier classes (`is-published`, `is-draft`, `is-in-review`, `is-deprecated`, `is-building`, `is-failed`, `is-not-configured`) to `shell.css` for these pages, plus nav links across all 153 existing pages (New Project under Design System; MD Export and NPM Package in the flat bottom group; History between Governance and Settings, per the brief's own placement instruction)."}, {"title": "Welcome modal (real, not a mockup)", "body": "Unlike the build-brief pages above, this one is genuinely functional — a modal that auto-opens on `overview.html` needs no backend to work for real, unlike NPM publishing or async generation.\n\n- Opens automatically when `overview.html` loads. Choice: \"Continue with existing project\" (just dismisses — there's no real multi-project backend to list from) or \"Create new project\" (reveals a form).\n- Form: Name, Designation, Email, Portal type. Name and Email are required before the two actions below will run.\n- **Copy link** — builds a real URL to `new-project.html` with the fields as query params and copies it via the Clipboard API.\n- **Export MD file** — generates real Markdown (front-matter + body) from the fields and downloads it via a Blob, client-side, no server involved.\n- **Continue to Foundations** — closes the modal and sends the user to `foundations.html#colors`, this portal's one actual working \"configure + Save\" flow, rather than inventing a fake save mechanism for the modal's own fields.\n- Real accessible modal behavior: focus moves in on open and returns to the trigger on close, Tab is trapped within the visible step, Escape and backdrop-click both dismiss it. (All three were verified by dispatching real DOM events directly — Playwright's synthetic `keyboard.press`/`mouse.click` don't reliably simulate input in this sandbox, a known false-negative already seen once before with the sidebar search's `/` shortcut. The underlying handlers work.)\n- New files: `welcome.js`. New CSS in `shell.css` (`.welcome-modal-*`, `.welcome-choice-btn`, `.form-demo-select`)."}, {"title": "Fixes implemented from the audit", "body": "**Cross-cutting (all 153 pages):**\n- `aria-expanded` / `aria-controls` added to the sidebar's collapsible nav groups (`shell.js`)\n- `aria-label` added to the global search input\n- Owner / Version / Changelog metadata line added to all 69 component detail pages that carry a status badge\n\n**P0:**\n- Table: added a full interaction contract — Selection (none/single/multi), Sort (client/server, `aria-sort`), Filter (per-column vs. global, empty vs. no-results), Pagination-vs-virtualization (500-row threshold), Density, and Do/Don't demos for Empty/Loading/Error states. Kept the Machine View JSON and Copy Prompt/Code output in sync.\n- Forms: added real validation content (required marking, timing, error copy, recovery) to all 21 Forms detail pages that had none — Password and Upload got the deepest treatment (WCAG 3.3.8, autofill, the 3 real upload failure modes).\n- Feedback: added live-region ARIA to Spin and Pop Confirm (the two highest-risk gaps), plus documented/added ARIA across Alert, Modal, Tooltip, Tour, Drawer, Skeleton — each with a deliberate decision on whether a live region actually applies (Tooltip and static Alert content correctly do **not** get one).\n\n**P3:**\n- Spacing, Radius, Borders — added an explicit \"AI Rule\" callout to each (new reusable `.ai-rule-callout` style)\n- Group Button — cross-referenced to Button\n- Breadcrumb — tightened the truncation rule to a concrete pattern\n- Statistic — cross-linked its number-formatting bullet to Data Format\n- Settings — now links directly to the working brand-color customization feature (under Foundations · Colors) instead of implying it doesn't exist\n- Corrected 3 audit findings that turned out to be wrong on closer inspection: Steps' state-switching already worked correctly (a different, better mechanism than assumed), Image's broken-state handling was already fully documented, Divider/Tree needed no changes"}, {"title": "Full-portal audit", "body": "Audited all 153 pages against a pasted product/UX specification document (deliverable contract: current state, UX/UI/a11y issues, AI-readiness gaps, recommended structure, priority). Delivered as a sample deep-dive on 4 representative pages (Overview, Design Values, Button, Table), then a full pass across every remaining category as tables of deltas from a shared baseline, plus a priority-tagged action list.\n\nKey cross-cutting findings: no live/interactive components (everything is static demo markup), no state coverage beyond \"rest\" on most components, no real governance/ownership metadata, no Motion foundation at all, and a real IA anomaly in Button's page structure (see above)."}, {"title": "Component fixes", "body": "- **Steps** — fixed a bug where the Default and Vertical listing cards both opened the same detail page with no visible difference; Vertical now deep-links via `?orientation=vertical`, which pre-selects the real Orientation property on load.\n- **Modal** — added a new \"Prompt\" type grounded in the real SLDS Modal spec (colored warning header, no close button, centered footer). Fixed a bug where the Default and Prompt cards opened to visually identical pages by adding a persistent \"jump to type\" highlight in the Live Preview matrix.\n- **Progress** — added an SLDS-grounded \"Vertical\" layout, then fully removed it at request; kept the square-corner and `role=\"progressbar\"` accessibility fixes from that work.\n- **Tabs** — added a \"Scoped\" type, then fully removed it after it read as visually broken in light theme (a `--graphite-900`/`--graphite-800` contrast-inversion issue). Left a code comment on the `TYPES` array documenting the lesson for next time.\n- **Tree** — corrected an initial \"Show Line\" feature (based on Ant Design, not this system's actual spec) to the real SLDS \"Metatext\" feature (a second muted line under a node label). Fixed the same both-cards-same-page bug as Modal.\n- **Tree Grid** — built from scratch (listing + detail page, ARIA `treegrid` semantics, sortable headers, etc.), grounded in the real SLDS spec and a reference screenshot, then fully removed at request along with all nav references.\n- **Theme toggle** — added CSS-only sun/moon icons to the light/dark toggle thumb.\n- **Button (Primary + Icon)** — added the missing **Loading** state (spinner) across all 7 icon-button types and the \"CTA With Dropdown\" split button in `button-dualicon-detail.js`. The page's own copy already promised 6 states; the code only implemented 5.\n- **Button (listing)** — found that 6 of 8 Button variant pages (Secondary, Tertiary, Ghost, Neutral, Destructive, Link) had no link pointing to them anywhere in the 153-page site. Added cards for them to `components.html`, then removed those same 6 cards again at explicit request. Currently only Primary and Primary+Icon are reachable from the listing; the other 6 pages still exist but are unlinked.\n- **Brand color / theme bug** — fixed a real bug where a custom brand color set in light theme didn't carry over to dark theme. Root cause: `foundations.js` only persisted a \"dark\" override when a checkbox was explicitly enabled, so a plain light-color save left no dark entry in storage at all. Fixed the save path to always persist one, and added a read-side fallback (in `shell.js` and the boot script duplicated across all 153 pages) so already-saved color choices are picked up immediately, without needing a re-save."}], "knownGaps": "- **Motion foundation doesn't exist at all** — flagged as the single biggest P0 gap in the whole audit, still untouched. (Motion *tokens* do exist in theme.css and are used consistently sitewide; there's just no dedicated Foundation page documenting them, the way Colors/Spacing/Radius/etc. each have one.)\n- Governance now has a real Changelog (this file, rendered at changelog.html) and an honest version number (bumped to v3.5.0 to reflect the accumulated real work since v3.4.0) - \"Owner: Design System Team\" is still a static string, since a single-team ownership model is a legitimate simple governance choice for a system this size, not a gap needing a multi-owner/RBAC model invented to fill it.\n- Component-tier tokens exist for 4 components (Button Primary, Table, Modal, Card) - real, valuable follow-up work is extending the same tier to the rest of the 63-component library, not something one pass reasonably finishes.\n- Radius and Typography font family now propagate sitewide (see above), including component detail pages' own \"Corner radius\" property-preview controls (33 files, see the follow-up entry above); Spacing, Borders, Shadows, Icons and Grid & Layout still don't - each needs its own real token-architecture work first (a semantic scale-per-role tier for Spacing/Shadows/Borders, a structural rework for Icons/Grid & Layout), not a mechanical wire-up like Radius/Typography got.\n- 13 of the 63 components' Live Preview matrix code has no matching mount element in their current HTML at all (see the Corner radius follow-up above for the list) - the render function silently no-ops instead of drawing anything. Real, valuable follow-up work is rebuilding each page's matrix HTML to match what its own `*-detail.js` already expects, not something this pass reasonably finishes.\n- Several P1 items from the audit remain: Design Values has no per-value AI Rule or priority order; Typography has no script-fallback/truncation rules; Navigation/Data Display components (Dropdown, Menu, Calendar, Collapse, Splitter, etc.) haven't had their keyboard/ARIA gaps addressed yet.\n- The 6 unlinked Button pages (Secondary/Tertiary/Ghost/Neutral/Destructive/Link) still exist as files but aren't reachable from the UI — left as-is per the last explicit instruction, not forgotten.\n\nAll changes were verified with `node --check` (JS syntax), a full-site tag-balance sweep, a full-site HTTP 200 sweep, and targeted Playwright browser tests for anything involving live JS behavior (theme switching, jump-to-type highlighting, the Button loading state)."};
